('levosulpiride', 'Levosulpiride', 'Levosulpiride', 'Levosulpiride', 'Levosulpiride', 'Gastrointestinal', 'The levorotatory enantiomer of sulpiride with dual enteric dopamine D2-receptor antagonism and 5-HT4 receptor agonism, widely used in India for functional dyspepsia and diabetic gastroparesis.', '[''25 mg'', ''75 mg SR'', ''25 mg/2 mL injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Sustained-Release Capsule'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Selectively blocks enteric and central dopamine D2 receptors, removing the inhibitory effect of dopamine on gastrointestinal motility; concurrently acts as an agonist at 5-HT4 receptors on enteric cholinergic motor neurons, stimulating acetylcholine release from the myenteric plexus.', 'Accelerates gastric emptying, improves antroduodenal coordination, enhances lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone, and reduces visceral gastrointestinal hypersensitivity; relieves postprandial fullness and bloating in functional dyspepsia.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1 to 2 hours; absolute oral bioavailability approx. 30-40%; take 30 minutes before meals.', 'Distributes into gastrointestinal tract and brain; low plasma protein binding (<40%); crosses blood-brain barrier.', 'Minimally metabolized in the liver (<10%); does not significantly inhibit or induce CYP enzymes.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration and tubular secretion (approx. 70-80%).', '30% to 40%', '4 to 6 hours', '<40%', '30 to 60 minutes', '8 to 12 hours (25 mg TID before meals, or 75 mg SR once daily)', '[''Functional dyspepsia (postprandial distress syndrome: bloating, early satiety, epigastric fullness)'', ''Diabetic gastroparesis (delayed gastric emptying)'', ''Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD - combined with PPIs)'', ''Non-ulcer dyspepsia refractory to acid suppression'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to levosulpiride'', ''Pheochromocytoma (dopamine antagonists can trigger hypertensive crises)'', ''Gastrointestinal bleeding, mechanical intestinal obstruction, or perforation'', ''Epilepsy or patients in manic states'', ''Hyperprolactinemia, prolactinoma, or hormone-dependent breast carcinoma'']', '["HYPERPROLACTINEMIA: Blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the pituitary gland, removing dopamine''s tonic inhibition on prolactin; causes galactorrhea, amenorrhea, gynecomastia, and loss of libido", ''EXTRAPYRAMIDAL SYMPTOMS: Can trigger drug-induced parkinsonism, acute dystonia, tremor, and tardive dyskinesia with prolonged use'', ''Drowsiness / somnolence (avoid driving)'', ''Limit therapy to 4-8 weeks to avoid neurological and endocrine side effects'']', '[''Somnolence / drowsiness'', ''Fatigue'', ''Amenorrhea (missed menstrual periods in females)'', ''Galactorrhea (breast milk leakage)'', ''Gynecomastia in males'', ''Weight gain'']', '[''Drug-induced parkinsonism / severe tremors'', ''Acute dystonic reactions (facial spasms, oculogyric crisis)'', ''Tardive dyskinesia (with prolonged use >3-6 months)'', ''Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (extremely rare)'']', '[''Anticholinergic drugs (atropine, dicyclomine - directly antagonize the prokinetic action of levosulpiride)'', ''Central nervous system depressants, alcohol (additive sedation)'', ''Dopamine agonists (levodopa, bromocriptine - mutual antagonism)'']', '[''Monitor for galactorrhea, menstrual irregularities, or breast tenderness at every visit'', ''Examine for tremor, rigidity, or facial muscle spasms (discontinue immediately if extrapyramidal signs appear)'', ''Serum prolactin levels if symptoms develop'']', 'Contraindicated in pregnancy; dopamine antagonists cross placenta and alter fetal neurodevelopment.', 'Contraindicated during breastfeeding; excreted in human milk and stimulates excessive lactation.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients under 18 years not established.', 'High susceptibility to drug-induced parkinsonism, tremor, and falls; use lowest effective dose (25 mg BID) for short durations.', 'Dose reduction required: CrCl 30-50 mL/min: reduce dose by 50%; CrCl < 30 mL/min: reduce dose by 70%.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''Dual D2 antagonism + 5-HT4 agonism provides superior prokinetic relief for postprandial fullness and bloating compared to older agents'', ''Enhances PPI efficacy in refractory GERD when used as fixed-dose combination (e.g., Pantoprazole + Levosulpiride SR)'', ''Low cardiac arrhythmia risk compared to cisapride'']', '[''Causes breast milk leakage (galactorrhea), missed periods, and gynecomastia due to elevated prolactin'', "Can cause drug-induced Parkinson''s tremors and facial spasms with long-term use"]', '[''Take 25 mg 30 minutes before meals for stomach bloating and fullness'', ''Stop the drug and call your doctor if you notice breast milk leakage, missed periods, or hand tremors'']', 'LEVO-sulpiride: Lower Enteric Velocity Overcome; Prokinetic D2 block; Prolactin surge (galactorrhea/amenorrhea); Parkinsonism risk.', '-sulpiride', 'lee-voe-SUL-pi-ride', 'लेवो-सल्पिराइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('cinitapride', 'Cinitapride', 'Cinitapride Hydrogen Tartrate', 'Cinitapride hydrogen tartrate', 'Cinitapride hydrogen tartrate', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A benzamide prokinetic with dual 5-HT4 agonism and 5-HT2 / D2 antagonism, used for functional dyspepsia and GERD with lower extrapyramidal risk than metoclopramide.', '[''1 mg'', ''3 mg ER'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Extended-Release Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Stimulates gastrointestinal 5-HT4 receptors, promotes acetylcholine release from myenteric plexus neurons, and blocks inhibitory 5-HT2 and dopamine D2 receptors; enhances gastrointestinal motor coordination.', 'Accelerates gastric emptying, increases lower esophageal sphincter (LES) resting pressure, improves intestinal transit, and reduces gastroesophageal reflux; does NOT prolong the QTc interval at therapeutic doses.', 'Rapidly absorbed from GI tract; peak plasma concentrations in 2 hours; take 15 minutes before meals.', 'Widely distributed; 82% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and to a lesser extent CYP2C8.', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 60%) via biliary elimination and urine (approx. 30%).', 'Approximately 60%', '3 to 5 hours', '82%', '30 to 60 minutes', '8 hours (1 mg TID before meals; 3 mg ER once daily)', '[''Functional dyspepsia (postprandial fullness, early satiety, epigastric bloating)'', ''Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD - add-on to PPIs)'', ''Delayed gastric emptying (gastroparesis)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cinitapride'', ''Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation'', ''Patients with a history of neuroleptic-induced tardive dyskinesia'']', '[''Take 15 minutes before meals'', ''Avoid concurrent strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin - increases cinitapride levels)'', ''Mild sedation and drowsiness may occur'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Mild drowsiness / sedation'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Abdominal colic'']', '[''Extrapyramidal reactions / acute dystonia (much less frequent than metoclopramide)'', ''Hyperprolactinemia (rare)'', ''Severe hypersensitivity rash'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin - increase cinitapride exposure)'', ''Anticholinergics (atropine - antagonize prokinetic effect)'', ''CNS depressants / alcohol (additive sedation)'']', '[''Symptomatic improvement in postprandial bloating and early satiety'', ''Screen for tremor or muscle stiffness'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; limited human clinical safety data.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'Initiate with standard or reduced dose; well tolerated in senior patients.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate renal disease.', 'Use with caution in severe hepatic impairment (metabolized by CYP3A4).', '[''Lower risk of extrapyramidal symptoms and hyperprolactinemia compared to metoclopramide and levosulpiride'', ''Does not cause cardiac QT prolongation, unlike cisapride'', ''Convenient 3 mg once-daily extended-release formulation'']', '[''Requires dosing 15 minutes before meals TID for standard tablets'', ''CYP3A4 metabolic interactions with azole antifungals and macrolides'']', '[''Prokinetic tablet that helps your stomach empty faster without causing breast swelling or spasms'', ''Take 1 mg 15 minutes before breakfast, lunch, and dinner'']', 'CINITA-pride: Clears Indigestion; Normalizes Intestinal Transit; Action via 5-HT4; Pride without QT prolongation.', '-pride', 'si-NI-ta-pride', 'सिनिटा-प्राइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('mosapride', 'Mosapride', 'Mosapride Citrate', 'Mosapride citrate', 'Mosapride citrate', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A selective gastroprokinetic 5-HT4 receptor agonist completely devoid of dopamine D2 and 5-HT1/5-HT2 affinity, providing pure prokinetic relief without extrapyramidal or cardiac QT toxicities.', '[''5 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively and potently stimulates 5-HT4 receptors on enteric cholinergic interneurons and motor neurons, stimulating intrinsic acetylcholine release from the myenteric plexus; lacks dopamine D2-receptor antagonist properties and does NOT block HERG potassium channels.', 'Enhances antral and duodenal contractile amplitude, accelerates gastric emptying, and coordinates upper GI motility; zero risk of extrapyramidal reactions (no D2 blockade) and zero risk of cardiac QT prolongation or Torsades de pointes (unlike cisapride).', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 0.8 to 1 hour; bioavailability approx. 30%; take before or after meals.', 'Widely distributed; highly bound to human serum albumin (>99%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to active des-4-fluorobenzyl metabolite (M-1).', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 60%) and urine (approx. 30-40% as metabolites); <1% unchanged parent drug in urine.', 'Approximately 30%', '1.5 to 2 hours', '>99%', '30 minutes', '8 hours (5 mg TID)', '[''Functional dyspepsia (postprandial distress syndrome: fullness, belching, nausea)'', ''Chronic gastritis with impaired gastric emptying'', ''Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD - combined with PPI therapy)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to mosapride citrate'', ''Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, obstruction, or perforation'']', '[''Liver function: rare cases of fulminant hepatitis and elevated liver transaminases reported (discontinue if jaundice appears)'', ''Do not use for prolonged periods if dyspeptic symptoms do not improve after 2 weeks'', ''Take consistently before or after meals'']', '[''Diarrhoea and loose stools (most common)'', ''Dry mouth'', ''Abdominal pain'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'']', '[''Fulminant hepatitis / severe drug-induced liver injury'', ''Eosinophilia (rare)'', ''Anaphylaxis'']', '[''Anticholinergic agents (scopolamine, dicyclomine - reduce mosapride efficacy)'', ''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (erythromycin, ketoconazole - increase mosapride blood levels)'']', '[''Liver function tests (AST, ALT, total bilirubin) prior to long-term therapy'', ''Symptomatic response in dyspepsia at 2 weeks'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient safety data.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding during therapy.', 'Safety and efficacy in children not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'Use with caution and monitor liver enzymes in preexisting hepatic disease.', '[''Pure 5-HT4 receptor agonist with ZERO dopamine D2 blockade: ZERO risk of tremors, facial spasms, or breast milk leakage'', ''ZERO cardiotoxicity: does not block HERG potassium channels or cause fatal arrhythmias (safe alternative to cisapride)'', ''Rapid onset of action within 30 to 60 minutes'']', '[''Short half-life requires dosing three times daily (5 mg TID)'', ''Rare reports of liver enzyme elevation requiring monitoring'']', '[''Safest stomach emptying pill: will NOT cause muscle tremors, stiff face, or breast milk leakage'', ''Take one 5 mg tablet three times daily before or after meals'']', 'MOSA-pride: Motility Organizer; Selective Agonist at 5-HT4; Pure prokinetic; Zero D2 spasms; Zero QT prolongation.', '-pride', 'MOE-sa-pride', 'मोसा-प्राइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('granisetron', 'Granisetron', 'Granisetron Hydrochloride', 'Granisetron hydrochloride', 'Granisetron hydrochloride', 'Antiemetics', 'A potent, highly selective second-generation 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with a 9-hour half-life, used to prevent nausea and vomiting from emetogenic chemotherapy and radiotherapy.', '[''1 mg'', ''2 mg'', ''1 mg/mL IV ampoule'', ''3.1 mg/24h transdermal patch'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'', ''Transdermal Patch'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Transdermal'']', 'Selectively and competitively blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents in the gastrointestinal tract and centrally in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and solitary tract nucleus (NTS); blocks peripheral and central serotonin emetic signaling.', 'Suppresses acute chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV) and radiation-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV); exhibits a longer half-life (9 hours) and higher 5-HT3 selectivity than ondansetron.', 'Rapidly absorbed; 60% oral bioavailability; peak plasma concentration in 2 hours; unaffected by food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 3 L/kg; 65% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 via N-demethylation and aromatic ring oxidation.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 12% unchanged parent drug; 38% as metabolites) and faeces (approx. 38% as metabolites).', 'Approximately 60%', '9 hours in cancer patients (longer than ondansetron''s 3-4 hours)', '65%', 'Oral: 1 hour; IV: within minutes', '24 hours (1-2 mg once or twice daily; transdermal patch lasts 7 days)', '[''Prevention of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV - moderately and highly emetogenic chemotherapy)'', ''Prevention and treatment of radiation-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)'', ''Prevention and treatment of postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to granisetron or 5-HT3 antagonists'']', '[''Dose-dependent QTc prolongation: use caution in patients with preexisting congenital long QT syndrome or hypokalemia/hypomagnesemia'', ''Severe constipation: slows colonic transit (monitor for bowel obstruction)'', ''Transdermal patch: apply 24-48 hours before chemotherapy; provides 7 days of continuous antiemetic coverage'']', '[''Headache (very common: up to 15-20%)'', ''Constipation (due to GI 5-HT3 blockade)'', ''Asthenia / fatigue'', ''Somnolence'', ''Transient transaminase elevation'']', '[''Severe QTc prolongation / Torsades de pointes (rare)'', ''Serotonin syndrome (when combined with SSRIs, SNRIs, or MAOIs)'', ''Severe bowel obstruction / fecal impaction'']', '[''Serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans - risk of serotonin syndrome)'', ''CYP3A4 inducers (phenobarbital, carbamazepine - increase granisetron clearance)'', ''QT-prolonging agents'']', '[''Baseline ECG for QTc interval in high-risk patients'', ''Serum potassium and magnesium before chemotherapy'', ''Stool frequency for constipation'']', 'Category B; widely used in pregnancy for refractory hyperemesis gravidarum without teratogenic harm.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in pediatric oncology patients 2 years and older (10-40 mcg/kg IV).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; clearance is lower in severe liver disease but well tolerated.', '[''Longer half-life (9 hours vs. 3-4 hours for ondansetron) allows once-daily or twice-daily dosing'', ''Transdermal patch (Sancuso) provides 7 days of continuous needle-free antiemetic protection during multi-day chemotherapy'', ''Higher selectivity for 5-HT3 receptors than ondansetron'']', '[''Headache and constipation are common'', ''Slight risk of QT prolongation'']', '[''Take 1-2 mg one hour before chemotherapy to prevent severe nausea and vomiting'', ''Transdermal patch can be placed on the upper arm for 7 days of continuous protection'']', 'GRANI-setron: Greater Receptor Affinity; Nine-hour half-life; Injection, oral & 7-day Patch; Stops emesis.', '-setron', 'gra-NI-se-tron', 'ग्रैनि-सेट्रॉन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('palonosetron', 'Palonosetron', 'Palonosetron Hydrochloride', 'Palonosetron hydrochloride', 'Palonosetron hydrochloride', 'Antiemetics', 'A potent second-generation 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with a 40-hour half-life and unique receptor allosteric binding, uniquely effective for both acute AND delayed chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.', '[''0.5 mg capsule'', ''0.25 mg/5 mL IV vial'']', '[''Soft Gelatin Capsule'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'High-affinity competitive and allosteric 5-HT3 receptor antagonist (binds with 100-fold higher affinity than ondansetron and granisetron); induces 5-HT3 receptor internalization and cross-talk inhibition with substance P (NK1) pathways.', 'Long-lasting suppression of both acute (day 1) and delayed (days 2-5) chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV); single IV dose provides 5 full days of antiemetic efficacy.', 'Rapidly absorbed; 97% oral bioavailability; peak plasma concentration in 5 hours; unaffected by food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 8.3 L/kg; 62% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Metabolized in the liver (approx. 50%) by CYP2D6, and to a lesser extent CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, to inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 80%, with 40% as unchanged parent drug) and faeces.', 'Approximately 97%', '40 hours (prolonged terminal half-life provides 5 days of antiemetic protection)', '62%', 'IV: immediate; Oral: 1 to 2 hours', 'Over 72 to 120 hours (5 full days)', '[''Prevention of acute and delayed chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV) associated with initial and repeat courses of moderately and highly emetogenic chemotherapy'', ''Prevention of postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV) for up to 24 hours following surgery'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to palonosetron or 5-HT3 antagonists'']', '[''Single dose covers BOTH acute and delayed nausea: DO NOT REDOSE on subsequent days of the chemotherapy cycle'', ''Constipation and headache'', ''Serotonin syndrome when combined with multiple serotonergic agents'']', '[''Headache (most common)'', ''Constipation'', ''Dizziness'', ''Fatigue'', ''Transient transaminitis'']', '[''Severe serotonin syndrome (rare)'', ''Severe hypersensitivity / anaphylaxis'', ''Bowel obstruction / fecal impaction (rare)'']', '[''Serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, SNRIs - risk of serotonin syndrome)'', ''CYP2D6 / CYP3A4 inhibitors (minimal clinical impact)'']', '[''Stool frequency and bowel movements'', ''Signs of serotonin syndrome (mental status changes, hyperreflexia, clonus)'']', 'Category B; animal studies show no teratogenicity; use only if clearly needed.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Approved in pediatric patients 1 month and older for CINV (weight-based IV dosing: 20 mcg/kg).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''Only 5-HT3 antagonist proven effective for DELAYED nausea (days 2 to 5) as well as acute nausea'', ''Ultra-long 40-hour half-life allows a single dose to cover the entire chemotherapy cycle'', ''No significant QTc prolongation at clinical doses (cleanest cardiac safety profile among setrons)'']', '[''Higher cost than older generic ondansetron'', ''Headache and constipation'']', '[''A single IV dose given 30 minutes before chemo protects against nausea for 5 whole days'', ''Does not need to be repeated every day of chemotherapy'']', 'PALONO-setron: Prolonged 40-hour Action; Lasts Over 5 days; Neutralizes delayed emesis; Once per chemo cycle.', '-setron', 'pa-loe-NOE-se-tron', 'पैलोनो-सेट्रॉन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('rolapitant', 'Rolapitant', 'Rolapitant Hydrochloride', 'Rolapitant hydrochloride', 'Rolapitant hydrochloride', 'Antiemetics', 'A long-acting neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptor antagonist with an ultra-long 169-hour (7-day) half-life that does not inhibit CYP3A4, combined with a 5-HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy.', '[''90 mg (two tablets = 180 mg)'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively and competitively blocks substance P from binding to neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and solitary tract nucleus in the brainstem; does NOT inhibit or induce CYP3A4.', 'Profoundly suppresses delayed nausea and vomiting (days 2-5) caused by substance P release following cisplatin, anthracycline/cyclophosphamide chemotherapy; unique lack of CYP3A4 inhibition eliminates the need to reduce dexamethasone doses.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 4 hours; absolute oral bioavailability approx. 100%; unaffected by food.', 'Volume of distribution approx. 460 L; highly bound to human plasma proteins (99.8%).', 'Metabolized in the liver primarily by CYP2C9 to active metabolite M19 (C4-pyrrolidine-hydroxylated rolapitant, which also possesses NK1 antagonism).', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 73%) and urine (approx. 14% as metabolites); zero unchanged drug in urine.', 'Approximately 100%', '169 hours (approx. 7 days; active metabolite half-life 158 hours)', '99.8%', '4 hours', 'Over 7 to 10 days (single dose per 2-week chemo cycle)', '[''Prevention of delayed nausea and vomiting associated with initial and repeat courses of moderately and highly emetogenic cancer chemotherapy (combined with a 5-HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone)'']', '[''Concurrent use of thioridazine (strong CYP2D6 substrate - risk of fatal ventricular arrhythmias / QT prolongation)'', ''Known hypersensitivity to rolapitant'']', '[''DO NOT READMINISTER within a 2-week interval (ultra-long 7-day half-life provides multi-day receptor blockade; minimum interval between doses is 14 days)'', ''MODERATE CYP2D6 INHIBITOR: Inhibits CYP2D6 for at least 7 days post-dose (increases levels of CYP2D6 substrates: dextromethorphan, nebivolol, thioridazine)'', ''DOES NOT INHIBIT CYP3A4: unlike aprepitant, NO dose reduction of oral dexamethasone is needed'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Fatigue / asthenia'', ''Headache'', ''Decreased appetite'', ''Dizziness'', ''Stomatitis'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity / anaphylaxis'', ''Severe ventricular arrhythmias (if combined with thioridazine)'', ''Neutropenia (chemotherapy-related)'']', '[''Thioridazine (STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED: CYP2D6 inhibition elevates thioridazine to lethal QT-prolonging levels)'', ''CYP2D6 substrates (dextromethorphan, pimozide, encainide - increased drug levels)'', ''Strong CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin - significantly reduces rolapitant levels)'']', '[''Chemotherapy-induced delayed nausea frequency on days 2 to 5'', ''Screen for concomitant CYP2D6 medications'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human clinical safety data.', 'Excreted in animal milk; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment (including hemodialysis).', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; not studied in severe cirrhosis.', '[''Ultra-long 7-day half-life: a single 180 mg dose before chemotherapy covers the entire delayed emesis window'', ''DOES NOT INHIBIT CYP3A4 (unlike aprepitant): no need to adjust dexamethasone or chemotherapy doses'', ''100% oral bioavailability'']', '[''Must NOT be readministered sooner than every 14 days'', ''Inhibits CYP2D6, interacting with drugs like thioridazine and dextromethorphan'']', '[''Take 2 tablets (180 mg) once, 1 to 2 hours before your chemotherapy starts'', ''Protects against delayed nausea for 7 full days; never repeat within 2 weeks'']', 'ROLA-pitant: Remains Over 7 days (169h half-life); Outclasses aprepitant (no CYP3A4 block); Lasting Anti-vomiting.', '-pitant', 'roe-LA-pi-tant', 'रोला-पिटेंट', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('polyethylene-glycol', 'Polyethylene Glycol 3350 (PEG 3350)', 'PEG 3350 Powder for Oral Solution', 'Polyethylene glycol 3350', 'Polyethylene glycol 3350', 'Gastrointestinal', 'An unabsorbable, non-fermentable osmotic laxative that retains water in the intestinal lumen, serving as the gold standard first-line treatment for chronic constipation and bowel preparation.', '[''17 g powder sachet'', ''119 g, 255 g, 510 g bulk powder bottles'']', '[''Powder for Oral Solution'']', '[''Oral'']', 'High molecular weight linear polymer of ethylene glycol that forms hydrogen bonds with water molecules; remains entirely within the intestinal lumen without being absorbed, metabolized, or fermented by gut microbiota; creates an osmotic gradient that retains water inside the stool.', 'Hydrates, softens, and increases stool volume, restoring normal physiological bowel motility; does NOT cause electrolyte shifts, systemic dehydration, abdominal cramping, or bacterial flatulence (unlike lactulose).', 'Negligible gastrointestinal absorption (<0.2%); acts purely locally in the bowel lumen.', 'Confined 100% to the gastrointestinal tract; zero systemic tissue distribution.', 'Not metabolized by human enzymes; not fermented by colonic bacterial flora (zero gas production).', 'Excreted 100% unchanged in the faeces; the trace absorbed fraction (<0.2%) is excreted in urine.', '0% (negligible systemic absorption)', 'Not applicable (not absorbed)', 'Not applicable', 'Produces bowel movement in 24 to 72 hours (chronic constipation); 1 to 3 hours (colonoscopy high-volume prep)', 'Active while passing through bowel', '[''Chronic constipation (first-line gold standard medical therapy: 17 g powder dissolved in 120-240 mL water once daily)'', ''Fecal impaction in children and adults (high-dose regimen)'', ''Colonoscopy bowel preparation and radiological cleansing (large-volume solutions combined with electrolytes)'']', '[''Known or suspected mechanical bowel obstruction'', ''Bowel perforation or toxic megacolon'', "Severe active inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn''s, toxic colitis)"]', '[''Dissolve powder completely in 120 to 240 mL of water, juice, tea, or soda before drinking'', ''May take 24 to 72 hours (1-3 days) to produce a soft bowel movement'', ''Not for acute emergency relief of severe impaction (use enema first)'', ''Safe for daily long-term use without dependency or tolerance'']', '[''Abdominal bloating / fullness'', ''Mild nausea'', ''Cramping'', ''Loose stools / diarrhoea (with excessive doses)'']', '[''Severe diarrhea with dehydration and electrolyte depletion (rare)'', ''Aspiration pneumonia (with large-volume colon prep in obtunded patients)'']', '[''No pharmacokinetic systemic drug interactions; may transiently decrease absorption of other oral medications taken simultaneously (separate by 2 hours)'']', '[''Stool frequency and consistency (Bristol Stool Scale)'', ''Fluid intake and hydration status'']', 'Category C; virtually zero systemic absorption; gold standard safe laxative in pregnancy.', 'Not absorbed into maternal circulation; completely safe during breastfeeding.', 'Approved and first-line choice for pediatric chronic functional constipation (0.4 to 0.8 g/kg/day).', 'First-line laxative of choice in the elderly (does not cause electrolyte depletion or gas).', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal failure (not absorbed).', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease (not absorbed).', '[''Gold standard first-line laxative globally: produces gentle, natural, predictable bowel movements without cramping'', ''Not fermented by gut bacteria, causing significantly less gas and bloating than lactulose'', ''Safe for long-term daily use without causing bowel dependence, tolerance, or electrolyte disturbances'']', '[''Slow onset: takes 1 to 3 days to produce a bowel movement in chronic constipation'', ''Requires dissolving 17 grams in a full glass of liquid daily'']', '[''Mix 1 capful (17 grams) in a glass of water or juice once daily; completely tasteless'', ''Softens stool gently over 1 to 3 days without painful cramping or bloating'']', 'PEG-3350: Purely Enteric Gradient; Pulls water into stool; Eliminates Constipation; Zero gas; Zero absorption.', '-glycol', 'pol-ee-ETH-i-leen GLYE-kol', 'पॉलीएथिलीन ग्लाइकॉल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('sodium-picosulfate', 'Sodium Picosulfate', 'Sodium Picosulfate Oral Solution / Drops', 'Sodium picosulfate', 'Sodium picosulfate', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A locally acting stimulant laxative prodrug converted by colonic bacteria to active bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane (BHPM), used for constipation and bowel cleansing.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''7.5 mg/mL oral drops'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Oral Drops'', ''Powder for Oral Solution (with Magnesium Citrate)'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Inert prodrug that traverses the stomach and small intestine unabsorbed; converted specifically in the colon by bacterial arylsulfatases into active bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane (BHPM); stimulates mucosal nerve endings (myenteric plexus), accelerating colonic peristalsis and reducing water absorption.', 'Produces targeted colonic propulsive peristalsis and fluid accumulation, resulting in soft, formed bowel movements; when combined with magnesium citrate (Prepopik / Picoprep), provides low-volume, highly effective colonoscopy bowel cleansing.', 'Poorly absorbed from upper GI tract; active metabolite BHPM acts locally in the colon; minimal systemic absorption (<5%).', 'Confined primarily to the colonic lumen; absorbed trace metabolites undergo enterohepatic circulation.', 'Bioactivated in the colon by bacterial enzymes; absorbed fraction is glucuronidated in the liver.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces as active metabolite and parent drug; trace absorbed glucuronide excreted in urine.', 'Minimal (<5% systemic absorption)', 'Active colonic effect 6 to 12 hours post-dose', 'Low', 'Oral drops: 6 to 12 hours (taken at bedtime for morning relief)', 'Active for 1 bowel movement', '[''Short-term treatment of acute or chronic constipation (5 to 10 mg at bedtime)'', ''Colon cleansing prior to colonoscopy, barium enema, or colorectal surgery (combined with magnesium citrate)'']', '[''Known or suspected mechanical bowel obstruction or ileus'', ''Acute surgical abdomen (acute appendicitis, perforation)'', ''Severe acute inflammatory bowel disease / toxic megacolon'', ''Severe dehydration'']', '[''Take at bedtime to produce a predictable bowel movement the following morning (6-12 hours later)'', ''Adequate fluid intake is mandatory during colonoscopy bowel cleansing to prevent severe dehydration and hyponatremia'', ''Not for indefinite daily use (stimulant laxative habituation risk)'']', '[''Abdominal cramps (colicky griping pain)'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Severe dehydration and hypokalemia / hyponatremia (especially with bowel prep regimens)'', ''Syncope / vasovagal collapse during defecation'']', '[''Antibiotics (broad-spectrum oral antibiotics may destroy colonic flora, preventing bioactivation of sodium picosulfate)'', ''Diuretics and corticosteroids (additive hypokalemia risk)'']', '[''Stool consistency and frequency'', ''Serum electrolytes (sodium, potassium, BUN) when used for colonoscopy bowel cleansing'']', 'Avoid in the first trimester; limited safety data; use only if osmotic laxatives fail.', 'Not excreted in breast milk (active metabolite is not absorbed); compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 4 years and older (weight-adjusted oral drops: 2.5-5 mg at bedtime).', 'Higher risk of dehydration and electrolyte shifts; ensure adequate fluid intake.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease (activated by gut bacteria).', '[''Targeted colonic bioactivation prevents small intestinal cramping and irritation'', ''Convenient oral drops allow precise dose titration in children and adults'', ''Combined with magnesium citrate, provides low-volume, palatable bowel prep for colonoscopy'']', '[''Requires 6 to 12 hours for bacterial activation (not for instant relief)'', ''Colic and abdominal cramping can occur'']', '[''Take at bedtime with water to produce a gentle bowel movement the next morning'', ''Oral drops allow you to adjust the dose drop-by-drop to find your ideal relief'']', 'PICO-sulfate: Prodrug for Intestinal Cleansing; Colonic bacteria convert to active form; Overnight relief.', '-sulfate', 'SOE-dee-um pye-koe-SUL-fate', 'सोडियम पिको-सल्फ़ेट', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('prucalopride', 'Prucalopride', 'Prucalopride Succinate', 'Prucalopride succinate', 'Prucalopride succinate', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A first-in-class, highly selective 5-HT4 receptor agonist that stimulates high-amplitude propagating contractions (HAPCs), serving as the premier enterokinetic agent for chronic idiopathic constipation.', '[''1 mg'', ''2 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively and potently binds to 5-HT4 receptors on enteric neurons (with >150-fold selectivity over all other 5-HT and dopamine receptors); triggers proximal colonic high-amplitude propagating contractions (HAPCs) and enhances colonic mass movements.', 'Restores normal physiological colonic transit, accelerates whole-gut transit, increases complete spontaneous bowel movements (CSBMs), and relieves abdominal bloating and straining; does NOT block HERG potassium channels (zero QT prolongation or cardiotoxicity).', 'Rapidly and almost completely absorbed (>90%); peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours; unaffected by food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 567 L; low plasma protein binding (approx. 30%).', 'Minimally metabolized in the liver (approx. 15-20%) to eight minor metabolites; does not inhibit or induce CYP enzymes.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in urine via active secretion and filtration (approx. 60-65%); approx. 13% in faeces.', '>90%', '18 to 20 hours (ideal for true once-daily dosing)', '30%', 'Accelerates colonic transit within 2 to 4 hours; bowel movement usually on day 1', '24 hours (once daily)', '[''Treatment of chronic idiopathic constipation (CIC) in adults when laxatives fail to provide adequate relief (2 mg once daily; 1 mg in elderly/renal patients)'', ''Opioid-induced constipation (second-line)'', ''Intestinal pseudo-obstruction (refractory motility failure)'']', '[''Renal impairment requiring dialysis'', ''Intestinal perforation or mechanical bowel obstruction'', "Severe active inflammatory conditions of the GI tract (Crohn''s, ulcerative colitis, toxic megacolon)", ''Hypersensitivity to prucalopride'']', '[''Transient headache and nausea: occurs in up to 25% on Day 1 of therapy, but spontaneously resolves within 1 to 2 days (encourage patient to continue)'', ''Suicidal ideation / depression: monitor for worsening mood or emergence of suicidal thoughts'', ''Dose must be reduced to 1 mg daily in moderate-to-severe renal impairment (CrCl < 50 mL/min)'']', '[''Headache (very common on day 1; resolves in 24-48 hours)'', ''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Abdominal pain'', ''Fatigue'']', '[''Severe depression / suicidal ideation (rare)'', ''Severe diarrhoea causing dehydration'', ''Severe hypersensitivity rash'']', '[''Minimal CYP450 interactions; erythromycin and ketoconazole increase prucalopride levels slightly without clinical significance'', ''Atropine and anticholinergics (antagonize prokinetic efficacy)'']', '[''Bowel movement frequency (number of complete spontaneous bowel movements per week)'', ''Monitor for mood changes, depression, or suicidal ideation'', ''Renal function (eGFR) to adjust dose'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; limited human data; animal studies show no teratogenicity.', 'Excreted in breast milk in low concentrations; compatible with caution.', 'Approved in children 6 months and older in Europe for refractory chronic constipation.', 'Start with 1 mg once daily in patients >65 years; titrate to 2 mg if needed.', 'Dose reduction mandatory: if CrCl < 50 mL/min, reduce dose to 1 mg once daily; contraindicated in dialysis.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; use 1 mg in severe cirrhosis.', '[''True enterokinetic agent that triggers natural giant migrating contractions in the colon, restoring physiological defecation'', ''Exceptional selectivity for 5-HT4 receptors: ZERO cardiotoxicity, ZERO QT prolongation, and ZERO extrapyramidal effects'', ''Once-daily oral tablet taken at any time with or without food'']', '[''Headache and nausea are very common on Day 1 (affects 1 in 4 patients, but resolves by Day 2-3)'', ''High prescription cost compared to standard osmotic laxatives'']', '[''High-tech nerve stimulator for the colon that restarts sluggish bowels; take 2 mg once daily'', ''A mild headache on the first day is very common and disappears completely by the second day'']', 'PRUCALO-pride: Premier Propulsive Colonic Agonist; Pure 5-HT4; Resolves Obstinate constipation; Day-1 headache.', '-pride', 'proo-KAL-oh-pride', 'प्रुकालो-प्राइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('linaclotide', 'Linaclotide', 'Linaclotide Capsules', 'Linaclotide', 'Linaclotide', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A 14-amino acid synthetic peptide guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) agonist that increases intestinal fluid secretion and decreases visceral pain, used for IBS with constipation (IBS-C).', '[''72 mcg'', ''145 mcg'', ''290 mcg'']', '[''Capsule'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds to and activates guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) receptors on the luminal surface of intestinal enterocytes, elevating intracellular cyclic GMP (cGMP); cGMP activates CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), stimulating secretion of chloride and bicarbonate into the lumen, pulling water into the bowel; extracellular cGMP inhibits pain-sensing visceral nociceptive nerves.', 'Accelerates colonic transit, softens stools, increases bowel movement frequency, and directly relieves chronic abdominal pain and visceral hypersensitivity in IBS-C (significantly superior to placebo across all endpoints).', 'Negligible systemic absorption (<0.1%); acts purely locally on the luminal surface of the gut; MUST BE TAKEN ON AN EMPTY STOMACH AT LEAST 30 MINUTES BEFORE THE FIRST MEAL OF THE DAY.', 'Confined entirely to the gastrointestinal tract lumen; zero systemic distribution; zero plasma protein binding.', 'Degraded enzymatically within the gastrointestinal lumen into an active metabolite (MM-419447) and smaller inactive peptides by loss of the terminal tyrosine.', 'Excreted 100% in faeces as degraded peptide fragments; zero renal elimination.', '0% (negligible systemic absorption)', 'Not applicable (acts locally in gut lumen)', 'Not applicable', 'Increases bowel frequency within 24 to 48 hours; abdominal pain relief peaks at 8 to 12 weeks', 'Active throughout gut transit', '[''Irritable Bowel Syndrome with Constipation (IBS-C) in adults (290 mcg once daily on an empty stomach)'', ''Chronic Idiopathic Constipation (CIC) in adults (72 mcg or 145 mcg once daily)'']', '[''Known or suspected mechanical gastrointestinal obstruction'', ''PEDIATRIC PATIENTS UNDER 2 YEARS OF AGE (black box contraindication: high risk of fatal dehydration due to hyperactivation of fluid secretion)'']', '[''Black box warning: CONTRAINDICATED IN CHILDREN UNDER 2 YEARS OF AGE; avoided in children under 18 years due to risk of severe, life-threatening dehydration'', ''DIARRHOEA: Diarrhoea is the primary adverse reaction (occurs in up to 16-20%); severe diarrhea reported in 2% (withhold dose if severe diarrhea develops)'', ''MUST BE TAKEN ON AN EMPTY STOMACH: Taking with or after meals significantly increases diarrhea risk'', ''Keep in original protective bottle with desiccant (extremely moisture-sensitive)'']', '[''Diarrhoea (most common adverse effect: up to 16-20%)'', ''Abdominal pain and cramping'', ''Flatulence'', ''Abdominal distension'', ''Viral gastroenteritis'']', '[''Severe dehydrating diarrhea'', ''Severe hypokalemia / electrolyte collapse (with uncorrected severe diarrhea)'', ''Bowel obstruction / fecal impaction (rare)'']', '[''No systemic pharmacokinetic drug interactions (not absorbed into circulation)'', ''Concomitant laxatives may increase the frequency and severity of diarrhoea'']', '[''Stool frequency and consistency (monitor for severe watery diarrhea)'', ''Abdominal pain scores on validated IBS scales'', ''Patient compliance with taking 30 minutes before breakfast'']', 'Category C; virtually zero systemic absorption; animal studies show no teratogenicity; use only if clearly needed.', 'Not absorbed into maternal bloodstream; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'BLACK BOX CONTRAINDICATION: Strictly contraindicated in children under 2 years of age; not approved under 18 years.', 'Higher susceptibility to dehydration from diarrhea; start with lowest dose (72 mcg daily).', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment (not absorbed).', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease (not absorbed).', '[''Dual-action: accelerates bowel transit AND directly shuts off pain signals in visceral nerve fibers, relieving severe IBS stomach pain'', ''Zero systemic absorption eliminates all liver, kidney, cardiac, and drug-drug interactions'', ''FDA-approved for both IBS-C and chronic constipation'']', '[''High rate of diarrhea (affects up to 20% of patients)'', ''Must be taken 30 minutes before breakfast on an empty stomach'', ''Black box warning against use in young children'']', '[''Take once daily on an empty stomach at least 30 minutes before breakfast'', ''Relieves both constipation and chronic stomach cramps by pulling fluid into the bowel and calming gut nerves'']', 'LINA-clotide: Luminal GC-C agonist; Increases cGMP; No Absorption (safe); Abdominal pain relief in IBS-C.', '-clotide', 'li-na-KLOE-tide', 'लिना-क्लोटाइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('lubiprostone', 'Lubiprostone', 'Lubiprostone Soft Gelatin Capsules', 'Lubiprostone', 'Lubiprostone', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A bicyclic fatty acid prostaglandin E1 derivative that selectively activates type-2 chloride channels (ClC-2), stimulating mucosal fluid secretion for chronic constipation, IBS-C, and opioid constipation.', '[''8 mcg'', ''24 mcg'']', '[''Soft Gelatin Capsule'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively activates type-2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical membrane of human intestinal epithelial cells, triggering active chloride and passive sodium/water efflux into the intestinal lumen; promotes mucosal healing and restores tight junction barrier integrity.', 'Increases intestinal fluid secretion without altering serum electrolyte concentrations, accelerates colonic transit, softens stool, and relieves straining and bloating in chronic constipation and opioid-induced constipation.', 'Low systemic bioavailability; peak plasma concentrations of active metabolite (M3) in 1.1 hours; MUST BE TAKEN WITH FOOD AND WATER to prevent nausea.', 'Acts locally in the gut lumen; active metabolite M3 is 94% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Rapidly metabolized locally in the stomach and jejunum by carbonyl reductase to active metabolite M3 (15-hydroxy-lubiprostone).', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 60% as inactive metabolites) and faeces (approx. 30%); parent drug undetectable in plasma.', 'Negligible parent drug; active M3 bioavailability is low', 'M3 metabolite half-life is 0.9 to 1.4 hours', '94% (M3)', 'Increases bowel movements within 24 to 48 hours', '12 hours (dosed twice daily with food)', '[''Chronic idiopathic constipation in adults (24 mcg twice daily with food)'', ''Irritable Bowel Syndrome with Constipation (IBS-C) in adult women (8 mcg twice daily with food)'', ''Opioid-Induced Constipation (OIC) in adults with chronic non-cancer pain (24 mcg twice daily with food)'']', '[''Known or suspected mechanical gastrointestinal obstruction'', ''Severe diarrhea'', ''Known hypersensitivity to lubiprostone'']', '[''NAUSEA: Nausea is the most common side effect (occurs in up to 30%); TAKE WITH FOOD AND A FULL GLASS OF WATER to significantly minimize nausea'', ''DYSPNEA: Causes acute, transient sensation of chest tightness or shortness of breath within 30-60 minutes of the first dose, resolving within 3 hours'', ''Severe diarrhea: discontinue if severe watery diarrhea develops'']', '[''Nausea (very common: up to 30% without food)'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Abdominal pain'', ''Headache'', ''Flatulence'', ''Transient dyspnea'']', '[''Severe dehydrating diarrhea'', ''Severe chest tightness / acute dyspnea'', ''Syncope / hypotension'']', '[''Diphenylheptane opioids (methadone may potentially decrease lubiprostone efficacy via ClC-2 inhibition)'', ''No significant CYP450 drug interactions'']', '[''Stool frequency and consistency'', ''Assess for nausea (ensure taking with meals)'', ''Evaluate for chest tightness post-dose'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies risk (avoid in first trimester).', 'Excreted in animal milk in low levels; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone; monitor for nausea.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'Dose reduction required in moderate to severe hepatic impairment: moderate (Child-Pugh B): reduce to 16 mcg BID for CIC/OIC, 8 mcg BID for IBS-C; severe (Child-Pugh C): reduce to 8 mcg BID/OD.', '[''First-line secretagogue approved across three major indications: chronic constipation, IBS-C, and opioid-induced constipation'', ''Stimulates natural chloride secretion without creating systemic electrolyte depletion'', ''Enhances intestinal mucosal barrier repair'']', '[''High incidence of nausea (affects up to 30% of patients; must take with meals)'', ''Can cause frightening but harmless chest tightness/shortness of breath within 1 hour of dosing'', ''Twice-daily dosing required'']', '[''Take twice daily WITH MEALS and a full glass of water to prevent stomach upset'', ''A mild feeling of chest tightness may occur after the first dose but fades in a few hours'']', 'LUBI-prostone: Luminal Chloride-2 channel activator; Unclogs Bowels in IBS-C & Opioids; Ingest with food (stops nausea).', '-prostone', 'loo-bi-PROS-tone', 'लुबि-प्रोस्टोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('racecadotril', 'Racecadotril (Acetorphan)', 'Racecadotril Capsules / Sachets', 'Racecadotril', 'Racecadotril', 'Gastrointestinal', 'An oral enkephalinase inhibitor prodrug that prevents the breakdown of endogenous enkephalins, acting as a pure antisecretory antidiarrheal without slowing intestinal transit or causing rebound constipation.', '[''10 mg sachet'', ''30 mg sachet'', ''100 mg capsule'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Oral Granules Sachet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Prodrug rapidly hydrolyzed by systemic esterases to thiorphan, an active inhibitor of neutral endopeptidase (enkephalinase); prevents the degradation of endogenous opioid enkephalins in the intestinal mucosal wall; elevated enkephalins activate delta-opioid receptors, selectively suppressing cholera toxin- and rotavirus-induced hypersecretion of water and electrolytes.', 'Halts intestinal water and chloride hypersecretion without altering intestinal motility, transit time, or smooth muscle tone; does NOT cause abdominal distension, bacterial stagnation, rebound constipation, or central nervous system depression.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration of active thiorphan in 1 hour; bioavailability unaffected by food.', 'Confined primarily to peripheral tissues; 90% bound to plasma proteins (mainly albumin).', 'Rapidly hydrolyzed in tissues and blood to active thiorphan; thiorphan is further metabolized to inactive disulfide metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 81%) and faeces (approx. 8%) as inactive metabolites.', 'Rapidly converted to active thiorphan', '3 hours (active thiorphan)', '90%', 'Antisecretory antidiarrheal action begins within 30 minutes', '8 hours (dosed 100 mg TID; pediatric sachets 1.5 mg/kg TID)', '[''Complementary symptomatic treatment of acute watery diarrhoea in infants (>3 months), children, and adults, alongside oral rehydration therapy (ORT)'', ''Acute secretory diarrhoea due to rotavirus, cholera, or bacterial enterotoxins'', "Traveller''s diarrhoea (secretory type)"]', '[''Hypersensitivity to racecadotril'', ''Pregnancy and lactation'', ''Renal or hepatic impairment (due to lack of clinical data)'']', '[''NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR ORAL REHYDRATION: Must ALWAYS be co-administered with Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) solution'', ''Angioedema risk: contains thiorphan which inhibits enkephalinase and ACE; avoid in patients taking ACE inhibitors'', ''Do not use in invasive bacterial dysentery with bloody stools and high fever (requires antibiotics)'']', '[''Headache'', ''Tonsillitis'', ''Vomiting'', ''Skin rash / erythema'', ''Constipation (rare: <1%)'']', '[''Angioedema / facial swelling (especially in patients on ACE inhibitors)'', ''Severe bullous erythema multiforme / Stevens-Johnson syndrome (rare)'', ''Anaphylaxis'']', '[''ACE inhibitors (ramipril, enalapril - dual inhibition of ACE and neutral endopeptidase increases bradykinin, causing severe angioedema; avoid combination)'']', '[''Hydration status and stool frequency'', ''Signs of facial swelling or breathing difficulty'']', 'Contraindicated in pregnancy due to lack of human safety data.', 'Contraindicated during breastfeeding.', 'Extensively used and approved in infants from 3 months of age (tasty pediatric granules sachets mixed with food/water: 1.5 mg/kg TID).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'Use with caution; clinical data limited in renal impairment.', 'Use with caution; clinical data limited in hepatic impairment.', '[''Pure antisecretory antidiarrheal: stops fluid loss WITHOUT paralyzing the bowel (unlike loperamide)'', ''Zero risk of toxic megacolon or bacterial overgrowth because it preserves natural gut transit'', ''Approved and exceptionally safe in infants from 3 months of age'']', '[''Must be paired with oral rehydration solution (ORS); does not cure dehydration alone'', ''Risk of angioedema when combined with ACE inhibitors'']', '[''Safe antidiarrheal for children and adults that stops watery diarrhea without causing constipation'', ''Always drink Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS) alongside this medicine'']', 'RACE-cadotril: Reduces Aqueous Cl- Excretion; Enkephalinase inhibitor; Continues normal motility; Essential with ORS.', '-cadotril', 'ra-se-KA-doe-tril', 'रेसेका-डोट्रिल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('dicyclomine', 'Dicyclomine (Dicycloverine)', 'Dicyclomine Hydrochloride', 'Dicyclomine hydrochloride', 'Dicyclomine hydrochloride', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A dual-action anticholinergic and direct smooth muscle relaxant antispasmodic, used for intestinal colic, biliary colic, and abdominal cramping in irritable bowel syndrome.', '[''10 mg'', ''20 mg'', ''10 mg/5 mL syrup'', ''10 mg/mL injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Capsule'', ''Oral Syrup'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Acts via a dual mechanism: competitively antagonizes muscarinic M1, M2, and M3 acetylcholine receptors on visceral smooth muscle, AND exerts a direct, non-competitive musculotropic spasmolytic effect on smooth muscle fibers.', 'Relieves visceral smooth muscle spasm, stops painful hypermotility, reduces gastrointestinal cramping, and alleviates functional bowel spasms; possesses classical anticholinergic adverse effects.', 'Rapidly absorbed from GI tract; peak plasma concentrations in 1 to 1.5 hours; absolute oral bioavailability approx. 67%.', 'Widely distributed; crosses blood-brain barrier; volume of distribution approx. 3.6 L/kg; 80% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via hydroxylation and glucuronidation.', 'Excreted predominantly in urine (approx. 80%) and faeces (approx. 10%).', 'Approximately 67%', '9 to 10 hours', '80%', '30 to 60 minutes', '4 to 6 hours (dosed 10-20 mg TID or QID)', '[''Symptomatic treatment of abdominal cramps and spasm in Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)'', ''Intestinal colic and functional gastrointestinal cramping'', ''Biliary and renal colic spasm (adjunct analgesic antispasmodic)'']', '[''INFANTS UNDER 6 MONTHS OF AGE (black box contraindication: fatal respiratory arrest, seizures, syncope, and coma)'', ''Severe ulcerative colitis or toxic megacolon'', ''Obstructive uropathy / benign prostatic hyperplasia with urinary retention'', ''Glaucoma (narrow-angle)'', ''Myasthenia gravis'', ''Paralytic ileus or GI obstruction'']', '[''Black box warning: STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED IN INFANTS UNDER 6 MONTHS (causes apnea, respiratory collapse, seizures, and death; do NOT use for infant colic)'', ''Heat prostration: suppresses sweating; risk of heat stroke and hyperpyrexia in high ambient temperatures'', ''Anticholinergic CNS side effects: drowsiness, blurred vision, confusion in the elderly'', ''Never administer intravenously (IM only for injection)'']', '[''Dry mouth (xerostomia - very common)'', ''Blurred vision'', ''Dizziness'', ''Drowsiness'', ''Constipation'', ''Urinary hesitancy'']', '[''Fatal respiratory collapse / apnea (in infants <6 months)'', ''Acute angle-closure glaucoma'', ''Urinary retention / bladder distension'', ''Heat stroke / hyperthermia'']', '[''Antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, phenothiazines (additive anticholinergic toxicity)'', ''Potassium chloride tablets (delayed gastric emptying increases ulceration)'', ''Digoxin (increased digoxin absorption)'']', '[''Intraocular pressure in patients with glaucoma'', ''Urinary output in elderly males'', ''Signs of heat intolerance and sweat suppression'']', 'Category B; widely used with no documented teratogenic harm.', 'BLACK BOX CONTRAINDICATION: Strictly contraindicated during breastfeeding (excreted in milk and causes fatal infant respiratory arrest).', 'BLACK BOX CONTRAINDICATION in infants <6 months; approved in older children with weight-adjusted dosing.', 'High risk of acute urinary retention, glaucoma attacks, confusion, and falls; avoid or use lowest dose.', 'No dosage adjustment required; monitor urinary output.', 'Use with caution in hepatic disease.', '[''Dual antimuscarinic and direct smooth muscle relaxant action relieves severe colicky abdominal cramps within 30 minutes'', ''Available in tablets, syrups, and intramuscular injections for acute emergency colic'', ''Affordable, widely available antispasmodic'']', '[''LETHAL in infants under 6 months of age (causes respiratory arrest)'', ''High rate of anticholinergic side effects (dry mouth, blurred vision, dizziness)'']', '[''Quick relief for painful stomach cramps and intestinal spasms; take before meals as needed'', ''NEVER give to infants under 6 months of age (can cause breathing to stop)'']', 'DICYCLO-mine: Dual action (muscarinic + direct muscle); Decreases intestinal colic; Danger: Fatal in infants under 6 months.', '-mine', 'dye-SYE-kloe-meen', 'डाइसाइक्लो-मीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('drotaverine', 'Drotaverine', 'Drotaverine Hydrochloride', 'Drotaverine hydrochloride', 'Drotaverine hydrochloride', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor antispasmodic that relaxes visceral smooth muscle without anticholinergic side effects, widely used for biliary, renal, and gastrointestinal colic.', '[''40 mg'', ''80 mg'', ''40 mg/2 mL IV/IM ampoule'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase type 4 (PDE4) in visceral smooth muscle cells, preventing cyclic AMP (cAMP) breakdown; elevated intracellular cAMP phosphorylates myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK), inactivating it and causing smooth muscle relaxation; completely lacks antimuscarinic activity.', 'Rapidly relieves spasm and hypermotility of the gastrointestinal, biliary, and urogenital smooth muscle; does NOT cause dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, or glaucoma exacerbations (unlike dicyclomine and atropine).', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract (approx. 90%); peak plasma concentration in 45 minutes; bioavailability approx. 65% due to first-pass metabolism.', 'Widely distributed into smooth muscle and visceral organs; 95-98% bound to plasma proteins (predominantly albumin and beta-globulins).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver to glucuronide and phenolic metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 50%) and faeces (approx. 30%) as metabolites; zero unchanged parent drug.', 'Approximately 65%', '16 to 22 hours', '95% to 98%', 'Oral: 30 minutes; IV: 3 to 5 minutes', '8 to 12 hours (dosed 40-80 mg TID)', '[''Spasms of smooth muscle associated with biliary tract diseases (cholelithiasis, cholecystitis, cholangitis)'', ''Spasms of the urinary tract (nephrolithiasis, ureterolithiasis, renal colic, cystitis)'', ''Gastrointestinal smooth muscle spasms (spastic colon, IBS cramps, gastritis, peptic ulcer spasm)'', ''Dysmenorrhea / severe uterine menstrual cramping'', ''Cervical dilation facilitation during labour'']', '[''Severe hepatic, renal, or cardiac failure (cardiogenic shock, AV block)'', ''Known hypersensitivity to drotaverine hydrochloride'']', '[''Safe in glaucoma and benign prostatic hyperplasia (completely free of anticholinergic properties)'', ''Rapid IV injection can cause severe hypotension, collapse, and cardiac arrhythmias (administer slowly over 3-5 minutes with patient recumbent)'', ''Use caution in patients with baseline hypotension'']', '[''Dizziness / vertigo'', ''Headache'', ''Nausea'', ''Palpitations'', ''Hypotension (with rapid IV administration)'']', '[''Severe circulatory collapse / shock (with rapid IV push)'', ''Cardiac arrhythmias / AV block'', ''Anaphylaxis (rare)'']', '[''Levodopa (drotaverine significantly antagonizes the antiparkinsonian effect of levodopa, worsening tremors and rigidity)'']', '[''Blood pressure and heart rate during IV administration'', ''Relief of colicky abdominal, renal, or menstrual pain'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy unless clearly needed (safe for cervical dilation during active labor under obstetric supervision).', 'Excretion in breast milk unknown; avoid during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 1 year and older (weight-adjusted dosing: 20-40 mg TID).', 'No dosage adjustment required; safe in elderly patients with glaucoma or prostate enlargement.', 'Contraindicated in severe renal failure; no adjustment in mild to moderate impairment.', 'Contraindicated in severe hepatic failure; monitor in liver disease.', '[''Selectively inhibits PDE4: zero anticholinergic side effects (no dry mouth, no blurred vision, no constipation)'', ''Completely safe in patients with glaucoma and enlarged prostate (BPH) where dicyclomine is contraindicated'', ''Rapid onset within 30 minutes orally and 3 minutes intravenously for acute kidney stone colic'']', '[''Rapid IV injection causes severe blood pressure drop'', "Directly counteracts Parkinson''s medications (levodopa)"]', '[''Cramp-relieving medicine that does NOT dry your mouth or blur your vision'', ''Safe for patients with glaucoma and enlarged prostate'']', 'DROT-averine: Direct Relaxation of Tubules; PDE4 inhibitor; No Anticholinergic dryness; Safe in glaucoma/prostate.', '-averine', 'droe-TAV-e-reen', 'ड्रोतावे-रीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('mebeverine', 'Mebeverine', 'Mebeverine Hydrochloride', 'Mebeverine hydrochloride', 'Mebeverine hydrochloride', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A musculotropic antispasmodic with dual sodium-channel blocking and intracellular calcium store-depleting actions, specifically normalizing colonic motility in irritable bowel syndrome without systemic anticholinergic effects.', '[''135 mg'', ''200 mg SR'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Modified-Release Capsule'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Exerts a direct musculotropic antispasmodic action on gastrointestinal smooth muscle cells: blocks voltage-operated fast sodium channels (inhibiting spasm) AND inhibits calcium influx and intracellular calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum; also blocks noradrenaline reuptake locally.', 'Normalizes dysregulated, spastic colonic motor activity in irritable bowel syndrome without completely paralyzing normal peristalsis; zero systemic anticholinergic effects (does NOT cause dry mouth, urinary retention, or blurred vision).', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract; undergoes rapid and complete first-pass hydrolysis (parent drug undetectable in plasma); modified-release (SR) capsule provides sustained 12-hour plasma concentrations; take 20 minutes before meals.', 'Widely distributed into intestinal tissues; 75% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Extensively hydrolyzed in the liver by esterases to mebeverine alcohol and veratric acid; mebeverine alcohol is metabolized to active demethylated metabolites.', 'Excreted almost entirely in urine (approx. 95% as metabolites within 24 hours); zero parent drug excreted.', 'Rapidly metabolized; sustained release provides 12-hour coverage', '5.8 hours (active metabolite mebeveric acid)', '75%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 hours (200 mg SR capsule dosed twice daily 20 minutes before meals)', '[''Symptomatic relief of abdominal pain, cramps, bowel spasms, and intestinal discomfort in Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)'', ''Gastrointestinal spasm secondary to organic abdominal diseases'']', '['