('otilonium-bromide', 'Otilonium Bromide', 'Otilonium Bromide', 'Otilonium bromide', 'Otilonium bromide', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A quaternary ammonium antispasmodic with dual L-type calcium channel and tachykinin NK2 receptor antagonism, acting locally in the gut lumen with near-zero systemic absorption for IBS.', '[''40 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Triple action: blocks L-type and T-type voltage-gated calcium channels in intestinal smooth muscle cells, antagonizes tachykinin neurokinin-2 (NK2) receptors, and mildly antagonizes muscarinic M3 receptors in the colon wall.', 'Suppresses colonic hypermotility, relieves painful intestinal spasms, and reduces visceral pain sensitivity; acts locally within the gastrointestinal tract with negligible (<3%) systemic absorption, eliminating systemic anticholinergic and cardiovascular side effects.', 'Poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract (<3% systemic bioavailability); acts locally on colonic smooth muscle; take 20 minutes before meals.', 'Confined primarily to the intestinal wall; trace absorbed drug is bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized locally in the gut and trace absorbed fraction in the liver.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces via biliary and fecal elimination (approx. 95-97%); <1% in urine.', '<3% (acts purely locally in the bowel)', 'Terminal half-life of trace absorbed fraction is 4 to 6 hours', 'Low', '30 to 60 minutes', '8 hours (40 mg dosed 2 to 3 times daily before meals)', '[''Treatment of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS - relief of spastic abdominal pain and distension)'', ''Painful spastic conditions of the distal gastrointestinal tract'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to otilonium bromide'', ''Mechanical intestinal obstruction or paralytic ileus'']', '[''Take 20 minutes before meals'', ''Virtually free of systemic side effects due to near-zero absorption'', ''Safe in elderly patients'']', '[''Mild nausea'', ''Epigastric discomfort'', ''Dry mouth (mild, rare)'', ''Fatigue'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity rash (rare)'']', '[''No clinically significant systemic drug interactions due to negligible systemic absorption'']', '[''Reduction in abdominal pain intensity and weekly bowel spasm episodes'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human safety data.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in children not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required; excellent tolerability in elderly patients.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease.', '[''Virtually zero systemic absorption (<3%) guarantees exceptional safety with zero heart, brain, or urinary side effects'', ''Dual calcium channel and NK2 receptor blockade directly shuts down visceral pain transmission in the colon'', ''Proven long-term reduction in IBS abdominal pain and relapse rates'']', '[''Requires dosing 2 to 3 times daily before meals'', ''Not available in all Western markets (widely used in Europe, Asia, and India)'']', '[''Targeted colon spasm pill that stays inside the bowel without entering your bloodstream'', ''Take one 40 mg tablet 20 minutes before meals'']', 'OTILON-ium: Only Targets Intestinal Lumen; Negligible absorption (<3%); Relieves IBS spasms via calcium block.', '-ium', 'oh-ti-LOE-nee-um BROE-mide', 'ओटिलोनियम ब्रोमाइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('chlordiazepoxide-clidinium', 'Chlordiazepoxide and Clidinium Bromide (Librax)', 'Chlordiazepoxide 5 mg + Clidinium 2.5 mg Capsules', 'Chlordiazepoxide and clidinium bromide', 'Chlordiazepoxide hydrochloride and clidinium bromide', 'Gastrointestinal', 'A synergistic fixed-dose combination of a central anxiolytic benzodiazepine and a peripheral anticholinergic antispasmodic, used for stress-triggered irritable bowel syndrome and peptic ulcer disease.', '[''5 mg chlordiazepoxide + 2.5 mg clidinium bromide'']', '[''Capsule'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Chlordiazepoxide enhances GABA-A receptor opening frequency in the limbic system, dampening brain-gut stress axis activation and central anxiety; clidinium bromide competitively blocks muscarinic M3 receptors on gastrointestinal smooth muscle and secretory glands, suppressing bowel spasms and gastric acid.', 'Simultaneously calms visceral anxiety and relieves intestinal hypermotility and spasm; breaks the vicious cycle of emotional stress triggering severe IBS flare-ups and spastic colon.', 'Chlordiazepoxide is rapidly and completely absorbed (>90%); clidinium bromide is a quaternary ammonium compound with low and variable absorption (approx. 20-30%); take 30 to 60 minutes before meals and at bedtime.', 'Chlordiazepoxide distributes widely into the brain (96% protein bound); clidinium bromide distributes to peripheral muscarinic receptors.', 'Chlordiazepoxide is extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to active metabolites (desmethylchlordiazepoxide, demoxepam, oxazepam); clidinium undergoes ester hydrolysis.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 50-60%) and faeces.', 'Chlordiazepoxide: >90%; Clidinium: 20-30%', 'Chlordiazepoxide: 24 to 48 hours (active metabolites up to 100 hours); Clidinium: 2 to 4 hours', '96% (chlordiazepoxide)', '30 to 60 minutes', '8 to 12 hours (dosed 1 capsule 3-4 times daily before meals and at bedtime)', '[''Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) exacerbated by stress, tension, and emotional anxiety'', ''Peptic ulcer disease (adjunct antispasmodic therapy)'', ''Acute enterocolitis and spastic colon with high anxiety'']', '[''Glaucoma (narrow-angle)'', ''Prostatic hypertrophy / urinary retention'', ''Severe respiratory depression, sleep apnea, or severe COPD'', ''Myasthenia gravis'', ''Pregnancy (all trimesters)'', ''Concurrent use with opioids (black box warning)'']', '[''Black box warning: CONCOMITANT USE WITH OPIOIDS increases the risk of profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma, and death'', ''Black box warning: Physical dependence, abuse, and withdrawal seizures with chronic use (taper gradually)'', ''Anticholinergic risks: dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, and constipation'', ''Avoid alcohol completely (severe CNS depression)'']', '[''Drowsiness / sedation (very common)'', ''Dry mouth (very common)'', ''Blurred vision'', ''Constipation'', ''Dizziness'', ''Urinary hesitancy'']', '[''Respiratory depression / coma (especially when combined with opioids or alcohol)'', ''Acute angle-closure glaucoma'', ''Urinary retention / bladder distension'', ''Benzodiazepine withdrawal syndrome / seizures upon sudden cessation'']', '[''Opioids (black box warning: fatal respiratory arrest; avoid combination)'', ''Alcohol and CNS depressants (severe sedation and coma)'', ''CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, cimetidine - increase chlordiazepoxide levels)'']', '[''Signs of physical dependence, tolerance, or misuse'', ''Assess mental alertness, drowsiness, and fall risk'', ''Intraocular pressure and urinary stream in elderly males'']', 'Strictly contraindicated in pregnancy (Category D/X); benzodiazepines cause neonatal respiratory depression, floppiness, and withdrawal.', 'Contraindicated during breastfeeding; excreted in breast milk.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'High risk of severe sedation, confusion, falls, urinary retention, and cognitive impairment; generally avoided or used with extreme caution.', 'Dose reduction required in renal impairment.', 'Dose reduction mandatory in severe hepatic impairment (chlordiazepoxide clearance is heavily liver-dependent).', '[''Combines central anxiety relief with peripheral gut spasm relief, breaking the stress-IBS connection'', ''Fast-acting relief for acute emotional-induced irritable bowel flare-ups'', ''Established clinical efficacy over decades'']', '[''Black box warnings for addiction, dependence, and fatal overdose with opioids'', ''High rate of drowsiness, dry mouth, blurred vision, and urinary retention'', ''Cannot be stopped abruptly (causes withdrawal)'']', '[''Calms both nervous tension and stomach cramps; take before meals and at bedtime'', ''Never drink alcohol or take opioid painkillers while using this medication; can cause drowsiness'']', 'LIBRAX: Links Intestine & Brain; Relieves Anxiety (chlordiazepoxide) & X-terminates cramps (clidinium).', '-poxide', 'klor-dye-az-e-POX-ide kli-DI-nee-um', 'क्लोर्डियाज़ेपॉक्साइड क्लिडीनियम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('ursodeoxycholic-acid', 'Ursodeoxycholic Acid (UDCA / Ursodiol)', 'Ursodeoxycholic Acid', 'Ursodeoxycholic acid', 'Ursodeoxycholic acid', 'Hepatology', 'A naturally occurring hydrophilic tertiary bile acid that displaces toxic hydrophobic bile salts, serving as the gold standard first-line disease-modifying treatment for primary biliary cholangitis (PBC).', '[''150 mg'', ''300 mg'', ''600 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Multiple hepatoprotective mechanisms: replaces toxic hydrophobic bile acids (cholic, deoxycholic, chenodeoxycholic acid) in the circulating bile acid pool; stimulates hepatobiliary secretion and bicarbonate secretion (''bicarbonate umbrella''); suppresses hepatocyte and cholangiocyte apoptosis; solubilizes cholesterol into liquid-crystalline mesophases, promoting dissolution of cholesterol gallstones.', 'Significantly slows disease progression, reduces serum bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase, prevents hepatic cirrhosis, and delays or eliminates the need for liver transplantation in primary biliary cholangitis (PBC); dissolves radiolucent gallstones in patients refusing cholecystectomy.', 'Well absorbed from small intestine (approx. 90%); undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass extraction (approx. 50-70%); take with meals to improve biliary secretion.', 'Concentrates heavily in bile, liver, and biliary tree; low systemic plasma concentrations; approx. 70% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Conjugated in the liver with glycine and taurine, then excreted into bile; undergoes extensive enterohepatic recirculation; small fraction 7-dehydroxylated by colonic bacteria to lithocholic acid (a hepatotoxic metabolite detoxified by sulfation).', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 99% via biliary route); less than 1% in urine.', 'Approximately 90% (fasting/meals)', '3.5 to 5.8 days (biological elimination half-life in bile acid pool)', '70%', 'Biliary enrichment reaches plateau within 3 to 4 weeks; gallstone dissolution requires 6 to 24 months', 'Continuous while administered', '[''Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC - gold standard first-line treatment: 13-15 mg/kg/day divided BID or once daily)'', ''Dissolution of radiolucent, non-calcified gallstones <20 mm in patients who are high surgical risks or refuse cholecystectomy'', ''Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy (ICP - relieves intense maternal pruritus and normalizes bile acids)'', ''Primary sclerosing cholangitis (symptomatic biochemical improvement; high doses >28 mg/kg avoided)'', ''Cystic fibrosis-associated hepatobiliary disease'']', '[''Complete mechanical biliary obstruction'', ''Acute cholecystitis, cholangitis, or gallstone pancreatitis'', ''Calcified radiopaque gallstones or non-functioning gallbladder'', ''Severe hypersensitivity to bile acids'']', '[''Long-term therapy required: gallstone dissolution takes 6 to 24 months (ultrasound every 6 months); gallstones recur in 50% within 5 years after stopping'', ''Avoid high doses in Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (>28-30 mg/kg/day increased mortality in trials)'', ''Monitor liver enzymes regularly during therapy'']', '[''Diarrhoea and loose stools (most common: up to 5-10%)'', ''Nausea'', ''Abdominal discomfort'', ''Pruritus (rare; usually relieves pruritus)'', ''Alopecia / hair thinning (rare)'']', '[''Worsening of cholestasis / hepatotoxicity (rare in PBC)'', ''Calcification of preexisting cholesterol gallstones'', ''Severe allergic rash'']', '[''Bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine, colestipol - bind UDCA in the gut; separate by at least 4 to 5 hours)'', ''Aluminum-based antacids (bind bile acids; separate by 2 hours)'', ''Estrogens, oral contraceptives, and fibrates (increase biliary cholesterol secretion, counteracting gallstone dissolution)'']', '[''Liver function tests (serum alkaline phosphatase, ALT, AST, total bilirubin) every 3 months for the first year, then every 6 months'', ''Biliary ultrasound every 6 months for gallstone dissolution monitoring'', ''Serum bile acid