('obeticholic-acid', 'Obeticholic Acid (OCA)', 'Obeticholic Acid', 'Obeticholic acid', 'Obeticholic acid', 'Hepatology', 'A potent semi-synthetic farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist that suppresses bile acid synthesis and enhances biliary output, used for primary biliary cholangitis unresponsive to UDCA.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds directly and potently to the nuclear farnesoid X receptor (FXR) with 100-fold higher affinity than chenodeoxycholic acid; activation of FXR down-regulates cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1), the rate-limiting enzyme in bile acid synthesis, and up-regulates bile salt export pump (BSEP) and fibroblast growth factor 19 (FGF19).', 'Dramatically reduces toxic intracellular bile acid accumulation in hepatocytes, suppresses hepatic inflammation, and inhibits hepatic stellate cell fibrogenesis; significantly reduces serum alkaline phosphatase and total bilirubin in PBC patients failing UDCA.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1.5 hours; absolute bioavailability not established; take with or without food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 618 L; highly bound to human plasma proteins (>99%).', 'Conjugated in the liver with glycine and taurine to active glyco- and tauro-obeticholic acid, which possess equal FXR agonist activity; undergoes enterohepatic recirculation.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 87% via biliary elimination); less than 3% in urine.', 'High biliary uptake', '24 hours (conjugated metabolites half-life prolonged in enterohepatic cycle)', '>99%', 'Reduces alkaline phosphatase within 2 to 4 weeks', '24 hours (once daily)', '[''Treatment of Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC) in combination with ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) in adults with an inadequate response to UDCA, or as monotherapy in adults intolerant to UDCA'']', '[''DECOMPENSATED CIRRHOSIS (Child-Pugh Class B or C, or patients with prior decompensation events, ascites, or variceal bleeding - black box contraindication: causes fatal hepatic failure)'', ''Complete biliary obstruction'', ''Known hypersensitivity to obeticholic acid'']', '[''Black box warning: HEPATIC DECOMPENSATION AND FAILURE IN CIRRHOTIC PBC PATIENTS: Inadvertently high or daily dosing in patients with moderate to severe hepatic impairment has resulted in liver failure and death; STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in decompensated cirrhosis'', ''SEVERE PRURITUS: Dose-dependent, intense pruritus occurs in up to 60-70% of patients (manage with bile acid resins, antihistamines, or dose reduction)'', ''Elevates LDL cholesterol and reduces HDL cholesterol'']', '[''Severe pruritus (intense itching in up to 60-70% of patients)'', ''Fatigue'', ''Abdominal pain and discomfort'', ''Arthralgia'', ''Constipation'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Fatal hepatic decompensation / acute liver failure'', ''Severe intractable pruritus requiring discontinuation'', ''Marked dyslipidemia / cardiovascular risk'']', '[''Bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine, colesevelam - bind obeticholic acid; separate by at least 4 to 6 hours)'', ''Warfarin (obeticholic acid may decrease INR; monitor prothrombin time)'', ''CYP1A2 substrates (theophylline, tizanidine - increases drug levels)'']', '[''Serum alkaline phosphatase and total bilirubin regularly'', ''Liver function and clinical signs of decompensation (ascites, jaundice, encephalopathy) - STOP IMMEDIATELY if decompensation occurs'', ''Lipid profile (LDL, HDL, triglycerides) at baseline and periodically'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; limited human data; animal studies show fetal toxicity at maternal toxic doses.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone; monitor liver function closely.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'BLACK BOX CONTRAINDICATION: Strictly contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B and C); in compensated cirrhosis, start at 5 mg once weekly, not daily.', '[''Potent nuclear FXR agonist that lowers toxic bile acid synthesis at the genetic transcription level'', ''Second-line lifeline for patients with Primary Biliary Cholangitis failing ursodeoxycholic acid'', ''Proven significant reduction in alkaline phosphatase and fibrosis markers'']', '[''BLACK BOX WARNING: Triggers fatal liver failure if given to patients with advanced or decompensated cirrhosis'', ''Severe, intense itching (pruritus) in up to 70% of patients'']', '[''Second-line liver medication for primary biliary cholangitis when UDCA alone is not enough'', ''DANGER: Strictly forbidden in advanced liver cirrhosis; report worsening jaundice or belly swelling immediately'']', 'OBETI-cholic: Operates on Bile via FXR; Exclusively for non-decompensated PBC; Terrible itching (pruritus); Inappropriate in cirrhosis.', '-cholic', 'oh-BET-i-KOE-lik AS-id', 'ओबेटिकोलिक एसिड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('silymarin', 'Silymarin (Milk Thistle Extract)', 'Silymarin Capsules / Tablets', 'Silymarin (flavonolignans: silybin, silychristin, silydianin)', 'Silymarin extract', 'Hepatology', 'A standardized botanical extract of Silybum marianum containing flavonolignans (silybin) with potent antioxidant, membrane-stabilizing, and antifibrotic hepatoprotective properties.', '[''70 mg'', ''140 mg'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Multiple hepatoprotective actions: scavenges free radicals, prevents lipid peroxidation of hepatocyte membranes, stimulates RNA polymerase I to accelerate ribosomal protein synthesis and liver regeneration, and inhibits nuclear factor-kappa B (NF-kB) to suppress inflammatory cytokines and hepatic stellate cell activation.', 'Inhibits toxin penetration into hepatocytes (protects against death-cap mushroom Amanita phalloides poisoning by blocking amatoxin entry); reduces liver enzymes (ALT, AST) in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and toxic liver injury.', 'Poorly absorbed from GI tract (20-50% bioavailability) due to low water solubility; take with meals to enhance absorption.', 'Rapidly taken up by hepatocytes; low systemic distribution; 75-90% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via glucuronidation and sulfation; undergoes enterohepatic recirculation.', 'Excreted predominantly in bile and faeces (approx. 80%); only 3-8% excreted in urine.', '20% to 50%', '6 hours (elimination half-life of free silybin)', '75% to 90%', 'Antioxidant and hepatoprotective effects develop over 4 to 8 weeks', '8 hours (dosed 140 mg TID with meals)', '[''Supportive adjuvant therapy in chronic liver diseases (non-alcoholic fatty liver disease - NAFLD/NASH, alcoholic liver disease, chronic viral hepatitis)'', ''Protection against hepatotoxins and drug-induced liver injury'', ''Intravenous silybin is the specific emergency antidote for Amanita phalloides (death cap mushroom) poisoning'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to silymarin or plants of the Asteraceae / Compositae family (daisies, ragweed, marigolds)'']', '[''Botanical adjuvant therapy: NOT a substitute for eliminating root causes (alcohol cessation, weight loss in NAFLD, or antiviral therapy in Hepatitis B/C)'', ''Mild laxative effect at higher doses (due to increased bile secretion)'', ''Allergic reactions in ragweed-sensitive patients'']', '[''Mild diarrhoea / loose stools'', ''Bloating and flatulence'', ''Nausea'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe allergic anaphylaxis (in patients with Asteraceae / ragweed allergy - rare)'']', '[''Mild inhibition of CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 at high concentrations; monitor narrow-index substrates (warfarin, phenytoin)'', ''Metronidazole (may modestly decrease metronidazole exposure)'']', '[''Serum transaminases (ALT, AST) and ultrasound for hepatic steatosis resolution'', ''Tolerance and gastrointestinal symptoms'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy due to limited clinical safety data.', 'Excretion in breast milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in young children not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone; excellent tolerability.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'Safe supportive agent across chronic liver conditions; no dose adjustment required.', '[''Natural antioxidant and hepatoprotective agent with an exceptional safety profile over decades of use'', ''Proven live-saving parenteral antidote for fatal Amanita phalloides mushroom poisoning'', ''Stimulates hepatic protein synthesis and natural liver cellular repair mechanisms'']', '[''Low oral bioavailability requires taking capsules three times daily with meals'', ''Acts as an adjuvant; cannot replace medical treatment for viral hepatitis or alcohol abstinence'']', '[''Natural milk thistle liver protector that shields liver cells from toxins and oxidative stress'', ''Take one 140 mg capsule three times daily with meals'']', 'SILY-marin: Shield for Liver; Inhibits lipid peroxidation; Life-saving in Amanita mushroom poisoning; Yeast/plant derived.', '-marin', 'sil-i-MA-rin', 'सिली-मारिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('indacaterol', 'Indacaterol', 'Indacaterol Maleate Inhalation Powder', 'Indacaterol maleate', 'Indacaterol maleate', 'Respiratory', 'An ultra-long-acting beta-2 adrenergic agonist (ultra-LABA) providing fast-onset (5 minutes) and 24-hour bronchodilation for once-daily maintenance therapy in COPD.', '[''75 mcg'', ''150 mcg'', ''300 mcg inhalation capsules'']', '[''Dry Powder Inhaler (DPI) Capsule'']', '[''Inhalation'']', 'Selective agonist at beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle; stimulates intracellular adenylate cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP, activating protein kinase A (PKA); reduces intracellular calcium and induces profound bronchial relaxation.', 'Produces rapid bronchodilation within 5 minutes of inhalation that persists for a full 24 hours; improves FEV1, reduces lung hyperinflation, relieves dyspnea, and reduces COPD exacerbations.', 'Inhaled fraction absorbed rapidly from lung tissue; swallowed fraction undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism; systemic bioavailability approx. 43%.', 'Widely distributed into lung tissue; volume of distribution approx. 2,557 L; 95% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver primarily by UGT1A1 (glucuronidation) and CYP3A4 (hydroxylation).', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 85% via biliary excretion); approx. 10% in urine as metabolites and unchanged drug.', 'Approximately 43%', '40 to 52 hours (supports true once-daily dosing)', '95%', 'Fast onset: within 5 minutes', '24 hours (once daily)', '[''Long-term once-daily maintenance bronchodilator treatment of airflow limitation in patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)'', ''Combined with glycopyrronium (Ultibro Breezhaler) for dual LABA/LAMA COPD maintenance'']', '[''Asthma without concomitant inhaled corticosteroid (LABA monotherapy black box warning)'', ''Known hypersensitivity to indacaterol or lactose (capsule carrier)'']', '[''Black box warning: LABA monotherapy in asthma increases the risk of asthma-related death (MUST ALWAYS BE COMBINED WITH AN INHALED STEROID IN ASTHMA; approved primarily for COPD)'', ''Paradoxical bronchospasm: may cause acute, life-threatening bronchospasm immediately after inhalation (treat with short-acting SABA)'', ''Cardiovascular effects: tachycardia, palpitations, hypertension, and arrhythmias in susceptible patients'']', '[''Nasopharyngitis / upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Cough (transient post-inhalation cough in up to 15-20%, resolving in seconds)'', ''Headache'', ''Muscle spasms'', ''Tremor'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm (immediate, life-threatening)'', ''Hypokalemia / hyperglycemia'', ''Cardiac arrhythmias / atrial fibrillation / QT prolongation'']', '[''Beta-blockers (cardioselective and non-selective - antagonize bronchodilator effect and may cause bronchospasm)'', ''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin - modestly increase indacaterol exposure)'', ''QT-prolonging drugs (hypokalemia and additive QT prolongation)'']', '[''Spirometry (FEV1 and FVC) at baseline and periodic follow-up'', ''Inhalation technique verification (ensure patient pierces capsule and inhales forcefully)'', ''Serum potassium and blood glucose in high-risk patients'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients under 18 years not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Ultra-fast 5-minute onset combined with a true 24-hour duration of action in a single daily inhalation'', ''Exceptional affinity and 24-fold higher beta-2 selectivity than formoterol'', ''Significantly reduces COPD exacerbations and improves quality of life'']', '[''Transient cough immediately after inhaling the dry powder (lasts 10-15 seconds; warn patient)'', ''Capsule must be loaded into the Breezhaler device, requiring good manual dexterity'']', '[''Inhale one capsule once daily at the same time every day using the Breezhaler device'', ''Do NOT swallow the capsule; a brief 10-second cough right after inhaling is harmless'']', 'INDA-caterol: Instant 5-minute onset; Non-stop 24-hour bronchodilation; Daily single dose; Asthma monotherapy outlawed.', '-caterol', 'in-da-KA-ter-ol', 'इन्डा-कैटेरोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('vilanterol', 'Vilanterol', 'Vilanterol Trifenatate (in Breo / Anoro Ellipta)', 'Vilanterol trifenatate', 'Vilanterol trifenatate', 'Respiratory', 'An ultra-long-acting beta-2 agonist (ultra-LABA) with 24-hour bronchodilation, co-formulated with fluticasone furoate (Breo) or umeclidinium (Anoro) in the once-daily Ellipta dry powder inhaler.', '[''25 mcg (co-formulated with fluticasone furoate or umeclidinium)'']', '[''Dry Powder Inhaler (Ellipta)'']', '[''Inhalation'']', 'Highly selective, long-acting beta-2 adrenergic receptor agonist; stimulates intracellular adenylate cyclase, elevating cyclic AMP; relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and inhibits release of inflammatory mediators from mast cells.', 'Produces sustained 24-hour bronchodilation with a rapid onset (within 10-15 minutes); significantly improves trough FEV1 and reduces annual rate of moderate-to-severe COPD and asthma exacerbations.', 'Oral inhalation delivers drug directly to the bronchial tree; swallowed portion is extensively metabolized; absolute oral bioavailability of vilanterol is <1%; total systemic bioavailability approx. 27%.', 'Widely distributed into lung tissue; volume of distribution at steady state approx. 165 L; 94% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Rapidly and extensively metabolized by CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites; virtually zero circulating parent drug after systemic absorption.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 70%) and faeces (approx. 30%) as inactive metabolites.', 'Approximately 27% (inhaled fraction)', '11 hours in plasma (supports true 24-hour pharmacodynamic duration)', '94%', '10 to 15 minutes', '24 hours (once daily in Ellipta device)', '[''Maintenance treatment of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD - combined with umeclidinium or fluticasone furoate)'', ''Maintenance treatment of Asthma in patients aged 12 years and older (ALWAYS combined with fluticasone furoate - Breo Ellipta)'']', '[''Primary treatment of status asthmaticus or acute episodes of asthma/COPD requiring intensive measures'', ''Asthma monotherapy without an inhaled corticosteroid (LABA black box warning)'', ''Severe hypersensitivity to milk proteins (contains lactose)'']', '[''Always co-formulated with an inhaled corticosteroid (fluticasone furoate) for asthma to eliminate LABA monotherapy mortality risk'', ''Paradoxical bronchospasm: discontinue immediately if bronchospasm occurs'', ''Cardiovascular effects: tachycardia, arrhythmias, and hypertension'', ''Rinse mouth with water without swallowing after inhalation (when combined with steroid)'']', '[''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Headache'', ''Candidiasis of mouth and throat (due to fluticasone component)'', ''Dysphonia (hoarseness)'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm (immediate)'', ''Cardiac arrhythmias / atrial fibrillation / tachycardia'', ''Hypokalemia / hyperglycemia'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin - increase systemic vilanterol levels; monitor heart rate)'', ''Beta-adrenergic receptor blockers (antagonize bronchodilation; may provoke severe bronchospasm)'', ''Non-potassium sparing diuretics (additive hypokalemia)'']', '[''FEV1 and clinical symptom scores'', ''Oral cavity inspection for thrush (oral candidiasis)'', ''Heart rate and blood pressure'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved for asthma in adolescents 12 years and older (Breo Ellipta 100/25 mcg).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; monitor in severe cirrhosis.', '[''True once-daily 24-hour ultra-LABA paired with fluticasone furoate in the user-friendly Ellipta inhaler'', ''Rapid onset within 15 minutes'', ''Extensively cleared by CYP3A4, resulting in low systemic exposure and excellent cardiac safety'']', '[''Available only in fixed-dose combinations (Breo Ellipta, Anoro Ellipta, Trelegy Ellipta); not sold as monotherapy'', ''Must rinse mouth after use to prevent oral thrush'']', '[''Inhale one puff once daily at the exact same time every day'', ''Rinse your mouth with water and spit it out after inhaling to prevent mouth infections'']', 'VILAN-terol: Versatile Inhaled LABA; All-day 24h control; Never used alone in asthma (Breo Ellipta).', '-terol', 'vil-AN-ter-ol', 'विलैन-टेरॉल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('olodaterol', 'Olodaterol', 'Olodaterol Inhalation Solution (Striverdi Respimat)', 'Olodaterol hydrochloride', 'Olodaterol hydrochloride', 'Respiratory', 'A once-daily ultra-long-acting beta-2 agonist delivered via the propellant-free Respimat Soft Mist Inhaler, providing 24-hour bronchodilation for COPD.', '[''2.5 mcg per actuation (dose = 2 puffs = 5 mcg)'']', '[''Inhalation Solution (Respimat Soft Mist Inhaler)'']', '[''Inhalation'']', 'Highly selective and potent beta-2 adrenergic receptor agonist; binds with high affinity to human beta-2 receptors (241-fold higher selectivity over beta-1); stimulates adenylate cyclase, elevating cAMP and relaxing airway smooth muscle.', 'Provides rapid bronchodilation within 5 minutes that lasts for over 24 hours; Respimat Soft Mist generation produces a slow-moving, long-lasting aerosol plume that maximizes deep lung deposition and minimizes oropharyngeal impaction.', 'Inhaled fraction rapidly absorbed through lungs; swallowed portion poorly bioavailable; total systemic bioavailability is approx. 30%.', 'Widely distributed; volume of distribution approx. 1,110 L; 60% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized by glucuronidation (UGT2B7, UGT1A1, UGT1A9) and O-demethylation by CYP2C9 and CYP2C8.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 53%) and urine (approx. 38% as metabolites); unchanged drug accounts for <5% in urine.', 'Approximately 30%', '45 hours (terminal elimination half-life)', '60%', 'Within 5 minutes', '24 hours (2 actuations once daily)', '[''Long-term, once-daily maintenance bronchodilator treatment of airflow limitation in patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)'', ''Combined with tiotropium (Spiolto Respimat) for dual bronchodilator therapy'']', '[''All LABAs are contraindicated in patients with asthma without the use of a long-term asthma controller medication (black box warning)'', ''Hypersensitivity to olodaterol'']', '[''Black box warning: Asthma-related death risk with LABA monotherapy (indicated ONLY for COPD, not asthma)'', ''Respimat Soft Mist Inhaler: requires slow, deep inhalation while pressing dose-release button'', ''Cardiovascular effects: tachycardia, palpitations, arrhythmias'', ''Paradoxical bronchospasm'']', '[''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Bronchitis'', ''Cough'', ''Dizziness'', ''Rash'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm'', ''Tachyarrhythmias / atrial fibrillation'', ''Hypokalemia / hyperglycemia'']', '[''Beta-blockers (antagonize bronchodilation; avoid non-selective beta-blockers)'', ''Adrenergic agonists (additive sympathetic stimulation)'', ''Diuretics (additive risk of hypokalemia)'']', '[''FEV1 and COPD assessment test (CAT) scores'', ''Respimat inhalation technique verification at every visit'', ''Heart rate and ECG in patients with cardiovascular disease'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excreted in animal milk in low amounts; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Respimat Soft Mist Inhaler creates a slow-moving cloud that requires less inspiratory effort than dry powder inhalers, ideal for weak or severe COPD patients'', ''Rapid 5-minute onset with sustained 24-hour bronchodilation'', ''Clean metabolic profile independent of CYP3A4'']', '[''Propellant-free Respimat device requires proper assembly and turning (TOP technique: Turn, Open, Press)'', ''Indicated only for COPD; not approved for asthma'']', '[''Take 2 puffs once daily at the same time every day using the Respimat Soft Mist Inhaler'', ''Breathe in slowly and deeply when you press the button; the gentle mist lasts over 1 second'']', 'OLODA-terol: Once-daily LABA for Obstruction; Delivered via Aerosol mist (Respimat); 24-hour action.', '-terol', 'oh-loe-DA-ter-ol', 'ओलोडै-टेरॉल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('glycopyrronium', 'Glycopyrronium (Glycopyrrolate)', 'Glycopyrronium Inhalation Powder / Injection', 'Glycopyrronium bromide', 'Glycopyrronium bromide', 'Respiratory', 'A quaternary ammonium long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) with fast onset and high M3-receptor selectivity, used as once-daily maintenance for COPD and parenterally to reduce secretions.', '[''50 mcg inhalation capsule (Seebri Breezhaler)'', ''0.2 mg/mL IV/IM vial'', ''1 mg, 2 mg oral tablets'']', '[''Inhalation Powder'', ''Solution for Injection'', ''Oral Tablet'']', '[''Inhalation'', ''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Oral'']', 'Quaternary ammonium antimuscarinic agent; competitively and reversibly blocks muscarinic M3 receptors on airway smooth muscle cells, inhibiting acetylcholine-induced bronchoconstriction; dissociates significantly faster from M2 receptors than M3 receptors, ensuring prolonged bronchodilation without tachycardia.', 'Produces rapid and sustained 24-hour bronchodilation, improves trough FEV1, reduces lung hyperinflation and air trapping, and significantly decreases moderate-to-severe COPD exacerbations; parenterally reduces salivation, tracheobronchial secretions, and gastric acidity.', 'Inhaled fraction rapidly absorbed through lungs; systemic bioavailability from inhalation is approx. 40%; quaternary ammonium structure prevents significant GI absorption of swallowed drug (<5%).', 'Confined primarily to the extracellular fluid; volume of distribution approx. 400 L; quaternary charge prevents crossing the blood-brain barrier; 38-44% protein bound.', 'Minimally metabolized in the liver (approx. 10-15%) via hydroxylation by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in urine via active tubular secretion (approx. 60-70% of systemic dose); remainder in bile/faeces.', 'Approximately 40% (inhaled)', '13 to 24 hours in plasma; biological M3 dissociation half-life is 6.1 hours (sustains 24-hour bronchodilation)', '38% to 44%', 'Fast onset: within 5 minutes of inhalation', '24 hours (once daily in Breezhaler)', '[''Maintenance bronchodilator treatment to relieve symptoms in adult patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)'', ''Dual combination with indacaterol (Ultibro Breezhaler) or triple with indacaterol/mometasone'', ''Parenteral: reduction of salivary, tracheobronchial, and pharyngeal secretions preoperatively or in palliative care / ALS'', ''Severe chronic drooling (sialorrhea) in pediatric cerebral palsy (oral solution)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to glycopyrronium or lactose'', ''Severe urinary retention or myasthenia gravis'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm: discontinue immediately and treat with short-acting bronchodilator'', ''Narrow-angle glaucoma: protect eyes from inhalation powder or mist (can trigger acute angle closure)'', ''Urinary retention: use with caution in patients with prostatic hypertrophy or bladder neck obstruction'', ''Dose adjustment required in severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min)'']', '[''Dry mouth (xerostomia - most common anticholinergic effect, usually mild)'', ''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Insomnia'', ''Urinary tract infection'', ''Gastroenteritis'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm (immediate)'', ''Acute angle-closure glaucoma (if powder enters eyes)'', ''Severe urinary retention / bladder distension'', ''Supraventricular tachycardia (rare)'']', '[''Anticholinergic agents (ipratropium, tiotropium, aclidinium - additive anticholinergic toxicity; avoid concurrent use)'', ''Potassium chloride tablets (delayed gastric emptying increases mucosal injury)'']', '[''FEV1 and COPD symptom scores'', ''Urinary stream in elderly men with prostate disease'', ''Renal function (creatinine clearance) in elderly or renal patients'']', 'Category B; quaternary ammonium structure does NOT cross the placenta in significant amounts; safe when indicated.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; quaternary charge prevents significant infant absorption; compatible with caution.', 'Approved for severe drooling in pediatric neurological disorders (oral solution).', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients with normal renal function.', 'Renal clearance accounts for 60-70%: in severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min) or ESRD on dialysis, monitor closely for anticholinergic side effects.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''Quaternary ammonium charge prevents penetration into the brain: ZERO central anticholinergic confusion, delirium, or drowsiness'', ''Fast onset of action within 5 minutes, significantly faster than tiotropium'', ''High kinetic selectivity for M3 receptors over M2 receptors ensures minimal cardiac heart rate stimulation'']', '[''Dry mouth in up to 5-10% of patients'', ''Cleared predominantly by kidneys; risk of accumulation in severe renal failure'']', '[''Inhale one capsule once daily through the Breezhaler device to keep your breathing tubes open all day and night'', ''Do NOT swallow the capsule; keep the powder away from your eyes'']', 'GLYCOPYRRO-nium: Guard against M3 spasms; Low CNS penetration (quaternary charge); Rapid 5-minute onset; Once daily.', '-nium', 'glye-koe-pye-ROE-nee-um', 'ग्लाइकोपिरो-नियम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('aclidinium', 'Aclidinium', 'Aclidinium Bromide Inhalation Powder (Tudorza Pressair)', 'Aclidinium bromide', 'Aclidinium bromide', 'Respiratory', 'A long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) rapidly hydrolyzed in human plasma by butyrylcholinesterase, providing potent local bronchodilation with near-zero systemic anticholinergic side effects.', '[''400 mcg per actuation'']', '[''Dry Powder Inhaler (Pressair / Genuair)'']', '[''Inhalation'']', 'Competitively and reversibly inhibits muscarinic M3 receptors in airway smooth muscle, blocking acetylcholine-mediated bronchoconstriction; possesses prolonged residence time at M3 receptors, but is rapidly broken down by esterases once it enters systemic circulation.', 'Produces sustained bronchodilation twice daily, improves daytime and nighttime COPD symptoms, reduces exacerbations; rapid enzymatic breakdown in human plasma prevents systemic anticholinergic adverse effects (dry mouth, urinary retention, cardiac arrhythmias).', 'Inhaled fraction reaches lung; systemic bioavailability of inhaled aclidinium is exceptionally low (<5%); swallowed drug is completely hydrolyzed in the GI tract.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 300 L; systemic plasma levels are barely detectable due to rapid plasma hydrolysis; >85% bound to plasma proteins.', 'RAPID ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSIS IN HUMAN PLASMA: Extensively and immediately cleaved by plasma esterases and butyrylcholinesterase into inactive alcohol and acid metabolites; minimal CYP450 involvement.', 'Excreted predominantly as inactive metabolites in urine (approx. 54-65%) and faeces (approx. 20-33%); <1% unchanged parent drug.', '<5% (negligible systemic bioavailability)', 'Plasma half-life of parent drug is extremely short: 2 to 3 minutes; biological M3 duration in lungs is 12 hours', '>85%', 'Rapid onset within 10 to 15 minutes', '12 hours (dosed 400 mcg twice daily)', '[''Long-term maintenance bronchodilator treatment to relieve symptoms of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)'', ''Combined with formoterol (Duaklir Pressair) for dual LAMA/LABA twice-daily therapy'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity to aclidinium bromide or milk proteins (contains lactose)'']', '[''Pressair inhaler features dual feedback (green to red window and audible click) confirming successful dose inhalation'', ''Paradoxical bronchospasm: discontinue immediately if acute wheezing occurs'', ''Use caution in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma or urinary retention (although systemic risk is exceptionally low due to plasma hydrolysis)'']', '[''Headache'', ''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Cough'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Dry mouth (rare: <1-2% due to rapid plasma breakdown)'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm (immediate)'', ''Acute angle-closure glaucoma (if powder enters eyes)'', ''Urinary retention (rare)'']', '[''Anticholinergic medications (tiotropium, ipratropium - avoid concurrent use)'', ''Zero clinically significant CYP450 drug interactions (hydrolyzed by plasma esterases)'']', '[''FEV1 and COPD symptom improvement'', ''Pressair dose indicator and correct patient technique'']', 'Category B; animal reproduction studies show no fetal harm; safe when indicated.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; negligible systemic absorption makes infant harm unlikely.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment (rapidly cleared by plasma esterases).', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment (metabolism occurs in blood, not liver).', '[''Rapidly destroyed in human blood within 2 to 3 minutes by plasma esterases, virtually eliminating systemic anticholinergic side effects (lowest dry mouth rate among all LAMAs)'', ''Twice-daily dosing provides superior control of nighttime and early morning COPD symptoms'', ''Pressair device provides audible click and green-to-red visual confirmation that the full dose was inhaled'']', '[''Requires twice-daily inhalation (morning and evening), unlike once-daily tiotropium or umeclidinium'', ''Contains lactose; avoid in severe milk protein allergy'']', '[''Inhale one puff twice daily (morning and evening) using the Pressair inhaler'', "Listen for the ''click'' and watch the window turn from green to red to know your dose was received"]', 'ACLIDI-nium: Airway Cleared; LAMA with Instant Destruction in blood (esterase breakdown); Zero systemic toxicity.', '-nium', 'ak-li-DI-nee-um', 'एक्लिडी-नियम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('umeclidinium', 'Umeclidinium', 'Umeclidinium Inhalation Powder (Incruse Ellipta)', 'Umeclidinium bromide', 'Umeclidinium bromide', 'Respiratory', 'A once-daily long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) delivered via the intuitive Ellipta dry powder inhaler, providing 24-hour bronchodilation for COPD maintenance.', '[''62.5 mcg per actuation'']', '[''Dry Powder Inhaler (Ellipta)'']', '[''Inhalation'']', 'Long-acting muscarinic receptor antagonist (anticholinergic); competitively blocks acetylcholine from binding to muscarinic M3 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle cells, preventing calcium-dependent airway constriction.', 'Produces sustained 24-hour bronchodilation, improves lung function (trough FEV1 improvement of 115-150 mL), reduces dyspnea and rescue inhaler use, and significantly lowers COPD exacerbations; available as monotherapy (Incruse) or combined with vilanterol (Anoro) or fluticasone/vilanterol (Trelegy).', 'Inhaled fraction absorbed from lung; oral bioavailability of swallowed drug is <1%; total absolute bioavailability of inhaled dose is approx. 13%.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 86 L; 89% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Metabolized in the liver primarily by CYP2D6 via hydroxylation and dealkylation, followed by glucuronidation.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 58% via biliary route) and urine (approx. 22% as metabolites); unchanged parent drug in urine is <1%.', 'Approximately 13%', '11 hours in plasma (supports true 24-hour pharmacodynamic duration)', '89%', 'Bronchodilation begins within 15 to 30 minutes', '24 hours (once daily in Ellipta device)', '[''Long-term, once-daily maintenance bronchodilator treatment of airflow limitation in patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)'', ''Dual combination with vilanterol (Anoro Ellipta)'', ''Triple combination with fluticasone furoate and vilanterol (Trelegy Ellipta)'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity to umeclidinium or milk proteins (contains lactose)'']', '[''Not for acute relief of sudden bronchospasm (keep rescue albuterol available)'', ''Paradoxical bronchospasm: discontinue immediately if wheezing worsens'', ''Worsening of narrow-angle glaucoma or urinary retention (use caution in prostatic hypertrophy)'', ''Single once-daily inhalation at the same time each day'']', '[''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Cough'', ''Dry mouth (mild)'', ''Arthralgia'', ''Headache'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm'', ''Acute angle-closure glaucoma'', ''Urinary retention / bladder outlet obstruction'', ''Atrial fibrillation / supraventricular tachycardia (rare)'']', '[''Other anticholinergics (tiotropium, ipratropium, glycopyrronium - avoid concurrent use)'', ''CYP2D6 inhibitors (minimal clinical impact due to low systemic levels)'']', '[''FEV1 and COPD symptom scores'', ''Urinary symptoms in elderly men'', ''Ellipta dose counter verification (counts down by 1 with each opening)'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients not established.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Once-daily 24-hour bronchodilation delivered in the single-step, highly rated Ellipta inhaler (open, inhale, close)'', ''Can be combined seamlessly into Anoro (dual) or Trelegy (triple single-inhaler therapy)'', ''Low systemic bioavailability (<13%) ensures minimal cardiac and urinary side effects'']', '[''Not indicated for asthma as monotherapy'', ''Dry mouth can occur in susceptible individuals'']', '[''Inhale one puff once daily at the same time every day using the Ellipta inhaler'', ''Do not open the cover until you are ready to inhale; the dose counter will drop by one'']', 'UMECLI-dinium: Ultra-long M3 block; Ellipta device; Clears airways for 24 hours; Low systemic absorption.', '-dinium', 'oo-me-kli-DIN-ee-um', 'उमेक्लि-डिनियम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('beclomethasone', 'Beclomethasone Dipropionate (BDP)', 'Beclomethasone Dipropionate Inhaler', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate', 'Respiratory', 'A synthetic halogenated glucocorticoid prodrug rapidly bioactivated in the lungs to active 17-BMP, delivered in extrafine solution aerosols for persistent asthma and allergic rhinitis.', '[''50 mcg'', ''100 mcg'', ''200 mcg per actuation (MDI)'', ''50 mcg/actuation nasal spray'']', '[''Pressurized Metered-Dose Inhaler (pMDI)'', ''Nasal Spray'']', '[''Inhalation'', ''Intranasal'']', 'Prodrug rapidly hydrolyzed in lung tissue by carboxylesterases to beclomethasone-17-monopropionate (17-BMP), which binds with 30-fold higher affinity to glucocorticoid receptors; translocates to the nucleus, inhibiting NF-kB and AP-1 transcription factors; down-regulates pro-inflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and adhesion molecules while up-regulating anti-inflammatory lipocortin-1 (annexin A1).', 'Suppresses airway eosinophilic inflammation, decreases bronchial hyperresponsiveness, reduces mucous gland secretion, and prevents asthma exacerbations; extrafine Modulite formulation generates sub-micron aerosol particles (1.1 micron) that reach small peripheral airways (<2 mm diameter).', 'Inhaled extrafine particles reach small airways with approx. 50-60% lung deposition; swallowed fraction is extensively metabolized during first-pass; systemic bioavailability of active 17-BMP is approx. 62%.', 'Volume of distribution of active 17-BMP approx. 424 L; 87% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Rapidly hydrolyzed in the lung and blood by esterases to active 17-BMP; 17-BMP is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 60%) via biliary elimination; approx. 12% in urine as metabolites.', 'Approximately 62% (active 17-BMP from lung)', '2.8 hours (17-BMP in plasma)', '87%', 'Anti-inflammatory benefit begins within 24 hours; maximal control in 1 to 2 weeks', '12 hours (dosed twice daily)', '[''Maintenance and prophylactic treatment of persistent asthma in adults and children'', ''Allergic rhinitis and vasomotor rhinitis (intranasal spray: 1-2 sprays per nostril BID)'', ''Combined with formoterol (Fostair) for maintenance and reliever therapy (MART) in asthma and COPD'']', '[''Primary treatment of status asthmaticus or other acute episodes of asthma where intensive measures are required'', ''Known hypersensitivity to beclomethasone dipropionate'']', '[''ALWAYS RINSE MOUTH AND SPIT after each inhalation to prevent oral candidiasis (thrush) and hoarseness (dysphonia)'', ''Adrenal suppression / growth deceleration in children: monitor height annually (lowest effective dose)'', ''Not a rescue inhaler (keep short-acting beta agonist available unless prescribed as part of MART protocol)'', ''Churg-Strauss / eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis during systemic steroid taper'']', '[''Oral candidiasis (oral thrush - white patches on tongue/pharynx in up to 5-10%)'', ''Dysphonia (hoarseness / voice weakness due to vocal cord myopathy)'', ''Throat irritation and cough'', ''Epistaxis (nasal spray)'']', '[''Systemic adrenal insufficiency / hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression (with prolonged high doses)'', ''Cataracts and glaucoma (with chronic high doses)'', ''Reduced bone mineral density / osteoporosis'', ''Growth velocity reduction in children (approx. 1 cm in year 1)'']', '["Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ritonavir, cobicistat, ketoconazole - increase systemic 17-BMP levels; risk of Cushing''s syndrome and adrenal suppression)", ''Systemic corticosteroids (additive HPA axis suppression)'']', '[''Peak Expiratory Flow (PEF) and spirometry'', ''Oral examination for thrush at every clinical visit'', ''Annual pediatric growth monitoring (standing height stadiometry)'', ''Ophthalmologic exams in long-term high-dose therapy'']', 'Category C; extensive human clinical registry data over decades shows excellent safety profile in pregnancy; inhaled corticosteroid of choice alongside budesonide.', 'Excreted in breast milk in negligible amounts; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved in pediatric asthma from 4 years of age (lowest effective dose; monitor linear growth).', 'No dosage adjustment required; monitor bone density and cataracts.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Extrafine formulation (Modulite) penetrates deep into the peripheral small airways (<2 mm) that classical inhalers miss'', ''Decades of reassuring pregnancy and pediatric safety data'', ''Active 17-BMP metabolite has 30-fold higher glucocorticoid receptor affinity than parent drug'']', '[''Requires twice-daily administration (morning and evening)'', ''Risk of oral thrush and hoarse voice if mouth is not rinsed after use'']', '[''Inhale twice daily (morning and night) every day to keep airway swelling down'', ''ALWAYS rinse your mouth with water and spit it out after inhaling to prevent white mouth sores'']', 'BECLO-methasone: Bronchial Eosinophils Calmed; Lung esterases convert to 17-BMP; Oral thrush prevented by rinsing.', '-sone', 'bek-loe-METH-a-sone', 'बेक्लो-मेथासोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('ciclesonide', 'Ciclesonide', 'Ciclesonide Inhalation Aerosol (Alvesco / Omnaris)', 'Ciclesonide', 'Ciclesonide', 'Respiratory', 'An on-site lung-activated steroid prodrug bioactivated in the bronchial epithelium with near-zero oral bioavailability (<1%), providing maximal asthma control with the lowest rates of oral thrush and hoarseness.', '[''80 mcg'', ''160 mcg per actuation (Alvesco pMDI)'', ''50 mcg/actuation nasal spray (Omnaris)'']', '[''Metered-Dose Inhaler (pMDI)'', ''Nasal Spray Suspension'']', '[''Inhalation'', ''Intranasal'']', 'Inhaled as an inactive prodrug that is selectively cleaved by intracellular esterases in human lung tissue to active desisobutyryl-ciclesonide (des-CIC); des-CIC binds with 100-fold higher affinity to glucocorticoid receptors, suppressing pro-inflammatory gene transcription (NF-kB inhibition); des-CIC reversibly conjugates with intracellular fatty acids, forming a lipophilic depot that slowly releases active drug.', 'Profoundly suppresses airway inflammation, reduces bronchial hyperresponsiveness, and prevents asthma exacerbations; because it is activated ONLY in the lungs, inactive drug deposited in the mouth and throat does NOT cause oropharyngeal candidiasis or dysphonia; swallowed drug undergoes >99% first-pass hepatic extraction (zero systemic steroid side effects).', 'Inhaled fraction penetrates small and large airways; swallowed fraction has <1% oral bioavailability due to complete hepatic first-pass extraction.', 'Apparent volume of distribution of active des-CIC approx. 897 L; highly bound to human plasma proteins (>99%).', 'Bioactivated on-site in airway tissue by esterases to active des-CIC; des-CIC is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 66% via biliary route) and urine (approx. 20%) as inactive metabolites.', '<1% (systemic bioavailability of swallowed drug)', 'des-CIC elimination half-life is 3.5 hours; intracellular lipid conjugation extends lung duration to 24 hours', '>99% (des-CIC)', 'Improvement in lung function begins within 24 to 48 hours; peak at 2 to 4 weeks', '24 hours (can be dosed once daily in mild-to-moderate asthma)', '[''Maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy in adult and adolescent patients 12 years and older (Alvesco: 80 to 320 mcg once or twice daily)'', ''Treatment of symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis (Omnaris nasal spray)'']', '[''Primary treatment of status asthmaticus or acute asthma exacerbations requiring intensive measures'', ''Hypersensitivity to ciclesonide'']', '[''ON-SITE LUNG ACTIVATION: Inactive prodrug is activated by lung esterases; inactive in the mouth, dramatically reducing oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) and hoarseness compared to other inhaled steroids'', ''Not for acute bronchospasm rescue'', ''Once-daily dosing option in the evening provides 24-hour asthma control'']', '[''Headache'', ''Nasopharyngitis'', ''Sinusitis'', ''Upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Oral candidiasis (exceptionally low: <1-2%)'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm (immediate)'', ''HPA axis suppression (only at supratherapeutic doses)'', ''Severe hypersensitivity / angioedema (rare)'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, ritonavir - increase des-CIC exposure; monitor for steroid effects)'', ''Zero significant interactions at recommended doses'']', '[''Asthma symptom control and FEV1/peak flows'', ''Adolescent growth monitoring'', ''Inhalation technique'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; <1% bioavailability makes infant exposure negligible; safe during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in adolescents 12 years and older (approved down to 6 years in Europe).', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '["True ''smart steroid'' prodrug activated ONLY inside the lungs: lowest incidence of oral thrush (<1%) and hoarseness among all inhaled steroids", ''Reversible intracellular fatty acid conjugation creates a 24-hour drug reservoir inside lung cells, enabling once-daily dosing'', ''Near-zero oral bioavailability (<1%) virtually eliminates systemic steroid absorption and adrenal suppression'']', '[''Higher cost than older generic beclomethasone or budesonide'', ''Not approved for acute asthma attacks'']', '[''High-tech lung-activated steroid that prevents asthma flare-ups without causing mouth infections'', ''Inhale once daily in the evening; rinse your mouth after use as a precaution'']', 'CICLE-sonide: Cleaved In Cells of Lung (prodrug); Exceptional safety; Lowest thrush & hoarseness; Evening once-daily.', '-sonide', 'si-KLE-soe-nide', 'सिक्ले-सोनाइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('mometasone', 'Mometasone Furoate', 'Mometasone Furoate Inhalation Powder / Nasal Spray', 'Mometasone furoate', 'Mometasone furoate', 'Respiratory', 'A potent synthetic 17-heterocyclic corticosteroid with negligible systemic bioavailability (<1%), widely used as a once-daily inhaled therapy for asthma and the premier topical spray for allergic rhinitis and nasal polyps.', '[''100 mcg'', ''200 mcg per actuation (Twisthaler / pMDI)'', ''50 mcg/actuation nasal spray (Nasonex)'', ''0.1% topical cream/ointment (Elocon)'']', '[''Dry Powder Inhaler (Twisthaler)'', ''Pressurized Metered-Dose Inhaler'', ''Nasal Spray'', ''Topical Cream / Ointment'']', '[''Inhalation'', ''Intranasal'', ''Topical'']', 'High-potency glucocorticoid; binds with ultra-high affinity to human glucocorticoid receptors (significantly higher affinity than fluticasone, budesonide, and dexamethasone); suppresses multiple inflammatory genes via NF-kB and AP-1 inhibition; inhibits leukotriene and cytokine release from respiratory mucosal cells.', 'Profoundly reduces bronchial inflammation, improves FEV1, and prevents asthma exacerbations; intranasally shrinks nasal polyps, restores sense of smell, and provides complete 24-hour relief of allergic rhinitis nasal congestion; negligible systemic bioavailability (<1%) prevents HPA axis suppression.', 'Oral inhalation and intranasal administration result in negligible systemic absorption (<1%); swallowed drug undergoes complete hepatic first-pass extraction.', 'Widely distributed into respiratory tissues; highly bound to human plasma proteins (98-99%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to multiple inactive metabolites; 6-beta-hydroxy-mometasone furoate is the primary metabolite.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 74% via biliary route) and urine (approx. 8%) as inactive metabolites.', '<1% (virtually zero systemic bioavailability)', '5 hours in plasma (prolonged biological receptor occupancy sustains 24-hour efficacy)', '98% to 99%', 'Nasal spray: within 12 hours; Inhaler: improvement in 24 hours, peak at 1 to 2 weeks', '24 hours (once daily in the evening)', '[''Maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy in patients aged 4 years and older (Asmanex Twisthaler: 110-440 mcg once daily in the evening)'', ''Treatment of nasal symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis in adults and children 2 years and older (Nasonex)'', ''Treatment of nasal polyps in adults aged 18 years and older (2 sprays per nostril BID)'', ''Combined with formoterol (Dulera) for moderate-to-severe asthma'', ''Topical dermatoses (Elocon 0.1% cream)'']', '[''Status asthmaticus or acute asthma attacks requiring intensive measures'', ''Hypersensitivity to mometasone furoate or milk proteins (Twisthaler contains lactose)'']', '[''Once-daily dosing in the evening provides 24-hour asthma control'', ''Rinse mouth and spit after inhalation to prevent oral candidiasis'', ''Nasal spray: inspect nasal septum periodically for ulceration or perforation'', ''Twisthaler device locks automatically when all doses are used'']', '[''Headache'', ''Pharyngitis / sore throat'', ''Upper respiratory tract infection'', ''Oral candidiasis (thrush)'', ''Epistaxis (nasal spray)'']', '[''Paradoxical bronchospasm'', ''Nasal septal perforation (intranasal use)'', ''Adrenal suppression / growth deceleration (rare at recommended doses)'', ''Glaucoma / cataracts (rare)'']', '["Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin - significantly increase mometasone plasma levels; risk of Cushing''s syndrome)"]', '[''FEV1 and peak flow rates'', ''Nasal mucosa inspection for ulceration in chronic nasal spray users'', ''Pediatric standing height annually'']', 'Category C; reassuring registry data; safe during pregnancy when clinically indicated.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; <1% bioavailability makes infant absorption negligible; safe during breastfeeding.', 'Approved down to 4 years of age for asthma (Asmanex) and down to 2 years for allergic rhinitis (Nasonex).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Highest glucocorticoid receptor affinity among inhaled corticosteroids'', ''Negligible systemic bioavailability (<1%) provides exceptional safety with near-zero adrenal suppression'', ''Approved as once-daily evening dosing for asthma, improving patient compliance'', ''Gold standard treatment for shrinking nasal polyps'']', '[''Contains lactose carrier in dry powder Twisthaler (avoid in severe milk protein allergy)'', ''Must rinse mouth after use to prevent thrush'']', '[''Inhale one puff once daily in the evening to keep asthma controlled all day and night'', ''Always rinse your mouth and spit after inhaling; for nasal spray, spray away from the middle septum'']', 'MOMETA-sone: Maximal receptor affinity; Once-daily evening dosing; Minimal absorption (<1%); Excellent for Polyps.', '-sone', 'moe-MET-a-sone', 'मोमेटा-सोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('roflumilast', 'Roflumilast', 'Roflumilast Tablets', 'Roflumilast', 'Roflumilast', 'Respiratory', 'An oral once-daily selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor that targets systemic and airway inflammation to reduce exacerbations in severe COPD associated with chronic bronchitis.', '[''250 mcg (starter pack)'', ''500 mcg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively and competitively inhibits phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4), the predominant cAMP-metabolizing enzyme in immune and inflammatory cells (neutrophils, macrophages, CD8+ T cells, bronchial smooth muscle); elevates intracellular cyclic AMP (cAMP), suppressing pro-inflammatory cytokine and chemokine release.', 'Significantly reduces sputum neutrophils, decreases bronchial wall thickness, improves post-bronchodilator FEV1 (by 50-80 mL), and reduces the rate of moderate and severe COPD exacerbations by approx. 17-20% in patients with severe COPD, chronic bronchitis, and frequent flare-ups.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentrations of active roflumilast N-oxide in 4 to 8 hours; absolute bioavailability approx. 80%; unaffected by food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 2.9 L/kg; highly bound to human plasma proteins (99% for parent; 97% for active N-oxide).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver: converted primarily by CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 to roflumilast N-oxide (which accounts for >90% of total in vivo PDE4 inhibitory activity).', 'Excreted predominantly in urine (approx. 70% as inactive metabolites) and faeces (approx. 20%); <1% unchanged parent drug in urine.', 'Approximately 80%', 'Parent drug: 17 hours; Active N-oxide: 30 hours (ideal for once-daily dosing)', '99%', 'Clinical reduction in COPD exacerbations develops over 4 to 12 weeks', '24 hours (once daily)', '[''Maintenance treatment to reduce the risk of COPD exacerbations in adult patients with severe COPD (FEV1 < 50% predicted) associated with chronic bronchitis and a history of frequent exacerbations (500 mcg once daily)'']', '[''Moderate to severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B or C - black box contraindication)'', ''Hypersensitivity to roflumilast'']', '[''PSYCHIATRIC EVENTS: Increased risk of psychiatric adverse reactions including insomnia, anxiety, new or worsening depression, and suicidal ideation/behavior; assess mental health history before prescribing'', ''WEIGHT LOSS: Causes progressive, unexplained weight loss in up to 7-10% (monitor body weight at every visit; discontinue if unexplained significant weight loss occurs)'', ''NOT A BRONCHODILATOR: Do NOT use for the relief of acute bronchospasm'', ''Start with 250 mcg once daily for 4 weeks to reduce initial GI adverse effects (diarrhea, nausea)'']', '[''Diarrhoea (very common in first 4 weeks: up to 10-15%)'', ''Weight loss (mean loss 2-3 kg)'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Insomnia'', ''Decreased appetite'']', '[''Suicidal ideation and completed suicide'', ''Severe depression / psychiatric disturbances'', ''Severe weight loss / cachexia'', ''Severe persistent diarrhea with dehydration'']', '[''Strong CYP inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital - drastically reduce roflumilast exposure; avoid combination)'', ''Combined CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 inhibitors (erythromycin, fluvoxamine, ketoconazole - increase roflumilast levels and side effects)'', ''Theophylline (concurrent use not recommended due to additive PDE inhibition)'']', '[''Body weight at baseline and every visit (weigh patient regularly)'', ''Mental health status, mood changes, depression, or emergence of suicidal thoughts'', ''Frequency of moderate to severe COPD exacerbations'']', 'Category C; teratogenic in animal models; avoid during pregnancy.', 'Excreted in animal milk; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'COPD is not a pediatric condition; safety and efficacy in children not established.', 'Higher susceptibility to weight loss and insomnia; monitor body weight closely.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment (including dialysis).', 'CONTRAINDICATED in moderate to severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B and C); clearance is heavily liver-dependent.', '[''Oral pill (non-inhaler) that targets the underlying neutrophil-driven chronic inflammation of severe bronchitis'', ''Proven in clinical trials to reduce moderate-to-severe COPD exacerbations by approx. 17-20% when added to standard inhalers'', ''Convenient once-daily dosing without needing inhalation technique'']', '[''High rate of diarrhea, nausea, and weight loss in the first 4 weeks (titrate with 250 mcg starter pack)'', ''Black box warning for depression and suicidal thoughts'', ''Strictly contraindicated in liver cirrhosis'']', '[''An oral tablet taken once daily to prevent severe chest flare-ups and bronchitis hospital visits'', ''Start with the 250 mcg starter dose for the first month to prevent diarrhea; report any mood changes or weight loss immediately'']', 'ROFLUMI-last: Reduces Outbursts of Flare-ups in Lung; Unclogs Mucus via PDE4; Causes weight loss; Liver caution; Insomnia/depression.', '-milast', 'roe-FLUE-mi-last', 'रोफ्लूमि-लास्ट', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('doxofylline', 'Doxofylline', 'Doxofylline Tablets / Syrup', 'Doxofylline', 'Doxofylline', 'Respiratory', 'A novel xanthine derivative with phosphodiesterase inhibitory and anti-inflammatory activity that lacks adenosine receptor affinity, providing bronchodilation with dramatically reduced cardiac and CNS toxicities compared to theophylline.', '[''400 mg'', ''100 mg/5 mL syrup'', ''100 mg/5 mL injection'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Oral Syrup'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Inhibits phosphodiesterase (PDE) enzymes, elevating intracellular cAMP in airway smooth muscle; CRITICALLY LACKS AFFINITY FOR ADENOSINE (A1, A2A, A2B) RECEPTORS and does NOT block calcium channels; suppresses inflammatory cell migration and reduces airway hyperreactivity.', 'Produces potent bronchodilation and anti-inflammatory action in asthma and COPD; because it does NOT block adenosine receptors, it does NOT cause the tachycardia, cardiac arrhythmias, insomnia, gastric acid hypersecretion, or seizures characteristic of traditional theophylline.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract (bioavailability approx. 62%); peak plasma concentration in 1 hour; unaffected by food.', 'Widely distributed into tissues; volume of distribution approx. 1 L/kg; 48% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver (approx. 90%) to inactive hydroxyethyltheophylline; minimal conversion to theophylline (<2%).', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 50% as metabolites) and faeces; <4% excreted unchanged in urine.', 'Approximately 62%', '7 to 8 hours', '48%', '30 to 60 minutes', '12 hours (dosed 400 mg twice or three times daily)', '[''Maintenance treatment of bronchial asthma'', ''Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) with reversible bronchospasm'', ''Asthmatic bronchitis and chronic spastic bronchitis'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to doxofylline or xanthines'', ''Acute myocardial infarction'', ''Severe hypotension'', ''Lactation'']', '[''Dramatically safer than theophylline, but use with caution in patients with severe cardiac disease, severe hypertension, or peptic ulcer disease'', ''Do not chew or crush tablets; take with water after meals'', ''No routine therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) required at standard therapeutic doses (unlike theophylline)'']', '[''Nausea and vomiting'', ''Headache'', ''Epigastric pain / heartburn'', ''Dizziness'', ''Insomnia (much less frequent than theophylline)'']', '[''Cardiac arrhythmias / tachycardia (rare at therapeutic doses)'', ''Convulsions / seizures (only at extreme supratherapeutic overdoses)'', ''Severe hypotension'']', '[''Erythromycin, clarithromycin, ciprofloxacin (inhibit hepatic metabolism, increasing doxofylline levels)'', ''Cimetidine (increases doxofylline levels)'', ''Phenytoin, rifampin (induce clearance, reducing doxofylline levels)'']', '[''FEV1 and peak expiratory flow rates'', ''Heart rate and blood pressure'', ''Symptoms of dyspepsia or nausea'']', 'Category B/C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Strictly contraindicated during lactation; excreted in breast milk.', 'Approved in children 6 years and older (syrup formulation: 6-12 mg/kg/day divided BID/TID).', 'Clearance may be decreased; start with 400 mg once or twice daily.', 'Use with caution and reduce dose in severe renal impairment.', 'Dose reduction required in severe hepatic cirrhosis.', '[''Free of adenosine receptor antagonism: 10-fold safer cardiac and CNS profile than theophylline (no sleep disturbance, no palpitations)'', ''Does not require tedious and expensive serum blood level monitoring (TDM) like theophylline'', ''Effective oral bronchodilator for patients who cannot master inhaler devices'']', '[''Requires twice-daily oral dosing (400 mg BID)'', ''Interacts with macrolide antibiotics and fluoroquinolones'']', '[''Safer modern cousin of theophylline that opens breathing tubes without causing heart racing or insomnia'', ''Take 400 mg twice daily with water after meals'']', 'DOXO-fylline: Devoid Of Xanthine toxicity; Opens airways; Zero Adenosine receptor block; Safer than theophylline.', '-fylline', 'dox-oh-FYE-leen', 'डोक्सो-फ़ाइलीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('ambroxol', 'Ambroxol', 'Ambroxol Hydrochloride', 'Ambroxol hydrochloride', 'Ambroxol hydrochloride', 'Respiratory', 'A secretolytic and secretomotor mucolytic agent that stimulates pulmonary surfactant production, clears viscous bronchial mucus, and possesses local anesthetic and anti-inflammatory properties.', '[''30 mg'', ''75 mg SR'', ''15 mg/5 mL, 30 mg/5 mL syrup'', ''15 mg/2 mL ampoule'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Sustained-Release Capsule'', ''Oral Syrup'', ''Solution for Injection / Inhalation'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Inhalation'']', 'Cleaves acidic mucopolysaccharide fibers in bronchial mucus, fragmenting high-molecular-weight polymers; directly stimulates Clara cells and alveolar type II pneumocytes to synthesize and secrete pulmonary surfactant; enhances ciliary beat frequency (mucociliary clearance); blocks neuronal voltage-gated sodium channels, providing throat-soothing local anesthesia.', 'Reduces mucus viscosity, enhances tracheobronchial clearance, facilitates productive coughing, soothes sore throats, and enhances the penetration of amoxicillin, cefuroxime, and erythromycin into bronchial secretions.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract; 70-80% oral bioavailability due to first-pass metabolism; peak plasma levels in 1 to 2.5 hours; take with food.', 'Widely distributed into lung and respiratory tissues; 90% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via glucuronidation and oxidation by CYP3A4 to dibromanthranilic acid.', 'Excreted predominantly in urine (approx. 85-90% as metabolites and unchanged drug); less than 10% in faeces.', '70% to 80%', '10 hours (supports twice-daily or once-daily SR dosing)', '90%', 'Secretolytic action begins within 30 minutes', '12 hours (30 mg TID or 75 mg SR once daily)', '[''Secretolytic therapy in acute and chronic bronchopulmonary diseases associated with abnormal mucus secretion and impaired mucus transport (COPD, bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis, asthma)'', ''Symptomatic relief of acute sore throat (lozenges: local anesthetic action)'', ''Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) in preterm neonates (stimulates fetal surfactant synthesis)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to ambroxol or bromhexine'', ''Active severe peptic ulcer disease (mucolytics may disrupt gastric mucosal barrier)'']', '[''Severe skin reactions: rare cases of erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) reported; discontinue immediately if progressive skin rash appears'', ''Maintain adequate hydration (drink plenty of fluids to assist mucus liquefaction)'', ''Safe for use in children from infancy'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Dyspepsia / heartburn'', ''Mild diarrhea'', ''Altered taste (dysgeusia)'', ''Oral numbness (with lozenges)'']', '[''Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (rare)'', ''Severe anaphylactic shock'', ''Angioedema'']', '[''Antibiotics (amoxicillin, cefuroxime, erythromycin, doxycycline - ambroxol significantly increases antibiotic penetration into bronchial secretions; therapeutic advantage)'', ''Antitussives (codeine, dextromethorphan - suppressing the cough reflex leads to mucus retention; avoid combination)'']', '[''Sputum volume and ease of expectoration'', ''Skin examination for emergence of rash'', ''Respiratory sounds (rhonchi and wheezing)'']', 'Category B; extensive clinical use shows no teratogenic risk; avoid in first trimester as a general precaution.', 'Excreted in breast milk in small amounts; safe during breastfeeding.', 'Approved across all pediatric age groups (syrup: 1.25-2.5 mg/kg/day).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'Dose reduction recommended in severe renal impairment (accumulation of metabolites).', 'Dose reduction recommended in severe hepatic cirrhosis.', '[''Triple action: thins mucus, speeds up ciliary escalator, AND stimulates lung surfactant production'', ''Synergistically boosts antibiotic concentrations inside lung tissue and phlegm'', ''Proven local anesthetic throat numbing relieves acute sore throat pain'']', '[''Do not combine with cough suppressants (codeine) which block coughing up thinned phlegm'', ''Rare risk of severe skin rashes (SJS/TEN)'']', '[''Thins thick phlegm and helps you cough it up easily; drink plenty of water while taking it'', ''Do NOT take cough-stopping syrups with this medicine (you need to cough the phlegm out)'']', 'AMBROX-ol: Alveolar Surfactant booster; Mucus Breakdown; Relieves Obstinate phlegm; eXcellent with antibiotics.', '-xol', 'am-BROX-ol', 'एम्ब्रोक्सोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('bromhexine', 'Bromhexine', 'Bromhexine Hydrochloride', 'Bromhexine hydrochloride', 'Bromhexine hydrochloride', 'Respiratory', 'A classic benzylamine secretolytic mucolytic derived from the Indian plant Adhatoda vasica (Vasaka), metabolized in vivo to active ambroxol to liquefy tenaciously thick phlegm.', '[''8 mg'', ''4 mg/5 mL syrup'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Oral Syrup'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Derived from the alkaloid vasicine; hydrolyzed in vivo to its primary active metabolite ambroxol; depolymerizes mucopolysaccharide protein fibers and stimulates lysosomal enzyme release from mucus-secreting goblet cells; increases serous bronchial gland secretion.', 'Decreases phlegm viscosity, enhances mucociliary transport velocity, transforms tenacious viscous mucus into fluid secretions, and promotes productive expectoration; increases bronchial antibiotic penetration.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract; undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism; absolute oral bioavailability approx. 20%; peak plasma levels in 1 hour; take after meals.', 'Widely distributed into bronchial tissues; highly bound to plasma proteins (approx. 95%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver to multiple metabolites, predominantly active ambroxol (approx. 20-30%) and dibromanthranilic acid.', 'Excreted predominantly in urine (approx. 85-90% as metabolites within 24 hours); less than 1% unchanged drug in urine.', 'Approximately 20% (due to high first-pass metabolism)', '12 hours (terminal elimination half-life)', '95%', 'Secretolytic effect begins within 30 minutes; full liquefaction in 2 to 3 days', '6 to 8 hours (dosed 8 mg TID)', '[''Secretolytic therapy in bronchopulmonary diseases associated with abnormal mucus secretion and impaired mucus transport (acute and chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis)'', ''Facilitation of expectoration in pneumonia and COPD'', "Sjögren''s syndrome (stimulates tear and salivary production)"]', '[''Hypersensitivity to bromhexine or ambroxol'', ''Active gastric or duodenal peptic ulcer disease'']', '[''Drink plenty of warm fluids throughout the day to support the thinning and clearance of mucus'', ''Do NOT co-prescribe with cough suppressants (codeine, dextromethorphan) as retained liquefied mucus can drown small airways'', ''Rare risk of severe allergic skin reactions (erythema multiforme, SJS)'']', '[''Nausea and vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Epigastric discomfort'', ''Headache'', ''Dizziness'', ''Sweating'']', '[''Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / severe cutaneous adverse reactions'', ''Bronchospasm in sensitive asthmatic patients'', ''Severe anaphylaxis'']', '[''Antitussive cough suppressants (codeine - leads to dangerous mucus pooling in lungs)'', ''Antibiotics (increases ampicillin, erythromycin, and tetracycline concentrations in sputum)'']', '[''Sputum viscosity, volume, and ease of expectoration'', ''Skin for any rash'']', 'Category B; widely used for decades without evidence of teratogenicity; avoid in first trimester.', 'Excreted in breast milk; avoid during breastfeeding.', 'Approved for children (syrup formulation: 2-6 years: 4 mg BID; 6-12 years: 4 mg TID).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'Dose reduction recommended in severe renal impairment.', 'Dose reduction recommended in severe hepatic disease.', '[''Natural plant heritage (derived from Ayurvedic Vasaka / Adhatoda vasica) with proven scientific mucolytic action'', ''Bioactivated into ambroxol in the liver, providing prolonged dual mucolytic activity'', ''Increases antibiotic concentrations inside bronchial phlegm'']', '[''Low bioavailability (20%) requires three-times-daily dosing (8 mg TID)'', ''Contraindicated with cough suppressants'']', '[''Helps loosen and dissolve tough chest phlegm; take 8 mg three times daily after meals with water'', ''Drink extra fluids and do not take cough-stopping medicines at the same time'']', 'BROM-hexine: Breakdown of Respiratory Obstruction & Mucus; Heritage from Vasaka; Expectorant; In vivo becomes ambroxol.', '-hexine', 'brom-HEX-een', 'ब्रोमहे-क्सीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('carbocisteine', 'Carbocisteine (Carbocysteine)', 'Carbocisteine Capsules / Syrup', 'Carbocisteine', 'Carbocisteine', 'Respiratory', 'A mucoregulatory agent that alters glycoprotein synthesis to restore the physiological balance of sialomucins and fucomucins, proven in the landmark PEACE study to reduce COPD exacerbations.', '[''375 mg'', ''750 mg'', ''250 mg/5 mL, 750 mg/5 mL syrup'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Oral Syrup'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Acts intracellularly on bronchial goblet cells as a mucoregulator: stimulates sialyltransferase to increase the synthesis of fluid sialomucins while suppressing the production of thick, viscous fucomucins; restores normal viscoelastic properties of bronchial secretions without breaking disulfide bonds.', 'Normalizes airway mucus rheology, restores the protective mucociliary escalator, reduces goblet cell hyperplasia, and reduces bacterial adherence to bronchial epithelium; landmark PEACE trial proved chronic carbocisteine reduced annual COPD exacerbations by 24.5%.', 'Rapidly and well absorbed from GI tract; peak plasma concentrations in 1.5 to 2 hours; bioavailability approx. 10%; take with or after meals.', 'Penetrates well into lung tissue and bronchial mucus; low plasma protein binding.', 'Metabolized in the liver via acetylation, decarboxylation, and sulfoxidation.', 'Excreted almost entirely in urine (approx. 80% within 24 hours as unchanged drug and metabolites).', 'Approximately 10% (systemic); acts locally on mucosal synthesis', '1.5 to 2 hours in plasma', 'Low', 'Mucoregulatory benefit builds over several days; chronic prevention in 4 to 12 weeks', '8 hours (initial 750 mg TID; maintenance 750 mg BID)', '[''Long-term mucoregulatory therapy in Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) to reduce exacerbation frequency (PEACE trial: 1500 mg/day)'', ''Adjunctive therapy of respiratory tract disorders characterized by excessive viscous mucus (chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis)'', ''Glue ear (otitis media with effusion in children)'']', '[''Active peptic ulcer disease (mucoregulators may alter gastric mucus barrier)'', ''Known hypersensitivity to carbocisteine'']', '[''PEACE trial proven: long-term therapy (1 year) significantly reduces COPD exacerbations and improves quality of life'', ''Use with caution in patients with a history of gastroduodenal ulcers'', ''Do NOT co-administer with antitussives'']', '[''Nausea and dyspepsia'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Gastrointestinal bleeding / epigastric pain'', ''Headache'', ''Skin rash'']', '[''Gastrointestinal ulceration / major GI bleeding'', ''Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / erythema multiforme'', ''Anaphylactic reactions'']', '[''Antitussive cough suppressants (codeine - prevents clearance of mobilized mucus)'', ''No significant CYP450 drug interactions'']', '[''COPD exacerbation frequency and hospitalization rates'', ''Stool for occult blood / signs of gastric ulceration'']', 'Avoid during the first trimester; limited human clinical data.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 2 years and older (syrup formulation).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone; monitor for gastric irritation.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate renal disease.', 'Use with caution in severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Proven disease-modifying role in COPD: landmark PEACE study demonstrated a 24.5% reduction in yearly exacerbations'', ''True mucoregulator: repairs the biochemical composition of mucus at the cellular level rather than just dissolving it'', ''Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties inhibit bacterial colonization'']', '[''High initial dosing requirement (750 mg three times daily)'', ''Potential for gastric irritation and ulceration'']', '[''Proven to cut COPD flare-ups and hospital visits by fixing sticky lung phlegm; take with meals'', ''Take regularly every day as long-term prevention, not just during sudden chest attacks'']', 'CARBO-cisteine: Corrects Abnormal Rheology of Bronchial Output; Outclasses exacerbations in PEACE trial.', '-cisteine', 'kar-boe-SIS-teen', 'कार्बोसि-स्टीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('bilastine', 'Bilastine', 'Bilastine', 'Bilastine', 'Bilastine', 'Antihistamines', 'A modern, highly selective, non-sedating second-generation H1-antihistamine with zero hepatic CYP metabolism and zero brain H1-receptor occupancy, used for allergic rhinitis and urticaria.', '[''20 mg'', ''10 mg orally disintegrating tablet (ODT)'', ''2.5 mg/mL oral solution'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Orally Disintegrating Tablet'', ''Oral Solution'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Potent, highly selective, and non-competitive peripheral histamine H1-receptor antagonist; completely lacks affinity for muscarinic, adrenergic, serotonergic, or other histamine receptor subtypes; substrate for P-glycoprotein efflux at the blood-brain barrier, resulting in near-zero brain H1-receptor occupancy.', 'Rapidly suppresses histamine-mediated vascular permeability, wheal and flare reactions, nasal pruritus, rhinorrhea, and ocular itching; TRUE NON-SEDATING PROFILE: does NOT impair psychomotor performance, driving ability, or cognitive function, even at twice the therapeutic dose; zero QTc prolongation.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1.3 hours; 61% oral bioavailability; FOOD AND FRUIT JUICES REDUCE ABSORPTION BY 30-33% (MUST BE TAKEN 1 HOUR BEFORE OR 2 HOURS AFTER FOOD OR JUICE).', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 1.29 L/kg; does NOT cross the blood-brain barrier; 84-90% bound to plasma proteins.', 'DOES NOT UNDERGO HEPATIC METABOLISM: >95% of circulating drug is unchanged bilastine; does not interact with any cytochrome P450 enzymes.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in faeces (approx. 67%) and urine (approx. 33%); zero liver toxicity.', 'Approximately 61% (MUST take on an empty stomach)', '14.5 hours (ideal for true once-daily dosing)', '84% to 90%', 'Fast onset: within 30 to 60 minutes', 'Over 24 hours (20 mg once daily on empty stomach)', '[''Symptomatic treatment of allergic rhinoconjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial: sneezing, nasal discharge, itching, congestion)'', ''Chronic spontaneous urticaria (hives: relieves pruritus, wheal size and number; dose can be titrated up to 4x in refractory hives)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to bilastine'']', '[''STRICT FOOD RULE: MUST be taken on an empty stomach (take 1 hour before breakfast or 2 hours after a meal, with water only); food or fruit juice (grapefruit/apple) decreases bioavailability by 30-33%'', ''Truly non-sedating: approved for pilots and drivers (zero central sedation at therapeutic and supra-therapeutic doses)'', ''Zero hepatic metabolism: ideal for patients with liver disease or polypharmacy'']', '[''Headache'', ''Somnolence (rate identical to placebo: 1.5-3%)'', ''Dizziness'', ''Fatigue'', ''Dyspepsia'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity / anaphylaxis (rare)'', ''Cardiac QT prolongation (NONE: clean cardiac profile)'']', '[''Fruit juices (grapefruit, apple - inhibit OATP1A2 uptake transporter, reducing bilastine levels by 30-33%; take with water only)'', ''P-glycoprotein inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin, cyclosporine - increase bilastine plasma levels; avoid in renal failure)'', ''Zero CYP450 interactions (does not interact with warfarin, statins, or liver-cleared drugs)'']', '[''Allergic rhinitis symptom scores (TNSS) and urticaria activity score (UAS7)'', ''Patient compliance with empty-stomach administration'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human clinical safety data.', 'Excretion in breast milk unknown; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 6 to 11 years (10 mg ODT or oral solution once daily on an empty stomach).', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients; exceptional safety profile.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment (avoid concurrent P-gp inhibitors if CrCl < 50 mL/min).', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment (zero liver metabolism).', '[''Zero brain penetration: absolute non-sedating antihistamine that does NOT impair driving, machinery operation, or flying'', ''Zero hepatic metabolism: safely co-prescribed with complex medications without drug-drug interactions'', ''Can be safely up-dosed to 40-80 mg daily (up to 4x) for refractory chronic hives'']', '[''MUST be taken on an empty stomach with plain water (food or juice cuts absorption by one-third)'', ''Not for acute emergency anaphylaxis'']', '[''Take one 20 mg tablet once daily on an empty stomach (1 hour before breakfast) with water only'', ''Will NOT make you sleepy or groggy; safe for driving and working'']', 'BILA-stine: Brain Is Left Alone (zero sedation); Intestinal absorption requires empty stomach; Liver cleared Zero percent.', '-stine', 'bi-LAS-teen', 'बिला-स्टीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('rupatadine', 'Rupatadine', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 'Rupatadine fumarate', 'Rupatadine fumarate', 'Antihistamines', 'A dual-action second-generation antihistamine and platelet-activating factor (PAF) receptor antagonist, providing superior relief of severe nasal congestion and allergic inflammation.', '[''10 mg'', ''1 mg/mL oral solution'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Oral Solution'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Dual molecular mechanism: selectively blocks peripheral histamine H1 receptors AND antagonizes platelet-activating factor (PAF) receptors; PAF is a potent non-histamine mediator of mucosal edema, severe nasal congestion, bronchial hyperreactivity, and vascular hyperpermeability.', 'Provides rapid, potent suppression of allergic rhinitis symptoms; uniquely relieves severe nasal congestion and obstruction superior to pure antihistamines; inhibits mast cell and eosinophil degranulation and cytokine release.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 0.75 to 1 hour; bioavailability unaffected by food (high-fat meals increase AUC by 23%); can be taken with or without food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 9,000 L; highly bound to human plasma proteins (98-99%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to active metabolites (desloratadine and hydroxylated derivatives), which contribute to sustained efficacy.', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 60%) and urine (approx. 35% as metabolites); <1% unchanged parent drug in urine.', 'Good oral absorption', '5.9 hours (parent drug; active metabolites sustain 24-hour action)', '98% to 99%', 'Rapid onset within 45 to 60 minutes', '24 hours (10 mg once daily)', '[''Symptomatic treatment of allergic rhinitis (seasonal and perennial) in adults and adolescents 12 years and older'', ''Chronic spontaneous urticaria (relief of itching and hives)'', ''Allergic rhinitis with prominent nasal obstruction / congestion'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to rupatadine'', ''Severe hepatic or renal impairment'']', '[''Avoid grapefruit juice (contains furanocoumarins that inhibit CYP3A4, increasing rupatadine exposure by 3.5-fold)'', ''Avoid strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin)'', ''Mild somnolence in up to 8-9% (take at bedtime if drowsiness occurs)'']', '[''Somnolence / drowsiness (approx. 8-9%)'', ''Headache'', ''Fatigue / asthenia'', ''Dry mouth'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity reactions'', ''QTc prolongation (minimal at recommended doses; use caution with other QT drugs)'']', '[''Grapefruit juice (STRICTLY AVOID: increases rupatadine levels by 350%)'', ''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin - increase drug levels)'', ''Alcohol / CNS depressants (additive mild sedation)'']', '[''Allergic rhinitis symptoms (especially nasal obstruction / congestion scores)'', ''Assess for daytime drowsiness'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human clinical safety data.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 2 to 11 years (oral solution formulation: weight-adjusted dosing).', 'Use with caution in elderly patients; well tolerated.', 'Use with caution in severe renal impairment (limited clinical data).', 'Contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment (metabolized by CYP3A4).', '[''Dual H1-antihistamine + PAF-receptor antagonist provides superior unclogging of severe nasal congestion compared to classical antihistamines'', ''Active metabolite includes desloratadine, extending 24-hour therapeutic coverage'', ''Effective once-daily dosing with or without food'']', '[''Avoid drinking grapefruit juice (causes 3.5-fold drug buildup)'', ''Slightly higher rate of mild drowsiness (8-9%) than bilastine or fexofenadine'']', '[''Dual-action allergy tablet that clears both sneezing and blocked, congested noses; take 10 mg once daily'', ''Do NOT drink grapefruit juice while taking this medication'']', 'RUPATA-dine: Relieves Urticaria; Potent Anti-PAF + Antihistamine; Targets severe congestion; Avoid grapefruit juice.', '-tadine', 'roo-pa-TA-deen', 'रूपाटा-डीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('ebastine', 'Ebastine', 'Ebastine Film-Coated / Fast-Dissolving Tablets', 'Ebastine', 'Ebastine', 'Antihistamines', 'A long-acting second-generation H1-receptor antagonist prodrug rapidly converted by CYP3A4 to active carebastine, providing 24-hour non-sedating relief of allergic rhinitis and chronic urticaria.', '[''10 mg'', ''20 mg'', ''10 mg, 20 mg orally disintegrating tablets (Fast-Tab)'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Orally Disintegrating Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Prodrug rapidly and almost completely converted in the liver by CYP3A4 to active carebastine; carebastine is a potent, long-acting, selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist with negligible penetration into the central nervous system.', 'Suppresses histamine-induced wheal and flare, relieves sneezing, rhinorrhea, nasal pruritus, and ocular itching, and clears chronic urticaria wheals; fast-dissolving tablet melts on the tongue in seconds without water.', 'Rapidly absorbed; extensively metabolized during first pass; active carebastine peaks in 2.6 to 4 hours; high-fat meals increase carebastine AUC by 50% without altering clinical efficacy.', 'Carebastine is widely distributed; does not cross the blood-brain barrier; highly bound to plasma proteins (>97%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to active carboxylic acid metabolite carebastine.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 66% as conjugated carebastine metabolites) and faeces; parent ebastine is undetectable in urine.', 'Rapidly converted to active carebastine', '15 to 19 hours (carebastine elimination half-life)', '>97% (carebastine)', '1 to 3 hours', '24 hours (10-20 mg once daily)', '[''Symptomatic treatment of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis'', ''Allergic conjunctivitis'', ''Chronic idiopathic urticaria (hives: 10 to 20 mg once daily; superior relief at 20 mg)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to ebastine or carebastine'', ''Severe hepatic impairment (for the 20 mg dose)'']', '[''Orally disintegrating tablet (Fast-Tab) dissolves on the tongue in seconds without water'', ''Caution with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin - increases carebastine levels)'', ''Safe cardiac profile at therapeutic doses (no significant QTc prolongation)'']', '[''Headache'', ''Somnolence (rate similar to placebo: 3-5%)'', ''Dry mouth'', ''Drowsiness (at 20 mg dose in sensitive patients)'', ''Nausea'']', '[''Severe allergic anaphylaxis (rare)'', ''Cardiac arrhythmias (only with massive overdose combined with QT drugs)'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin - increase carebastine plasma concentrations)'', ''Alcohol and CNS depressants (additive mild sedation)'']', '[''Allergic rhinitis and urticaria symptom scores'', ''Liver function tests in chronic therapy with hepatic disease'']', 'Category B; animal studies show no teratogenicity; limited human data; use only if clearly needed.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Approved in children 12 years and older (approved down to 2 years with syrup in certain countries).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'Maximum dose 10 mg daily in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C).', '[''Fast-dissolving tablet (Fast-Tab) melts on the tongue in seconds without water, ideal for travel and acute allergy attacks'', ''Active carebastine provides rock-solid 24-hour allergy relief in a single daily dose'', ''Higher 20 mg dose provides superior control for severe, refractory chronic hives'']', '[''Metabolized by CYP3A4, requiring caution with azole antifungals and macrolides'', ''Mild drowsiness can occur at the higher 20 mg dose in sensitive patients'']', '[''Take once daily with or without food; fast-dissolving tablet melts on the tongue without water'', ''Provides complete 24-hour relief from sneezing, runny nose, and itchy hives'']', 'EBA-stine: Extended 24h action; Bioactivated to carebastine; Antihistamine with Fast-Tab (melts on tongue).', '-stine', 'e-BAS-teen', 'एबा-स्टीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('mizolastine', 'Mizolastine', 'Mizolastine Modified-Release Tablets', 'Mizolastine', 'Mizolastine', 'Antihistamines', 'A dual-action second-generation H1-receptor antagonist and 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) inhibitor, combining antihistaminic and anti-leukotriene actions for allergic rhinitis and urticaria.', '[''10 mg'']', '[''Modified-Release Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively and competitively blocks peripheral histamine H1 receptors; concurrently inhibits 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX), suppressing the biosynthesis of inflammatory leukotrienes (LTB4, LTC4); also inhibits mast cell degranulation and ICAM-1 expression.', 'Provides dual anti-allergic and anti-inflammatory action; suppresses early-phase allergic symptoms (itching, sneezing) and late-phase allergic inflammation (nasal congestion, tissue eosinophilia); non-sedating at recommended doses.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentrations in 1.5 hours; bioavailability approx. 65%; food does not impair absorption.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 1.4 L/kg; does not cross the blood-brain barrier; highly bound to plasma proteins (98.4%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver primarily by glucuronidation and by CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP3A4.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 65% via biliary elimination) and urine (approx. 10% as metabolites).', 'Approximately 65%', '8 to 13 hours', '98.4%', 'Within 1 hour', '24 hours (10 mg once daily)', '[''Symptomatic treatment of seasonal allergic rhinoconjunctivitis (hay fever)'', ''Perennial allergic rhinitis'', ''Chronic idiopathic urticaria (hives)'']', '[''Significantly impaired hepatic function'', ''Severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min)'', ''Preexisting QT interval prolongation or cardiac arrhythmias'', ''Clinically significant bradycardia or hypokalemia'', ''Concurrent use of macrolides, systemic azoles, or antiarrhythmics (Class I and III)'']', '[''CARDIAC QT PROLONGATION RISK: Mizolastine can cause minor prolongation of the QTc interval; STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED with QT-prolonging drugs, macrolides, or azole antifungals'', ''Do not exceed the recommended dose of 10 mg once daily'', ''Avoid in patients with cardiac disease or electrolyte imbalances'']', '[''Somnolence / drowsiness (approx. 7%)'', ''Fatigue / asthenia'', ''Headache'', ''Dry mouth'', ''Nausea'', ''Weight gain / increased appetite'']', '[''QTc prolongation / ventricular arrhythmias (with overdose or CYP inhibitors)'', ''Severe hypersensitivity / angioedema'', ''Elevated transaminases (rare)'']', '[''CYP3A4/CYP1A2 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin, cimetidine - increase mizolastine levels and QT risk; CONTRAINDICATED)'', ''QT-prolonging drugs (amiodarone, sotalol, quinidine - CONTRAINDICATED)'', ''Alcohol (additive sedation)'']', '[''Baseline 12-lead ECG in patients with cardiovascular risk factors'', ''Serum potassium and magnesium'', ''Allergic rhinitis and urticaria symptom scores'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human clinical safety data.', 'Excreted in animal milk; contraindicated during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years not established.', 'Use with caution in elderly patients; assess cardiac history and ECG.', 'Contraindicated in severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min).', 'Contraindicated in significant hepatic impairment.', '[''Dual-action: blocks histamine H1 receptors AND suppresses 5-lipoxygenase (leukotrienes), attacking allergy from two pathways'', ''Modified-release tablet provides continuous 24-hour relief with a single morning tablet'', ''Relieves both watery drainage and inflammatory nasal blockage'']', '[''Risk of cardiac QT prolongation requires strict screening and avoidance of macrolides/azoles'', ''Contraindicated in severe liver or kidney disease'']', '[''Take one 10 mg tablet once daily in the morning with water'', ''Do NOT take this medication if you have heart rhythm issues or are taking erythromycin/ketoconazole'']', 'MIZOLA-stine: Multi-action (H1 block + 5-Lipoxygenase inhibition); Once daily; Zero macrolides (QT risk).', '-stine', 'mye-ZOE-las-teen', 'मिज़ोला-स्टीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('olopatadine', 'Olopatadine', 'Olopatadine Ophthalmic Solution / Nasal Spray', 'Olopatadine hydrochloride', 'Olopatadine hydrochloride', 'Antihistamines', 'A dual-action mast cell stabilizer and selective H1-antihistamine, serving as the gold standard topical agent for allergic conjunctivitis and allergic rhinitis.', '[''0.1% eye drops (Patanol - BID)'', ''0.2% eye drops (Pataday - once daily)'', ''0.7% eye drops (Pazeo - once daily)'', ''0.6% nasal spray (Patanase)'']', '[''Ophthalmic Solution'', ''Nasal Spray Solution'']', '[''Ophthalmic'', ''Intranasal'']', 'Dual pharmacological action: selectively and competitively antagonizes histamine H1 receptors, AND stabilizes mast cell membranes, preventing antigen-induced degranulation and release of histamine, leukotrienes, and pro-inflammatory cytokines; inhibits human conjunctival epithelial cell cytokine release.', 'Rapidly halts ocular itching within 3 minutes of eye drop instillation and provides 24-hour comfort (0.2% and 0.7% formulations); intranasally relieves sneezing, rhinorrhea, and itchy nose within 30 minutes; topical application produces minimal systemic exposure.', 'Minimal systemic absorption following ocular instillation (plasma levels generally below quantification limit); nasal spray produces low systemic bioavailability (approx. 57%).', 'Acts locally in the conjunctiva and nasal mucosa; 55% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Minimally metabolized in the liver; primary metabolites are N-desmethyl and N-oxide olopatadine.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in urine (approx. 60-70%) via glomerular filtration.', 'Minimal systemic (ocular); 57% (nasal)', '8 to 12 hours in plasma; ocular receptor residence provides 16 to 24 hours of symptom relief', '55%', 'Immediate (ocular itching stops within 3 minutes); Nasal: 30 minutes', '16 to 24 hours (0.2% and 0.7% eye drops once daily)', '[''Treatment of ocular itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis (Patanol 0.1% BID; Pataday 0.2% once daily; Pazeo 0.7% once daily)'', ''Relief of symptoms of seasonal allergic rhinitis in adults and children 6 years and older (Patanase 0.6% nasal spray)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to olopatadine hydrochloride or benzalkonium chloride (preservative)'']', '[''CONTACT LENS PRECAUTION: Contains benzalkonium chloride, which can be absorbed by soft contact lenses; remove contact lenses before instilling eye drops and wait at least 10-15 minutes before reinserting'', ''Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface or the eye (prevents bacterial contamination)'', ''Nasal spray: spray away from the nasal septum to prevent epistaxis and bitter taste'']', '[''Ocular burning / stinging upon instillation (transient)'', ''Bitter taste in mouth (dysgeusia - especially with nasal spray in up to 12%)'', ''Dry eye sensation'', ''Epistaxis (nasal spray)'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity / contact blepharitis'', ''Nasal septal ulceration (nasal spray)'']', '[''No clinically significant drug interactions due to low systemic absorption'']', '[''Relief of ocular redness, itching, and chemosis'', ''Nasal mucosa inspection in chronic nasal spray users'']', 'Category C; negligible systemic exposure following eye drops; safe when clinically needed in pregnancy.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; negligible systemic absorption makes infant risk unlikely.', 'Approved in children 2 years and older for allergic conjunctivitis (Pataday).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone; well tolerated.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''Dual-action: immediately blocks histamine AND permanently stabilizes mast cells to prevent future allergic attacks'', ''Fastest ocular relief: stops intense eye itching within 3 minutes of putting in the drop'', ''Once-daily formulation (Pataday 0.2% / Pazeo 0.7%) provides 24 hours of clear, itch-free eyes'']', '[''Contains benzalkonium chloride (must remove contact lenses for 15 minutes)'', ''Nasal spray can cause a bitter taste in the back of the throat'']', '[''Put 1 drop in each itchy eye once daily in the morning; stops eye itching within 3 minutes'', ''Take out contact lenses before putting the drops in; wait 15 minutes before putting lenses back in'']', 'OLOPATA-dine: Ocular allergy relief; Lasts 24 hours (once daily); Opposes histamine + Stabilizes mast cells.', '-tadine', 'oh-loe-PAT-a-deen', 'ओलोपेटा-डीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('azelastine', 'Azelastine', 'Azelastine Ophthalmic Solution / Nasal Spray', 'Azelastine hydrochloride', 'Azelastine hydrochloride', 'Antihistamines', 'A fast-acting, second-generation topical H1-antihistamine and mast cell stabilizer that relieves nasal symptoms within 15 minutes, co-formulated with fluticasone propionate (Dymista) for severe allergic rhinitis.', '[''0.1% nasal spray (137 mcg/spray)'', ''0.15% nasal spray (205.5 mcg/spray)'', ''0.05% eye drops'']', '[''Nasal Spray Solution'', ''Ophthalmic Solution'']', '[''Intranasal'', ''Ophthalmic'']', 'Multiple anti-allergic actions: potent, competitive antagonist at peripheral histamine H1 receptors; stabilizes mast cell membranes, inhibiting antigen-triggered histamine release; down-regulates ICAM-1 expression and inhibits pro-inflammatory leukotriene and endothelin-1 generation.', 'Produces ultra-rapid relief of nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, sneezing, and nasal itching within 15 minutes of nasal spray administration (faster than oral antihistamines and nasal steroids); combined with fluticasone propionate (Dymista) to provide synergistic, gold-standard relief of moderate-to-severe allergic rhinitis.', 'Intranasal administration delivers drug directly to the nasal mucosa; systemic bioavailability from nasal spray is approx. 40%; ophthalmic absorption is minimal.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 14.5 L/kg; 88% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, CYP2D6, and CYP2C19 to active metabolite desmethylazelastine (equally potent at H1 receptors).', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 75% via biliary elimination) and urine (approx. 25%); <10% unchanged drug in urine.', 'Approximately 40% (nasal)', '22 hours (parent drug; active desmethyl metabolite half-life is 54 hours)', '88%', 'Ultra-rapid: nasal relief begins within 15 minutes', '12 to 24 hours (1-2 sprays per nostril BID or once daily)', '[''Symptomatic relief of seasonal allergic rhinitis in adults and children 2 years and older'', ''Perennial allergic rhinitis and vasomotor / non-allergic rhinitis'', ''Combined with fluticasone propionate (Dymista) for moderate-to-severe allergic rhinitis refractory to monotherapy'', ''Ocular itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis (0.05% eye drops)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to azelastine hydrochloride'']', '[''BITTER TASTE (DYSGEUSIA): Up to 20% experience a bitter metallic taste; PREVENT BY PROPER TECHNIQUE: keep head tilted slightly DOWNWARD during spraying, do NOT tilt head backward, and do NOT inhale deeply through the nose'', ''Mild somnolence in up to 2-5% due to systemic nasal absorption (caution when driving)'', ''Avoid alcohol and CNS depressants (additive sedation)'']', '[''Bitter taste in mouth (dysgeusia - very common if sprayed incorrectly: up to 20%)'', ''Nasal burning / stinging'', ''Somnolence / drowsiness (mild: 2-5%)'', ''Epistaxis'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity / angioedema'', ''Nasal mucosal ulceration (with improper technique)'']', '[''Alcohol and CNS depressants (additive somnolence and impaired psychomotor function)'', ''Cimetidine (increases azelastine plasma levels by 65%)'']', '[''Allergic rhinitis symptom scores (TNSS)'', ''Nasal mucosal integrity'', ''Patient technique to eliminate bitter taste'']', 'Category C; safe when indicated; negligible fetal risk from topical application.', 'Excreted in human milk in low amounts; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 2 years and older for seasonal allergic rhinitis (0.1% nasal spray).', 'No dosage adjustment required in elderly patients; well tolerated.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease.', '[''Fastest-acting allergy nasal spray: relieves nasal congestion and sneezing in just 15 minutes'', ''Effective for both allergic rhinitis AND non-allergic / vasomotor rhinitis'', ''Co-formulated with fluticasone (Dymista) as the number-one recommended prescription spray for severe allergic rhinitis'']', '[''Bitter taste in the throat if sprayed with head tilted back (technique must be taught)'', ''Can cause mild drowsiness in sensitive individuals'']', '[''Spray into each nostril with your chin tilted slightly down toward your chest; do not sniff hard to prevent a bitter taste'', ''Relieves stuffy and runny nose within 15 minutes'']', 'AZELA-stine: Action in Zero time (15 min); Excellent for Non-allergic rhinitis; Look down to avoid bitter taste.', '-stine', 'a-ZEL-a-steen', 'एज़ेला-स्टीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('cloxacillin', 'Cloxacillin', 'Cloxacillin Sodium', 'Cloxacillin sodium', 'Cloxacillin sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A semi-synthetic penicillinase-resistant isoxazolyl penicillin active against penicillinase-producing staphylococci, used for MSSA skin, soft tissue, and respiratory tract infections.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'', ''250 mg vial'', ''500 mg vial'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Powder for Injection'', ''Oral Solution'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting the transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis; bulky isoxazolyl side chain prevents degradation by staphylococcal beta-lactamase.', 'Time-dependent bactericidal action against methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) and Streptococcus species; inactive against MRSA and Enterococcus.', 'Incompletely absorbed (approx. 50%); food significantly impairs absorption (take 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals).', 'Diffuses into synovial, pleural, and bone fluids; poor CSF penetration in the absence of inflamed meninges; 94% bound to albumin.', 'Partially metabolized in the liver (approx. 20-30%) to active and inactive metabolites.', 'Eliminated in urine by tubular secretion and glomerular filtration (approx. 40-60% unchanged); significant biliary excretion.', 'Approximately 50% (fasting)', '0.5 to 1 hour', '94%', 'Oral peak in 1 to 2 hours', '6 hours', '[''Infections caused by penicillinase-producing staphylococci (MSSA)'', ''Skin and soft tissue infections (boils, cellulitis, impetigo)'', ''Staphylococcal osteomyelitis and septic arthritis'', ''Infective endocarditis due to MSSA'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cloxacillin, penicillins, or cephalosporins'', ''History of cholestatic jaundice or hepatic dysfunction with isoxazolyl penicillins'']', '[''Must be taken on an empty stomach to avoid severe absorption loss'', ''Cholestatic jaundice and hepatitis may occur (especially with prolonged therapy)'', ''Severe anaphylaxis in penicillin-allergic patients'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Epigastric discomfort'', ''Rash'', ''Heartburn'']', '[''Severe anaphylactic shock'', ''Drug-induced cholestatic jaundice / hepatitis'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis (C. difficile)'', ''Interstitial nephritis'']', '[''Probenecid (blocks renal tubular secretion, increasing cloxacillin levels)'', ''Warfarin (may alter INR)'', ''Methotrexate (decreases clearance)'']', '[''Hepatic transaminases in courses >10 days'', ''Renal function and complete blood count'', ''Clinical resolution of skin lesions'']', 'Category B; widely considered safe in pregnancy when clinically indicated.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children; dose calculated by weight (50-100 mg/kg/day divided every 6 hours).', 'Age-related renal decline requires monitoring; no primary adjustment if hepatic function normal.', 'Dose reduction needed only in severe renal failure (CrCl < 10 mL/min) due to secondary biliary clearance.', 'Contraindicated in patients with a history of cloxacillin-induced cholestatic jaundice.', '[''Resistant to staphylococcal penicillinase enzymes (unlike penicillin G or ampicillin)'', ''Effective oral and parenteral option for confirmed MSSA infections'', ''Dual renal and biliary excretion limits toxicity in renal disease'']', '[''Completely inactive against MRSA'', ''Must be taken on an empty stomach due to severe food-dependent absorption reduction'']', '[''First-line for penicillinase-producing staphylococcal skin infections (MSSA)'', ''Always take 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals on an empty stomach'']', 'CLOX-acillin: CLOCKS MSSA staph; Check empty stomach.', '-cillin', 'klox-a-SIL-in', 'क्लोक्सा-सिलिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('oxacillin', 'Oxacillin', 'Oxacillin Sodium', 'Oxacillin sodium', 'Oxacillin sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A parenteral penicillinase-resistant isoxazolyl penicillin indicated for severe systemic methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) infections including bacteremia, endocarditis, and osteomyelitis.', '[''500 mg vial'', ''1 g vial'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection'']', '[''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), causing cell wall weakening and osmotic lysis; stable against staphylococcal beta-lactamases.', 'Time-dependent bactericidal killing against MSSA and Streptococcus pneumoniae/pyogenes; inactive against MRSA, Enterococci, and Gram-negative bacilli.', 'Administered parenterally (IM or IV infusion); 100% bioavailable.', 'Distributes into pericardial fluid, bone, and pleural fluid; does not cross non-inflamed meninges; 90-94% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Metabolized partially in the liver to active and inactive metabolites; undergoes substantial biliary excretion.', 'Excreted in urine via glomerular filtration and tubular secretion (approx. 40-50%) and in bile (approx. 20-30%).', '100% (IV)', '0.5 to 0.7 hours', '92%', 'Immediate peak upon IV infusion', '4 to 6 hours', '[''Severe systemic infections caused by penicillinase-producing staphylococci'', ''MSSA bacteremia and sepsis'', ''MSSA infective endocarditis (standard first-line therapy)'', ''Staphylococcal osteomyelitis and septic arthritis'', ''Complicated skin and skin structure infections'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to oxacillin, penicillins, or other beta-lactams'', ''History of oxacillin-induced hepatitis'']', '[''Hepatotoxicity: elevated serum transaminases and hepatitis can occur (more frequent than with cefazolin)'', ''Hypersensitivity / anaphylactic reactions'', ''Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhoea'']', '[''Phlebitis at infusion site'', ''Elevated AST and ALT'', ''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash'']', '[''Anaphylactic shock'', ''Drug-induced hepatitis / cholestatic jaundice'', ''Acute interstitial nephritis'', ''Neutropenia (with prolonged high-dose therapy >2 weeks)'']', '[''Probenecid (prolongs serum half-life)'', ''Aminoglycosides (synergistic against MSSA, but must not be mixed in same IV bag)'', ''Methotrexate (increased methotrexate toxicity)'']', '[''Serum AST, ALT, and alkaline phosphatase weekly during high-dose therapy'', ''Complete blood count with differential weekly for neutropenia'', ''Renal function tests'']', 'Category B; safe during pregnancy for severe MSSA infections.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Weight-based dosing in pediatric patients (100-200 mg/kg/day IV divided every 6 hours).', 'Monitor renal and hepatic parameters; adjust based on clinical response.', 'No dose adjustment required in mild to moderate renal impairment; in severe renal failure with hepatic impairment, reduce dose.', 'Dose reduction and close liver enzyme monitoring required in patients with severe preexisting liver disease.', '[''First-line gold standard bactericidal agent for MSSA endocarditis and bacteremia'', ''Does not require renal dose adjustments in mild-to-moderate impairment due to biliary elimination'', ''Rapid bacterial clearance in deep MSSA abscesses'']', '[''High rate of chemical phlebitis requiring dedicated central line or frequent IV site rotation'', ''Requires frequent dosing (every 4 hours IV)'']', '[''Gold standard IV drug for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) bacteremia'', ''Rotate IV sites or use a central line due to risk of vein irritation (phlebitis)'']', 'OXA-cillin: Out-maneuvers Staph beta-lactamase; eXcellent for MSSA bacteremia.', '-cillin', 'ox-a-SIL-in', 'ऑक्सा-सिलिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('benzathine-penicillin', 'Benzathine Penicillin G', 'Benzathine Penicillin G (Penicillin G Benzathine)', 'Benzathine penicillin G', 'Benzathine penicillin G', 'Antibiotics', 'An ultra-long-acting repository intramuscular penicillin that releases penicillin G over 3 to 4 weeks, serving as the global first-line curative therapy for syphilis and secondary prophylaxis of rheumatic fever.', '[''600,000 units vial'', ''1,200,000 units (1.2 MU) vial'', ''2,400,000 units (2.4 MU) vial'']', '[''Sterile Suspension for Deep IM Injection'']', '[''Intramuscular'']', 'Slowly releases penicillin G from the deep intramuscular depot; penicillin G binds to bacterial PBPs, inhibiting cross-linking of peptidoglycan chains, resulting in bacterial lysis.', 'Low but sustained bactericidal serum concentrations maintained for 21 to 28 days; exquisitely lethal against Treponema pallidum and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus).', 'Administered exclusively by deep intramuscular injection; extremely slow absorption from depot site over 1 to 4 weeks.', 'Distributes into extracellular fluid, placenta, and tissues; does NOT achieve therapeutic levels in the central nervous system (not indicated for neurosyphilis); 60% protein bound.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism.', 'Excreted slowly by the kidneys via tubular secretion as active penicillin G over 3 to 4 weeks.', 'Depot release ensures 100% systemic bio-availability over 3-4 weeks', 'Depot elimination half-life is 14 to 30 days', '60%', 'Serum levels detectable within 1 to 2 hours, sustained for 21-28 days', '3 to 4 weeks', '[''Syphilis (primary, secondary, early latent: single 2.4 MU dose; late latent: 2.4 MU weekly for 3 doses)'', ''Prophylaxis of rheumatic fever (1.2 MU every 3 to 4 weeks)'', ''Streptococcal pharyngitis (single injection treatment)'', ''Yaws and pinta eradication'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to penicillin or procaine'', ''NEVER ADMINISTER INTRAVENOUSLY (causes fatal cardiorespiratory arrest and emboli)'']', '[''Black box warning: FOR DEEP INTRAMUSCULAR USE ONLY; inadvertent IV administration causes fatal cardiac arrest and permanent neurovascular damage (Hoigné syndrome / Nicolau syndrome)'', ''Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, chills, headache, hypotension) within 24 hours of treating syphilis'', ''Skin allergy testing recommended in suspected allergy'']', '[''Severe localized pain at intramuscular injection site'', ''Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (in syphilis patients)'', ''Fever'', ''Fatigue'', ''Urticaria'']', '[''Fatal pulmonary embolism / cardiac arrest if accidentally injected IV'', ''Severe anaphylactic shock'', ''Nicolau syndrome (livedoid dermatitis and tissue necrosis from arterial injection)'']', '[''Probenecid (prolongs serum penicillin levels)'', ''Bacteriostatic antibiotics (tetracyclines - may antagonize bactericidal action)'', ''Methotrexate'']', '[''Prior history of penicillin allergy before every dose'', ''Monitor for 30 minutes post-injection for acute anaphylaxis'', ''VDRL / RPR titers at 3, 6, 12, and 24 months post-syphilis therapy'']', 'Category B; drug of choice and ONLY proven curative treatment for syphilis in pregnant women (prevents congenital syphilis).', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Single dose of 600,000 units (for children <27 kg) for rheumatic fever prophylaxis and streptococcal tonsillitis.', 'Assess injection site muscle mass; use appropriate needle gauge (18-20G) to prevent depot clogging.', 'Dosing interval may be extended in severe renal failure, but depot clearance is predominantly slow dissolution.', 'No dosage adjustment required.', '[''Single-dose curative therapy for primary, secondary, and early latent syphilis'', ''Monthly deep IM injection guarantees 100% compliance for rheumatic fever prophylaxis'', ''Only therapy proven to prevent congenital syphilis in pregnancy'']', '[''STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED VIA IV: IV injection is immediately fatal'', ''Deep intramuscular injection is painful and causes local muscle tenderness'']', '[''CRITICAL SAFETY RULE: For deep INTRAMUSCULAR injection ONLY; NEVER inject IV'', ''Single 2.4 million unit dose cures early syphilis and prevents congenital syphilis in pregnancy'']', 'BENZATHINE: Binds to Extracellular Non-vascular muscle; Zero IV allowed (FATAL); Syphilis savior.', '-cillin', 'BEN-za-theen pen-i-SIL-in', 'बेंज़ाथीन पेनिसिलिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('benzylpenicillin', 'Benzylpenicillin (Penicillin G Sodium/Potassium)', 'Penicillin G Sodium / Potassium', 'Benzylpenicillin sodium', 'Benzylpenicillin sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A natural parenteral penicillin with high intrinsic potency against sensitive streptococci, Neisseria meningitidis, Treponema pallidum (neurosyphilis), and clostridial infections.', '[''1,000,000 units (1 MU) vial'', ''5,000,000 units (5 MU) vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan polymers in bacterial cell walls, triggering autolysins and osmotic cell death.', 'Potent concentration-dependent and time-dependent bactericidal activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Neisseria meningitidis, Treponema pallidum, and Clostridium perfringens.', 'Inactivated by gastric acid (oral bioavailability negligible); administered exclusively IV or IM.', 'Distributes into synovial, pericardial, and pleural fluids; penetrates inflamed meninges in bacterial meningitis (CSF levels approx. 5-10% of serum); 60% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Metabolized in the liver (approx. 20-30%) to penicilloic acid; minimal CYP involvement.', 'Excreted rapidly by the kidneys through active tubular secretion (approx. 90%) and glomerular filtration (approx. 10%).', '100% (IV)', '30 to 50 minutes (prolonged in neonates and severe renal failure to 4-10 hours)', '60%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '4 to 6 hours', '[''Neurosyphilis and ocular syphilis (18 to 24 million units IV daily for 10-14 days)'', ''Meningococcal meningitis due to Neisseria meningitidis'', ''Severe streptococcal pneumonia and bacteremia'', ''Infective endocarditis caused by viridans group streptococci (combined with gentamicin)'', ''Gas gangrene (Clostridium perfringens) and tetanus (Clostridium tetani)'', ''Actinomycosis'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to penicillin G or any beta-la