('cefotetan', 'Cefotetan', 'Cefotetan Disodium', 'Cefotetan disodium', 'Cefotetan disodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A second-generation cephamycin antibiotic with potent anaerobic activity against Bacteroides fragilis, used for surgical prophylaxis and mixed pelvic and intra-abdominal infections.', '[''1 g vial'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis; 7-alpha-methoxy group on the beta-lactam ring confers extreme stability against beta-lactamases produced by anaerobic bacteria.', 'Broad bactericidal action against aerobic Gram-negative bacilli, Streptococcus, and importantly anaerobes (Bacteroides fragilis, Prevotella, and Peptostreptococcus).', 'Administered parenterally (IV or IM); 100% bioavailable.', 'Distributes into peritoneal fluid, pelvic tissues, gallbladder, and skin; poor CSF penetration; 88% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Not significantly metabolized in the liver (approx. 10% converted to a tautomer with similar antibacterial activity).', 'Excreted primarily unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration and tubular secretion (approx. 50-80%); remaining eliminated in bile.', '100% (IV)', '3 to 4.6 hours (longer than cefoxitin, allowing twice-daily dosing)', '88%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '12 hours (dosed every 12 hours)', '[''Surgical antibiotic prophylaxis (colorectal surgery, abdominal/vaginal hysterectomy, cesarean section)'', ''Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID - combined with doxycycline)'', ''Intra-abdominal infections (peritonitis, diverticulitis, appendicitis)'', ''Gynecological infections (endometritis, pelvic cellulitis)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cefotetan, cephalosporins, or cephamycins'', ''History of cephalosporin-associated hemolytic anaemia'']', '[''Contains an N-methylthiotetrazole (NMTT) side chain: risk of hypoprothrombinemia, coagulopathy, and bleeding (vitamin K deficiency)'', ''Disulfiram-like ethanol reaction with alcohol ingestion (nausea, vomiting, flushing, tachycardia)'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Injection site thrombophlebitis'', ''Hypoprothrombinemia'', ''Eosinophilia'']', '[''Severe bleeding / coagulopathy due to NMTT-mediated vitamin K inhibition'', ''Disulfiram-like ethanol reaction'', ''Severe hemolytic anaemia'', ''Anaphylaxis'']', '[''Alcohol / ethanol (severe disulfiram-like reaction; avoid alcohol during and for 72 hours post-therapy)'', ''Oral anticoagulants / warfarin (prolonged prothrombin time and major bleeding risk)'', ''Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity)'']', '[''Prothrombin time (PT/INR) in patients on prolonged courses or with nutritional deficits (supplement with vitamin K)'', ''Renal function tests'', ''Stool frequency for C. difficile'']', 'Category B; used safely for surgical prophylaxis in cesarean section and pelvic procedures.', 'Excreted in human milk in low concentrations; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy established in children for intra-abdominal infections and prophylaxis (20-40 mg/kg q12h).', 'Dose reduction required if renal clearance is impaired; monitor PT/INR.', 'Dose adjustment based on CrCl: if CrCl 30-50 mL/min, give usual dose q24h or half dose q12h; if CrCl 10-30 mL/min, give half dose q24h; if CrCl < 10 mL/min, give quarter dose q24h.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic disease.', '[''Potent anaerobic coverage against Bacteroides fragilis without needing metronidazole'', ''Longer half-life than cefoxitin allows convenient 12-hourly dosing'', ''Single preoperative dose provides reliable coverage for colorectal and pelvic surgeries'']', '[''NMTT side chain causes hypoprothrombinemia, bleeding, and severe disulfiram reaction with alcohol'', ''High rate of C. difficile colitis'']', '[''Covers anaerobes (Bacteroides fragilis) for pelvic and abdominal infections'', ''Warn patients to avoid all alcohol (severe disulfiram reactio