('cefoxitin', 'Cefoxitin', 'Cefoxitin Sodium', 'Cefoxitin sodium', 'Cefoxitin sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A classical parenteral cephamycin with high beta-lactamase resistance and anaerobic efficacy against Bacteroides fragilis, used for surgical prophylaxis and pelvic inflammatory disease.', '[''1 g vial'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), causing bacterial cell wall synthesis inhibition; 7-alpha-methoxy group provides extreme resistance to inactivation by Gram-negative and anaerobic beta-lactamases.', 'Bactericidal against aerobic Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella), Gram-positive cocci, and anaerobes (Bacteroides fragilis, Clostridium species); does NOT contain the NMTT side chain (no hypoprothrombinemia).', 'Administered exclusively parenterally (IV or deep IM); 100% bioavailable.', 'Widely distributed into pleural, peritoneal, synovial, and ascitic fluids; minimal CSF penetration; 65-80% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Metabolized in small amounts (approx. 2-5%) to inactive descarbamylcefoxitin.', 'Excreted rapidly and primarily unchanged in urine via active tubular secretion (approx. 85%) and glomerular filtration.', '100% (IV)', '45 to 60 minutes', '70%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '6 to 8 hours (requires dosing every 6-8 hours)', '[''Surgical prophylaxis in colorectal, appendectomy, gynecological, and clean-contaminated procedures'', ''Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID - combined with oral doxycycline as CDC first-line parenteral regimen)'', ''Intra-abdominal infections (peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses)'', ''Diabetic foot infections involving mixed aerobes and anaerobes'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cefoxitin or cephalosporins/cephamycins'']', '[''Short half-life necessitates intraoperative redosing for surgical procedures lasting >2-3 hours'', ''Induces chromosomal AmpC beta-lactamases in Enterobacter species (avoid in Enterobacter infections)'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Local thrombophlebitis'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Maculopapular rash'', ''Eosinophilia'']', '[''Severe anaphylactic reactions'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis (C. difficile)'', ''Acute interstitial nephritis'', ''Leukopenia / thrombocytopenia (rare)'']', '[''Probenecid (significantly blocks tubular secretion, increasing serum cefoxitin levels)'', ''Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity risk)'']', '[''Renal function tests (BUN and creatinine)'', ''Complete blood count with differential in courses >10 days'', ''Inspection of peripheral IV sites for phlebitis'']', 'Category B; first-line CDC-recommended parenteral regimen for pelvic inflammatory disease in pregnancy.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved in children 3 months and older (80-160 mg/kg/day divided every 4-6 hours).', 'Renal function declines with age; adjust dose and interval accordingly.', 'Dose reduction required: if CrCl 30-50 mL/min, give 1-2 g q8-12h; if CrCl 10-29 mL/min, give 1-2 g q12-24h; if CrCl < 10 mL/min, give 0.5-1 g q24-48h.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''First-line CDC-recommended parenteral regimen for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)'', ''Does NOT contain the NMTT side chain (no bleeding or disulfiram reaction, unlike cefotetan)'', ''Excellent anaerobic coverage for abdominal and gynecological surgery'']', '[''Short half-life requires dosing every 6 to 8 hours (or intraoperative redosing during long surgeries)'', ''Strong inducer of AmpC beta-lactamases in Enterobacter, Serratia, and Citrobacter'']', '[''CDC first-line inpatient treatment for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID + doxycycline)'', ''Safe anaerobic coverage without bleeding complications because it lacks the NMTT ring'']', 'CEFOX-itin: Classic Emergency Cephamycin for Female pelvic inflammatory disease (PID); No NMTT.', '-oxitin', 'se-FOX-i-tin', 'सेफ़ोक्सि-टिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, N