('lornoxicam', 'Lornoxicam', 'Lornoxicam', 'Lornoxicam', 'Lornoxicam', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'An oxicam-class NSAID with potent analgesic and anti-inflammatory properties, characterised by a relatively short elimination half-life.', '[''4 mg'', ''8 mg'', ''8 mg injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Rapid-release Tablet'', ''Powder for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Potent balanced inhibitor of cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2, suppressing prostaglandin biosynthesis and IL-6 release.', 'High analgesic potency comparable to moderate opioids in post-operative and acute pain settings, alongside anti-inflammatory action.', 'Rapidly absorbed after oral administration; peak plasma levels reached in 1 to 2 hours.', 'Extensively bound to plasma proteins, predominantly albumin; good penetration into synovial fluid.', 'Extensively metabolised in the liver, primarily via cytochrome P450 2C9 (CYP2C9) to inactive 5-hydroxy-lornoxicam.', 'Approximately two-thirds excreted via bile and faeces, and one-third via the kidneys.', 'Approximately 90-100%', '3 to 5 hours', 'Approximately 99%', '30 to 45 minutes', '8 to 12 hours', '[''Acute mild-to-moderate pain'', ''Post-operative surgical pain'', ''Osteoarthritis flare-ups'', ''Rheumatoid arthritis'', ''Lumbago and sciatica'']', '[''Active peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding'', ''Severe thrombocytopenia or bleeding diathesis'', ''Severe heart failure'', ''Severe renal or hepatic impairment'']', '[''Gastrointestinal bleeding and perforation'', ''Cardiovascular thrombotic events'', ''Renal impairment particularly in hypovolemic patients'']', '[''Mild dizziness'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Abdominal pain'']', '[''Severe GI bleeding'', ''Acute tubular necrosis'', ''Stevens-Johnson syndrome (rare)'', ''Thrombocytopenia'']', '[''Warfarin and other oral anticoagulants (bleeding risk)'', ''Methotrexate (increased toxicity)'', ''Sulfonylureas (enhanced hypoglycaemic effect)'', ''Diuretics'']', '[''Renal function'', ''Platelet count'', ''Liver function in extended therapy'']', 'Contraindicated in the third trimester; not recommended during first and second trimesters unless essential.', 'Excretion in human milk not fully established; avoid during breastfeeding.', 'Not recommended in children and adolescents under 18 years of age.', 'Use with caution; start at lower dose range due to reduced renal and hepatic clearance.', 'Avoid in severe renal impairment (GFR < 30 mL/min); monitor fluid status.', 'Dose adjustment necessary in moderate hepatic impairment; contraindicated in severe liver disease.', '[''Short half-life reduces chronic accumulation risk'', ''Potent analgesia suitable for post-operative recovery'', ''Parenteral formulation available'']', '[''Typical NSAID-associated GI and renal liabilities'', ''CYP2C9 poor metabolisers may exhibit elevated levels'']', '[''Short half-life distinguishes it from other oxicams like piroxicam'', ''Take after meals to protect gastric mucosa'']', 'Lorn-Oxi-cam: Fast-acting Oxicam with Lower persistence (short half-life).', '-oxicam', 'lor-NOX-i-kam', 'लॉर्नॉक्सी-कैम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('dexketoprofen', 'Dexketoprofen', 'Dexketoprofen', 'Dexketoprofen trometamol', 'Dexketoprofen trometamol', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'The pharmacologically active (S)-(+)-enantiomer of ketoprofen formulated with trometamol for rapid absorption and analgesia at half the racemic dose.', '[''25 mg'', ''50 mg/2 mL injection'', ''1.25% gel'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'', ''Gel'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'', ''Topical'']', 'Selective activation of analgesia by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis through COX-1 and COX-2 blockade, with higher chiral potency than the racemic mixture.', 'Rapid-onset analgesia for acute nociceptive pain; anti-inflammatory and antipyretic efficacy with lower total chemical load.', 'Extremely fast dissolution and absorption due to the trometamol salt; Tmax reached within 15-30 minutes.', 'High binding to plasma albumin; volume of distribution is approximately 0.25 L/kg.', 'Hepatic glucuronidation; undergoes no chiral inversion to the R-(-) enantiomer.', 'Almost entirely eliminated by renal excretion as glucuronide conjugates within 24 hours.', 'High (over 90%)', '1.65 hours', '99%', '15 to 30 minutes', '6 hours', '[''Acute dental pain'', ''Musculoskeletal sprains and strains'', ''Post-operative pain'', ''Renal colic'', ''Dysmenorrhoea'']', '[''History of asthma triggered by NSAIDs'', ''Active peptic ulcer disease'', ''Severe hepatic or renal failure'', ''Third trimester of pregnancy'']', '[''Gastrointestinal ulceration'', ''Dehydration increases risk of nephrotoxicity'', ''Cardiovascular precautions'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Stomach pain'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Peptic ulcer haemorrhage'', ''Bronchospasm'', ''Anaphylaxis'', ''Renal impairment'']', '[''Anticoagulants'', ''Lithium'', ''Methotrexate'', ''Other NSAIDs'', ''ACE inhibitors'']', '[''Pain response'', ''Signs of GI irritation'', ''Hydration status'']', 'Contraindicated in third trimester; use only when strictly indicated in first two trimesters.', 'Excretion in breast milk unknown; use not recommended during lactation.', 'Not recommended in pediatric patients under 18 years.', 'Initiate at lower doses (e.g., 12.5 mg) and titrate carefully.', 'Reduce dosage in mild renal impairment; avoid in moderate-to-severe renal failure.', 'Reduce total daily dose in mild-to-moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Rapid onset of analgesia within 15-30 minutes'', ''Lower dose needed than racemic ketoprofen'', ''Trometamol salt provides high aqueous solubility'']', '[''Short duration requiring multiple daily doses for sustained pain'', ''Standard NSAID GI cautions'']', '[''Trometamol formulation speeds up gastric emptying and absorption'', ''Ideal for acute dental and musculoskeletal pain spikes'']', 'Dex = Dextro (active half), fast acting like an express train.', '-profen', 'dex-kee-toe-PROH-fen', 'डेक्स-कीटो-प्रो-फेन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('nabumetone', 'Nabumetone', 'Nabumetone', 'Nabumetone', 'Nabumetone', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A non-acidic prodrug NSAID that is metabolized in the liver to 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6-MNA), with reduced direct gastric mucosal toxicity.', '[''500 mg'', ''750 mg'', ''1000 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Inactive prodrug converted in the liver to 6-MNA, which preferentially inhibits COX-2 over COX-1, reducing inflammatory prostaglandins.', 'Long-acting anti-inflammatory and analgesic efficacy; lower direct topical acid-induced gastric injury because the parent compound lacks a free carboxylic acid.', 'Well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; food increases rate of absorption and conversion to active metabolite.', 'Active metabolite (6-MNA) is >99% bound to plasma proteins and penetrates into synovial fluid.', 'Extensive first-pass hepatic transformation into active metabolite 6-MNA, followed by O-demethylation and glucuronide conjugation.', 'Approximately 80% recovered in urine (chiefly as conjugates of 6-MNA) and 10% in faeces.', 'Approximately 80% as active metabolite 6-MNA', '22 to 30 hours for the active metabolite 6-MNA', '>99%', '1 to 2 hours; steady-state therapeutic effect over several days', '24 hours', '[''Osteoarthritis'', ''Rheumatoid arthritis'', ''Chronic inflammatory joint disease'']', '[''Aspirin triad (asthma, rhinitis, nasal polyps)'', ''Active GI bleeding'', ''Severe heart failure'', ''Severe renal disease'']', '[''Systemic COX inhibition can still precipitate GI ulcers and renal dysfunction'', ''Thrombotic cardiovascular events'', ''Fluid retention'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Abdominal cramps'', ''Constipation'', ''Tinnitus'']', '[''GI ulceration and bleeding'', ''Nephrotic syndrome'', ''Severe skin reactions'', ''Cardiovascular thrombosis'']', '[''Warfarin'', ''Aspirin and other NSAIDs'', ''ACE inhibitors'', ''Diuretics'', ''Lithium'']', '[''Complete blood count'', ''Renal function tests'', ''Stool occult blood in long-term therapy'']', 'Avoid in the third trimester; fetal vascular complications reported with NSAIDs.', '6-MNA is excreted in breast milk; avoid use while nursing.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in pediatric patients.', 'Steady-state plasma concentrations of 6-MNA may be higher; monitor closely.', 'Caution in moderate renal impairment; avoid in severe renal insufficiency (GFR < 30 mL/min).', 'Conversion to active metabolite occurs in liver; use caution in severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Once-daily dosing due to long half-life'', ''Prodrug nature avoids direct acidic contact irritation to gastric mucosa'', ''Preferential COX-2 activity'']', '[''Delayed peak onset compared to fast-acting analgesics'', ''Systemic GI ulceration risk remains with chronic use'']', '[''Non-acidic prodrug converted to 6-MNA'', ''Convenient once-daily dosing after dinner'']', 'Nabumetone: Non-Acidic Body, Metabolised to active 6-MNA.', '', 'na-BYOO-me-tone', 'नाब्यू-मी-टोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('sulindac', 'Sulindac', 'Sulindac', 'Sulindac', 'Sulindac', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'An indene derivative prodrug NSAID whose sulfide metabolite reversibly inhibits COX enzymes; historically noted for relative renal-sparing characteristics.', '[''100 mg'', ''200 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Prodrug reduced in vivo to active sulindac sulfide, which inhibits COX-1 and COX-2; active metabolite undergoes enterohepatic circulation and renal oxidation back to inactive sulfoxide.', 'Exerts anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic actions; active sulfide metabolite has less effect on renal prostaglandin synthesis compared to standard NSAIDs.', 'Approximately 90% of oral dose is absorbed; peak plasma levels of parent drug in 1-2 hours and active sulfide in 3-4 hours.', 'Extensively bound to plasma albumin (>93%); active sulfide metabolite reaches high concentrations in synovial fluid.', 'Reversible reduction to active sulfide metabolite and irreversible oxidation to inactive sulfone metabolite in the liver.', 'About 50% excreted in urine (mainly as sulfone and glucuronide) and 25% in faeces.', 'Approximately 90%', '7 to 8 hours for sulindac; 16 to 18 hours for active sulfide metabolite', '93% to 98%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 to 24 hours', '[''Osteoarthritis'', ''Rheumatoid arthritis'', ''Ankylosing spondylitis'', ''Acute gouty arthritis'', ''Acute painful shoulder (bursitis/tendinitis)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to sulindac or aspirin'', ''Active gastrointestinal bleeding'', ''Severe hepatic dysfunction'', ''Third trimester of pregnancy'']', '[''Pancreatitis has been reported more frequently with sulindac than some other NSAIDs'', ''Hepatotoxicity can occur'', ''Renal and cardiovascular alerts'']', '[''Gastrointestinal pain'', ''Nausea'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash'']', '[''Acute pancreatitis'', ''Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis'', ''Cholestatic jaundice'', ''Aplastic anaemia'']', '[''Anticoagulants (warfarin)'', ''Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO - may reduce active sulfide levels)'', ''Methotrexate'', ''Lithium'', ''Diuretics'']', '[''Liver function tests (ALT/AST)'', ''Renal function'', ''Amylase/lipase if severe abdominal pain develops'']', 'Contraindicated in third trimester; risk of premature closure of ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use not recommended.', 'Not established in pediatric populations.', 'Higher risk of adverse renal and hepatic events; monitor closely.', 'Relatively less inhibitory on renal prostacyclin, but caution still required in renal impairment.', 'Dose reduction necessary in hepatic disease; liver injury reported.', '[''Enterohepatic recycling provides twice-daily dosing convenience'', ''Relative renal sparing compared to indomethacin'']', '[''Pancreatitis and hepatic reactions observed'', ''Typical NSAID GI adverse effects'']', '[''Prodrug reduced to active sulfide metabolite'', ''Watch for unexplained abdominal pain due to pancreatitis risk'']', 'SUL-indac: Sparing of Urinary/renal prostacyclins, Liver produces active Sulfide.', '', 'sul-IN-dak', 'सुलिन-डैक', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('roxithromycin', 'Roxithromycin', 'Roxithromycin', 'Roxithromycin', 'Roxithromycin', 'Antibiotics', 'A semi-synthetic 14-membered ring macrolide antibiotic with high oral bioavailability and long half-life, used for respiratory, skin, and genital tract infections.', '[''50 mg DT'', ''150 mg'', ''300 mg'']', '[''Dispersible Tablet'', ''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Suspension'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds reversibly to the 50S subunit of bacterial 70S ribosomes, inhibiting translocation and peptide chain elongation, blocking bacterial protein synthesis.', 'Bacteriostatic spectrum covering Gram-positive cocci (Streptococcus, Staphylococcus), atypical pathogens (Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella), and select Gram-negatives.', 'Rapidly absorbed; significantly more acid-stable and bioavailable than erythromycin; take 15 minutes before food.', 'High tissue and intracellular penetration, especially into alveolar macrophages, tonsils, and bronchial mucosa; 96% protein bound (to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein).', 'Partially metabolised in the liver; predominantly eliminated unchanged.', 'Excreted mainly in faeces via biliary elimination (approx. 53%) and in urine (approx. 10%).', 'Approximately 50%', '10 to 12 hours (allowing once or twice daily dosing)', '96%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 to 24 hours', '[''Community-acquired pneumonia'', ''Acute pharyngitis and tonsillitis'', ''Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis'', ''Skin and soft tissue infections'', ''Non-gonococcal urethritis'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to macrolides'', ''Severe hepatic impairment with concurrent ergot alkaloid therapy (ergotism risk)'', ''Concurrent use of terfenadine, astemizole, or cisapride'']', '[''Prolongation of QT interval and risk of ventricular arrhythmias'', ''Hepatotoxicity and cholestatic jaundice'', ''Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhoea'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Epigastric pain'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'']', '[''QT prolongation / Torsades de pointes'', ''Cholestatic hepatitis'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'', ''Severe allergic reactions'']', '[''Ergotamine (severe peripheral vasospasm/ergotism)'', ''Theophylline (modest increase in theophylline levels)'', ''Warfarin (potentiates anticoagulant effect)'', ''Digoxin (increased bioavailability)'']', '[''Liver function in prolonged therapy'', ''ECG in patients with cardiac risk factors or concurrent QT-prolonging drugs'']', 'Category B; use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excreted in small amounts in breast milk; use with caution.', 'Dispersible tablet formulations used widely in pediatric respiratory infections.', 'No primary dose adjustment required unless severe renal and hepatic failure co-exist.', 'No major dose reduction in mild to moderate renal disease; reduce dose in severe renal impairment (eGFR < 15 mL/min).', 'Dose reduction (150 mg once daily) in severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Higher oral bioavailability and acid stability than erythromycin'', ''Once or twice daily dosing improves compliance'', ''Concentrates heavily in respiratory tissues and macrophages'']', '[''Lower activity against Haemophilus influenzae than amoxicillin-clavulanate'', ''Potential for QT prolongation'']', '[''Take at least 15 minutes before food or on an empty stomach for optimal absorption'', ''Excellent penetration into respiratory tissues'']', 'ROXI-thromycin: Rocks the respiratory tract twice daily; take before meals.', '-thromycin', 'rox-ith-roe-MYE-sin', 'रॉक्सिथ्रो-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('moxifloxacin', 'Moxifloxacin', 'Moxifloxacin', 'Moxifloxacin hydrochloride', 'Moxifloxacin hydrochloride', 'Antibiotics', 'A fourth-generation ''respiratory'' fluoroquinolone with enhanced Gram-positive, anaerobic, and atypical coverage, widely used for pneumonia, tuberculosis, and intra-abdominal infections.', '[''400 mg'', ''400 mg/250 mL IV infusion'', ''0.5% eye drops'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Solution for Infusion'', ''Ophthalmic Solution'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Ophthalmic'']', 'Bactericidal dual-target inhibition of bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV, preventing DNA replication, transcription, and repair.', 'Concentration-dependent bactericidal killing (AUC/MIC driven); potent against Streptococcus pneumoniae, Mycoplasma, Legionella, anaerobes, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis.', 'Well absorbed (approx. 90%) from GI tract; food delays absorption slightly but does not reduce overall exposure.', 'Extensive penetration into lung tissue, alveolar macrophages, bronchial mucosa, and sinus tissues; 30-50% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Metabolized by phase II glucuronide and sulfate conjugation in the liver; not significantly dependent on cytochrome P450 enzymes.', 'Approximately 45% eliminated unchanged in urine and faeces; remainder excreted as glucuronide and sulfate metabolites.', 'Approximately 90%', '12 hours (allowing once-daily dosing)', '30% to 50%', 'Peak serum concentration in 1 to 3 hours', '24 hours', '[''Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)'', ''Acute bacterial sinusitis'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal infections (with anaerobic coverage)'', ''Complicated skin and soft tissue infections'', ''Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB second-line)'']', '[''History of tendon disorders related to fluoroquinolone treatment'', ''Documented QT prolongation or concurrent use of Class IA/III antiarrhythmics'', ''Severe hepatic impairment'', ''Myasthenia gravis'']', '[''Black box warnings: Tendinitis and tendon rupture (especially Achilles tendon)'', ''Peripheral neuropathy and central nervous system toxicities (confusion, hallucinations)'', ''Exacerbation of myasthenia gravis'', ''Aortic aneurysm rupture risk'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Dizziness'', ''Headache'', ''Dyspepsia'']', '[''Achilles tendon rupture'', ''QT prolongation / Torsades de pointes'', ''Severe peripheral neuropathy'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Hepatotoxicity'']', '[''Antacids containing aluminum/magnesium, iron supplements, sucralfate (chelation reduces absorption - separate by 4 hours)'', ''Class IA and III antiarrhythmics (additive QT prolongation)'', ''Oral hypoglycaemics (dysglycaemia risk)'', ''NSAIDs (increased seizure risk)'']', '[''ECG in patients at risk of arrhythmia'', ''Signs of tendinopathy (discontinue at first sign of tendon pain or swelling)'', ''Blood glucose in diabetics'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; fluoroquinolones induce cartilage damage in juvenile animal models.', 'Excreted in human milk; avoid during breastfeeding.', 'Generally avoided in children due to arthropathy risk, except in multidrug-resistant tuberculosis.', 'Higher risk of tendon rupture and severe QT prolongation; use with caution.', 'No dosage adjustment needed in renal impairment (including dialysis) as it undergoes hepatic conjugation.', 'Contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C).', '[''Once-daily dosing with 90% oral bioavailability'', ''No renal dosage adjustment needed'', ''Excellent anaerobic and atypical coverage alongside pneumococcal potency'']', '[''Class warnings for tendinitis, aortic aneurysm, and QT prolongation'', ''Should be reserved for severe or refractory respiratory infections'']', '[''No dosage adjustment needed in renal disease (distinct from ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin)'', ''Do not take with antacids or iron pills at the same time'']', 'MOXI-floxacin: Mixed anaerobic/aerobic activity, Once-daily, eXcellent lung penetration.', '-floxacin', 'mox-i-FLOX-a-sin', 'मॉक्सी-फ़्लोक्