('ofloxacin', 'Ofloxacin', 'Ofloxacin', 'Ofloxacin', 'Ofloxacin', 'Antibiotics', 'A second-generation broad-spectrum fluoroquinolone widely utilized in India for gastrointestinal infections, enteric fever, and genitourinary tract infections.', '[''100 mg DT'', ''200 mg'', ''400 mg'', ''200 mg/100 mL IV infusion'', ''0.3% eye/ear drops'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Dispersible Tablet'', ''IV Infusion'', ''Ophthalmic/Otic Drops'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Ophthalmic'', ''Otic'']', 'Bactericidal inhibition of DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV, halting bacterial chromosome supercoiling, transcription, and division.', 'Broad-spectrum activity against Gram-negative bacilli (Enterobacteriaceae, Salmonella, Shigella) and atypical bacteria; moderately active against Gram-positives.', 'Rapidly and almost completely absorbed from the GI tract (approx. 98% bioavailability); peak levels in 1 to 2 hours.', 'Extensive tissue distribution into prostate, lungs, kidneys, bile, and blister fluid; 20-30% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism (<10%); less than 5% metabolised to desmethylofloxacin.', 'Predominantly eliminated unchanged by the kidneys via tubular secretion and glomerular filtration (>80%).', 'Near 100%', '5 to 7 hours (prolonged in renal failure)', '20% to 30%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 hours', '[''Enteric fever (typhoid fever) and infectious gastroenteritis (often with ornidazole)'', ''Urinary tract infections (pyelonephritis, complicated cystitis)'', ''Bacterial prostatitis'', ''Pelvic inflammatory disease and non-gonococcal urethritis'', ''Bacterial conjunctivitis and otitis externa (drops)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to quinolones'', ''History of fluoroquinolone-induced tendinitis'', ''Epilepsy / pre-existing central nervous system seizure disorders'', ''Myasthenia gravis'']', '[''Tendon rupture and tendinopathy'', ''CNS stimulation and seizure precipitation'', ''Separate administration from multivalent cations (iron, calcium, magnesium, aluminum) by at least 2 hours'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Insomnia / sleep disturbances'', ''Headache'', ''Dizziness'', ''Diarrhoea'']', '[''Achilles tendinitis / rupture'', ''Convulsions'', ''Peripheral neuropathy'', ''QT interval prolongation'', ''Toxic epidermal necrolysis (rare)'']', '[''Multivalent cation antacids and iron (markedly impairs absorption)'', ''Theophylline (modest inhibition of metabolism)'', ''NSAIDs (increased seizure risk)'', ''Warfarin'']', '[''Renal function tests (BUN / creatinine)'', ''Signs of tendon pain or swelling'', ''Blood sugar in diabetic patients'']', 'Avoid during pregnancy due to potential for juvenile cartilage damage.', 'Excreted in human breast milk; avoid use or discontinue breastfeeding.', 'Contraindicated in children and growing adolescents, except under strict specialist indications (e.g. MDR infections).', 'Renal clearance is reduced; dose adjustment based on creatinine clearance.', 'Dose reduction mandatory: if CrCl 20-50 mL/min, give 200 mg every 24h; if CrCl < 20 mL/min, give 100 mg every 24h.', 'Caution in severe liver cirrhosis (maximum 400 mg daily).', '[''Near 100% oral bioavailability: oral and IV doses are interchangeable'', ''Excellent penetration into prostatic tissue and urinary tract'', ''Established track record in enteric infections'']', '[''Class warnings for tendon and neurological toxicities'', ''Substantial resistance emergence in Enterobacteriaceae and Salmonella'']', '[''Widely combined with ornidazole for acute infectious diarrhoea in India'', ''Dose must be reduced in kidney disease because it is eliminated by the kidneys'']', 'O-FLOX-acin: Over 95% bioavailability; Outflows through kidney.', '-floxacin', 'oh-FLOX-a-sin', 'ओफ़्लोक्सा-सिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('norfloxacin', 'Norfloxacin', 'Norfloxacin', 'Norfloxacin', 'Norfloxacin', 'Antibiotics', 'A synthetic fluoroquinolone that achieves very high concentrations in the urinary tract and gastrointestinal lumen with low systemic tissue levels, making it ideal for UTIs and bacterial diarrhoea.', '[''100 mg'', ''200 mg'', ''400 mg'', ''0.3% eye/ear drops'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Ophthalmic/Otic Drops'']', '[''Oral'', ''Ophthalmic'', ''Otic'']', 'Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, preventing DNA supercoiling, synthesis, and cell division.', 'Bactericidal activity against Gram-negative urinary and gastrointestinal pathogens (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter).', 'Rapidly but incompletely absorbed (30-40% bioavailability); food or milk impairs absorption.', 'Low serum concentrations; concentrates intensely in renal parenchyma, bladder urine, and intestinal lumen; 14% protein bound.', 'Partially metabolised in the liver to six active metabolites possessing weaker antibacterial activity.', 'Approximately 30-40% excreted unchanged in urine within 24 hours; about 30% excreted in faeces.', '30% to 40%', '3 to 4 hours (prolonged in renal failure)', '14%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 hours', '[''Acute and recurrent uncomplicated urinary tract infections (UTIs)'', ''Complicated UTIs (except with systemic sepsis)'', "Bacterial infectious gastroenteritis / traveller''s diarrhoea", ''Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) prophylaxis in cirrhotics'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to fluoroquinolones'', ''History of tendinitis or tendon rupture'', ''Myasthenia gravis'', ''Pregnancy and lactation'']', '[''Do not use for systemic bloodstream infections (poor systemic tissue penetration)'', ''Tendon disorders and central neurotoxicity'', ''Take 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals with plenty of fluids'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Abdominal cramps'', ''Headache'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Tendon rupture (Achilles)'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Crystalluria (especially in alkaline urine with low fluid intake)'']', '[''Antacids containing Al/Mg, calcium, iron supplements (drastically reduce absorption)'', ''Theophylline (increases theophylline levels)'', ''Warfarin (increased INR)'']', '[''Urinary symptoms and urine culture'', ''Adequate hydration to prevent crystalluria'', ''Renal function in elderly or impaired patients'']', 'Avoid during pregnancy; potential arthropathy in growing joints.', 'Excreted in breast milk; avoid use.', 'Not recommended in children and adolescents under 18 years.', 'Dose adjustment for renal impairment; higher tendon rupture risk.', 'If CrCl ≤ 30 mL/min, reduce dose to 400 mg once daily.', 'No dose adjustment necessary.', '[''High urinary and fecal concentrations with low systemic toxicity'', ''Established agent for SBP prophylaxis in chronic liver disease'']', '[''Poor systemic absorption makes it inappropriate for pneumonia or deep-seated systemic sepsis'', ''Rising fluoroquinolone resistance in uropathogens'']', '[''Drink plenty of water to prevent crystals in the urine'', ''Take on an empty stomach with a full glass of water'']', 'NOR-floxacin: Narrower systemic levels, NOT for pneumonia, Optimal for Renal/gut tract.', '-floxacin', 'nor-FLOX-a-sin', 'नॉरफ़्लोक्सा-सिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('faropenem', 'Faropenem', 'Faropenem', 'Faropenem sodium', 'Faropenem sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'An orally bioavailable penem antibiotic with high stability against extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs), used for resistant respiratory, ENT, and urinary tract infections.', '[''150 mg'', ''200 mg'', ''300 mg'', ''100 mg DT'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Dispersible Tablet'', ''Dry Syrup'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds with high affinity to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), primarily PBP1A, 1B, 2, and 3, inhibiting peptidoglycan cell wall cross-linking and triggering autolysis.', 'Broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against Gram-positive, Gram-negative, and anaerobic pathogens; highly resistant to hydrolysis by class A, C, and D beta-lactamases including ESBLs.', 'Rapidly absorbed after oral administration; peak plasma levels reached in 1 to 1.5 hours.', 'Distributes well into sputum, tonsils, sinus mucosa, and middle ear fluid; 96% bound to plasma proteins in humans.', 'Hydrolyzed in renal brush border by renal dehydropeptidase-I (DHP-I) to inactive metabolites, but more stable to DHP-I than imipenem.', 'Approximately 15-20% excreted unchanged in urine, with remainder as ring-opened inactive metabolites.', 'Approximately 70-80%', '1 hour', '96%', 'Peak serum level within 1 to 1.5 hours', '8 hours (administered three times daily)', '[''Community-acquired pneumonia with suspected or proven ESBL organisms'', ''Acute bacterial sinusitis and otitis media refractory to first-line agents'', ''Complicated and recurrent urinary tract infections'', ''Skin and soft tissue infections'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to faropenem, penems, or other carbapenem beta-lactam antibiotics'', ''History of anaphylaxis to cephalosporins or penicillins'']', '[''Reserve for confirmed or highly suspected resistant infections to prevent emergence of carbapenemase resistance'', ''Diarrhoea and risk of Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Cross-reactivity with beta-lactam allergy'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Abdominal discomfort'', ''Nausea'', ''Loose stools'', ''Rash'']', '[''Pseudomembranous colitis'', ''Anaphylaxis'', ''Liver transaminase elevations'', ''Seizures (extremely rare)'']', '[''Imipenem/cilastatin or other carbapenems (do not combine)'', ''Sodium valproate (penems/carbapenems may lower valproate levels)'', ''Probenecid (prolongs faropenem excretion)'']', '[''Clinical response'', ''Stool frequency (watch for severe watery diarrhoea)'', ''Renal function'']', 'Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed and potential benefit justifies potential risk.', 'Excreted into breast milk in small amounts; use caution.', 'Paediatric dry syrup and dispersible tablets used in selected refractory respiratory infections under specialist supervision.', 'Assess renal function; reduce dose in impaired renal clearance.', 'Dose adjustment required in renal impairment: if CrCl 30-50 mL/min, give 150-200 mg q12h; if CrCl < 30 mL/min, give 150 mg q24h.', 'No dosage adjustment needed for hepatic impairment.', '[''Only orally active penem/carbapenem-like antibiotic available in outpatient practice'', ''Stable against extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs)'']', '[''Short half-life requiring three-times-daily dosing'', ''Should be used judiciously as a reserve antibiotic to prevent resistance'']', '[''Oral reserve antibiotic for ESBL-producing bacteria'', ''Take three times daily at evenly spaced intervals'']', 'FARO-penem: Oral FAR-reaching Penem against ESBLs.', '-penem', 'fa-roe-PEN-em', 'फ़ैरो-पेनेम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('telithromycin', 'Telithromycin', 'Telithromycin', 'Telithromycin', 'Telithromycin', 'Antibiotics', 'A semi-synthetic ketolide antibiotic derived from erythromycin, engineered to overcome macrolide resistance in Streptococcus pneumoniae.', '[''400 mg'', ''800 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds to two distinct domains (II and V) on the 23S rRNA of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit, maintaining high affinity even against MLSB-resistant strains.', 'Concentration-dependent bactericidal action against penicillin- and erythromycin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae and respiratory pathogens.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration attained within 1 to 3 hours; unaffected by food.', 'High penetration into respiratory tissues, alveolar macrophages, and white blood cells; 60-70% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized by CYP3A4 (50%) and non-CYP pathways (50%).', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 7%) and urine (approx. 13% unchanged).', 'Approximately 57%', '10 hours', '60% to 70%', '1 to 2 hours', '24 hours', '[''Community-acquired pneumonia due to multi-drug resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae (MDRSP)'']', '[''Myasthenia gravis (black box warning: risk of fatal respiratory failure)'', ''History of telithromycin-associated hepatitis or jaundice'', ''Concurrent use with pimozide, cisapride, or ergot alkaloids'']', '[''Severe hepatotoxicity including acute liver failure and death'', ''Visual disturbances (blurred vision, difficulty focusing) requiring caution when driving'', ''QTc interval prolongation'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Dizziness'', ''Dysgeusia (altered taste)'', ''Blurred vision'']', '[''Fulminant liver failure'', ''Exacerbation of myasthenia gravis (fatal)'', ''Ventricular arrhythmias / Torsades de pointes'', ''Loss of consciousness / syncope'']', '[''Simvastatin / atorvastatin (severe rhabdomyolysis due to CYP3A4 inhibition)'', ''Digoxin (increased levels)'', ''Ketoconazole / itraconazole'', ''Midazolam'']', '[''Liver enzymes at first sign of fatigue or jaundice'', ''Visual acuity'', ''ECG in patients with cardiac risk'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy unless benefits clearly outweigh potential fetal risks.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding during therapy.', 'Not recommended in children under 18 years of age.', 'Assess hepatic and cardiac function; monitor visual symptoms closely.', 'Dose reduction (400 mg daily) in severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min).', 'Contraindicated in patients with prior telithromycin hepatotoxicity; caution in hepatic impairment.', '[''Retains activity against macrolide-resistant pneumococci due to dual 23S rRNA binding'', ''Convenient once-daily 800 mg regimen'']', '[''Severe hepatotoxicity and myasthenia gravis warnings limit use to strict reserve indication'', ''Visual disturbances can impair driving'']', '[''Strictly contraindicated in myasthenia gravis'', ''Reserve solely for community-acquired pneumonia when other options fail'']', 'TELI-thromycin: Tough on resistant bugs, but Terribly Toxic to the liver and myasthenia.', '-thromycin', 'tel-ith-roe-MYE-sin', 'टेलिथ्रो-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('gemifloxacin', 'Gemifloxacin', 'Gemifloxacin', 'Gemifloxacin mesylate', 'Gemifloxacin mesylate', 'Antibiotics', 'An advanced fluoroquinolone with potent dual-targeting against bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, specifically indicated for acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and mild-to-moderate CAP.', '[''320 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Inhibits both topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase) and topoisomerase IV, resulting in rapid bacterial DNA fragmentation and cell death.', 'High potency against Streptococcus pneumoniae (including penicillin-resistant strains), Haemophilus influenzae, and atypical pathogens.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration within 0.5 to 2 hours.', 'Excellent penetration into lung parenchyma and bronchial mucosa; 55-73% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Undergoes limited hepatic metabolism; not extensively metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzymes.', 'Approximately 36% eliminated in urine and 61% in faeces as unchanged drug and metabolites.', 'Approximately 71%', '7 hours', '55% to 73%', '1 hour', '24 hours', '[''Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis (AECB)'', ''Mild to moderate community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to fluoroquinolones'', ''History of quinolone-associated tendinopathy'', ''Myasthenia gravis'']', '[''Maculopapular rash occurs more frequently, particularly in females under 40 and with therapy exceeding 7 days'', ''Tendinitis and tendon rupture'', ''QT prolongation'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash (erythematous/maculopapular)'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Abdominal pain'']', '[''Achilles tendon rupture'', ''Severe cutaneous adverse reactions'', ''QTc prolongation'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Antacids containing magnesium/aluminum, iron, sucralfate (take gemifloxacin 2 hours before or 3 hours after)'', ''Class IA and III antiarrhythmics'', ''Probenecid (increases AUC)'']', '[''Skin inspection for rash (discontinue if rash develops)'', ''Tendon pain or swelling'', ''Renal function'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; potential arthropathy in weight-bearing joints.', 'Excreted in breast milk; avoid use during lactation.', 'Contraindicated in children under 18 years of age.', 'No dose adjustment based on age alone; monitor renal function.', 'If CrCl ≤ 40 mL/min, reduce dose to 160 mg every 24 hours.', 'No dose adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''High potency against multi-drug resistant S. pneumoniae'', ''Short 5-day course effective for AECB'']', '[''Higher rash incidence compared to other fluoroquinolones'', ''Standard fluoroquinolone class side effects'']', '[''Limit treatment duration to 5-7 days to minimize rash incidence'', ''Once daily 320 mg tablet'']', 'GEMI-floxacin: GEM for the lungs, but watch for rash in 5 days.', '-floxacin', 'jem-i-FLOX-a-sin', 'जेमी-फ़्लोक्सा-सिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('teicoplanin', 'Teicoplanin', 'Teicoplanin', 'Teicoplanin', 'Teicoplanin', 'Antibiotics', 'A glycopeptide antibiotic related to vancomycin with lower nephrotoxicity and red-man syndrome rates, administered once daily or via intramuscular injection for serious Gram-positive infections.', '[''200 mg vial'', ''400 mg vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection/Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Inhibits cell wall peptidoglycan synthesis by binding tightly to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of cell wall precursor pentapeptides, preventing polymerization.', 'Bactericidal against methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), enterococci, and Streptococcus species; lacks activity against Gram-negative organisms.', 'Not absorbed orally (oral use restricted to C. difficile enterocolitis); 100% bioavailable after IM or IV administration.', 'Distributes well into lung tissue, synovial fluid, and bone; 90-95% bound to plasma proteins; prolonged elimination half-life.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism (<3%); does not undergo significant biotransformation.', 'Approximately 80% eliminated unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration.', '90% after intramuscular injection; 100% IV', '100 to 170 hours (very long elimination half-life)', '90% to 95%', 'Loading doses required over first 48 hours to reach target therapeutic trough levels (>15-20 mcg/mL)', '24 hours (once-daily maintenance after loading)', '[''Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) bacteremia and endocarditis'', ''Complicated bone and joint infections (osteomyelitis)'', ''Complicated skin and soft tissue infections'', ''Hospital-acquired pneumonia due to MRSA'', ''Oral therapy for Clostridioides difficile colitis (second-line)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to teicoplanin or glycopeptides'']', '[''Loading doses are essential to achieve therapeutic trough levels rapidly'', ''Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, particularly when combined with aminoglycosides'', ''Cross-reactivity in vancomycin-allergic patients is possible though low'']', '[''Rash'', ''Local pain at intramuscular injection site'', ''Fever'', ''Mild transient serum creatinine rise'']', '[''Ototoxicity (tinnitus, high-frequency hearing loss)'', ''Nephrotoxicity / acute kidney injury'', ''Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia'', ''Red man-like syndrome (infrequent)'']', '[''Aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, colistin (additive nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity)'', ''Loop diuretics (additive ototoxicity)'', ''Ciclosporin'']', '[''Trough serum levels (target >15 mcg/mL for uncomplicated, >20-30 mcg/mL for endocarditis/osteomyelitis)'', ''Renal function (BUN/creatinine)'', ''Audiometry in prolonged courses'']', 'Use in pregnancy only if the potential benefit outweighs the risk of fetal ototoxicity.', 'Excretion in human milk is unknown; use with caution.', 'Approved for neonates and children; loading dose based on age and weight.', 'Renal clearance decreases with age; adjust maintenance dose based on creatinine clearance.', 'Dose adjustment required after day 4: if CrCl 30-80 mL/min, halve maintenance dose; if CrCl < 30 mL/min, give maintenance dose every third day.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''Can be administered intramuscularly as well as IV bolus'', ''Significantly lower incidence of nephrotoxicity and red-man syndrome than vancomycin'', ''Once-daily maintenance dosing'']', '[''Long half-life means slow correction if toxicity develops'', ''Requires multiple loading doses in first 48 hours to achieve therapeutic levels'']', '[''Three loading doses (every 12 hours) are crucial before switching to once-daily maintenance'', ''Can be given by direct IV injection over 5 minutes or IM injection'']', 'TEICO-planin: Takes Easier Injection (IM allowed, no slow infusion needed), Calmer than vancomycin.', '-planin', 'tye-koe-PLAN-in', 'टाइको-प्लानिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('cefotaxime', 'Cefotaxime', 'Cefotaxime', 'Cefotaxime sodium', 'Cefotaxime sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A parenteral third-generation cephalosporin with excellent cerebrospinal fluid penetration, widely used for neonatal sepsis, bacterial meningitis, and serious systemic infections.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'', ''1 g'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting bacterial peptidoglycan cell wall synthesis, resulting in cell wall lysis and bacterial death.', 'Potent bactericidal activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, and enteric Gram-negative bacilli; active metabolite desacetylcefotaxime acts synergistically.', 'Not absorbed orally; 100% bioavailable upon IV injection; peak levels within 30 minutes after IM.', 'Excellent penetration into CSF (inflamed meninges), pleural, peritoneal, and synovial fluid; 30-50% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Partially metabolized in the liver to desacetylcefotaxime, which possesses significant intrinsic antibacterial activity and synergy.', 'Approximately 60% excreted in urine unchanged, and 20% as active desacetyl metabolite.', '100% (parenteral)', '1 to 1.5 hours for parent drug; 1.5 hours for desacetyl metabolite', '30% to 50%', 'Immediate (IV)', '6 to 8 hours', '[''Acute bacterial meningitis (especially in neonates and young infants)'', ''Neonatal sepsis'', ''Community-acquired and hospital-acquired pneumonia'', ''Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)'', ''Complicated urinary tract infections and intra-abdominal sepsis'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cefotaxime or other cephalosporins'', ''History of severe immediate anaphylaxis to penicillins'']', '[''Dose reduction in renal failure to prevent neurotoxicity and encephalopathy'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis risk'', ''Safe in neonates compared to ceftriaxone (does not cause biliary sludging or kernicterus)'']', '[''Phlebitis at IV site'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash'', ''Nausea'', ''Eosinophilia'']', '[''Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Anaphylaxis'', ''Neutropenia and agranulocytosis (prolonged therapy)'', ''Neurotoxicity / encephalopathy in renal failure'']', '[''Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity)'', ''Probenecid (decreases renal tubular secretion)'', ''Loop diuretics'']', '[''Renal function'', ''Complete blood count in courses exceeding 10 days'', ''Neurological status in renal insufficiency'']', 'Category B; safe and widely used during pregnancy.', 'Excreted in low concentrations in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Preferred third-generation cephalosporin in neonates under 28 days of life (avoids kernicterus associated with ceftriaxone).', 'Assess renal clearance; adjust dosing interval in renal impairment.', 'If CrCl < 20 mL/min, reduce maintenance dose by 50% or extend interval to every 12 hours.', 'No major dosage adjustment required.', '[''Preferred cephalosporin in neonates because it does not displace bilirubin from albumin (no kernicterus risk)'', ''Excellent CSF penetration for meningitis'', ''Active metabolite acts synergistically'']', '[''Requires dosing every 6-8 hours (shorter half-life than ceftriaxone)'', ''Parenteral only'']', '[''First-choice 3rd-generation cephalosporin in neonates (safe from kernicterus)'', ''Administered every 6 to 8 hours for meningitis'']', 'CEFO-TAX-ime: Safe TAX for neonates (avoids ceftriaxone bilirubin displacement).', '-taxime', 'sef-oh-TAX-eem', 'सेफ़ो-टैक्सिम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('cefepime', 'Cefepime', 'Cefepime', 'Cefepime hydrochloride', 'Cefepime hydrochloride', 'Antibiotics', 'A fourth-generation parenteral cephalosporin with zwitterionic structure providing rapid penetration across outer bacterial membranes, highly active against Pseudomonas and Enterobacteriaceae.', '[''500 mg'', ''1 g'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to multiple penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2, PBP3, PBP1a/1b) inhibiting peptidoglycan synthesis; zwitterionic charge allows rapid diffusion through outer membrane porins.', 'Bactericidal activity against Gram-positive cocci (MSSA, Streptococcus) and Gram-negative bacilli (Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacter cloacae, Citrobacter); resists AmpC chromosomal beta-lactamases.', '100% bioavailable intravenously; rapid absorption following IM administration.', 'Widely distributed into lung, peritoneal fluid, bile, and inflamed meninges; approximately 20% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism (<10%); excreted primarily as parent drug.', 'Approximately 85% eliminated unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration.', '100% (IV)', '2 hours', '20%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '8 to 12 hours', '[''Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenia in haematology-oncology patients'', ''Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia (HAP/VAP)'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal infections (with metronidazole)'', ''Complicated urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis'', ''Severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cefepime, cephalosporins, or severe beta-lactam allergy'']', '[''Neurotoxicity (encephalopathy, myoclonus, seizures, non-convulsive status epilepticus) occurs if dose is not properly adjusted for renal dysfunction'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Positive direct Coombs test'']', '[''Phlebitis'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'']', '[''Cefepime-induced neurotoxicity / encephalopathy'', ''Non-co