('ceftazidime', 'Ceftazidime', 'Ceftazidime', 'Ceftazidime pentahydrate', 'Ceftazidime pentahydrate', 'Antibiotics', 'A third-generation parenteral cephalosporin with potent antipseudomonal activity, used for hospital-acquired infections, febrile neutropenia, and melioidosis.', '[''500 mg'', ''1 g'', ''2 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Binds to essential penicillin-binding proteins (PBP3), inhibiting peptidoglycan synthesis and producing filamentation and lysis of bacterial cells.', 'High bactericidal activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Gram-negative bacilli; weaker Gram-positive activity compared to cefotaxime/ceftriaxone; cornerstone agent for Burkholderia pseudomallei (melioidosis).', '100% bioavailable parenterally; rapid peak within 1 hour following IM injection.', 'Good penetration into bronchial secretions, pleural fluid, bone, and cerebrospinal fluid (inflamed meninges); <10% protein bound.', 'Not metabolized; excreted entirely as parent drug.', '80% to 90% eliminated unchanged in urine by glomerular filtration within 24 hours.', '100% (IV)', '1.5 to 2 hours (prolonged in renal impairment)', '<10%', 'Immediate (IV)', '8 hours', '[''Severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections'', ''Hospital-acquired pneumonia and ventilator-associated pneumonia'', ''Acute intensive phase of Melioidosis (Burkholderia pseudomallei)'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal and urinary tract infections'', ''Febrile neutropenia in immunocompromised patients'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to ceftazidime, cephalosporins, or severe immediate beta-lactam anaphylaxis'']', '[''Renal dose adjustment necessary to avoid neurotoxicity'', ''Induction of AmpC beta-lactamases in Enterobacter species'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Phlebitis at injection site'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Transient eosinophilia'', ''Maculopapular rash'', ''Nausea'']', '[''Encephalopathy and seizures in renal failure'', ''Agranulocytosis'', ''Pseudomembranous coliti