('ceftazidime-avibactam', 'Ceftazidime / Avibactam', 'Ceftazidime + Avibactam', 'Ceftazidime pentahydrate and avibactam sodium', 'Ceftazidime pentahydrate and avibactam sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A novel beta-lactam / non-beta-lactam diazabicyclooctane beta-lactamase inhibitor combination active against carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (KPC, OXA-48).', '[''2.5 g vial (2 g ceftazidime / 0.5 g avibactam)'']', '[''Powder for Concentrate for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Ceftazidime inhibits cell wall synthesis; avibactam is a non-beta-lactam beta-lactamase inhibitor that reversibly inhibits Class A (KPC), Class C (AmpC), and some Class D (OXA-48) beta-lactamases via covalent carbamylation.', 'Restores ceftazidime activity against carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae (KPC), OXA-48 carbapenemases, and ESBLs; inactive against Class B metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM) unless paired with aztreonam.', '100% bioavailable intravenously; administered as a 2-hour IV infusion.', 'Good penetration into lungs, abdominal cavity, and kidneys; <10% (ceftazidime) and 8% (avibactam) protein bound.', 'Neither component undergoes significant hepatic metabolism.', 'Both excreted predominantly unchanged in urine by glomerular filtration (>80% within 24 hours).', '100% (IV)', '2 to 2.7 hours', '<10%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '8 hours', '[''Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE) infections producing KPC or OXA-48'', ''Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated bacterial pneumonia'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal infections (with metronidazole)'', ''Complicated urinary tract infections / pyelonephritis'', ''Synergistic combination with aztreonam for NDM metallo-beta-lactamase producers'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity to ceftazidime, avibactam, or cephalosporins'']', '[''Renal dose adjustment is mandatory to prevent underdosing and treatment failure or neurotoxicity'', ''Not active against metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM) on its own'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'']', '[''Positive direct Coombs test'', ''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'', ''Constipation'']', '[''Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Encephalopathy / neurotoxicity in renal impairment'', ''Anaphylaxis'', ''Thrombocytopenia'']', '[''OAT1/OAT3 inhibitors (may increase avibactam levels slightly)'', ''Probenecid'', ''Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity)'']', '[''Renal function tests (eGFR daily in ICU patients)'', ''Coombs test if hemolytic anaemia suspected'', ''Bacterial susceptibility profile'']', 'Category B; use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; use caution.', 'Approved for infants 3 months and older for complicated UTI, intra-abdominal infections, and HAP/VAP.', 'Renal clearance is frequently decreased; adjust dose based on CrCl.', 'Dose adjustments required: if CrCl 31-50 mL/min, give 1.25 g q8h; if CrCl 16-30 mL/min, give 0.9375 g q12h; if CrCl 6-15 mL/min, give 0.9375 g q24h; on hemodialysis, give 0.9375 g after each dialysis session.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''Life-saving reserve agent for KPC and OXA-48 carbapenem-resistant infections'', ''Non-beta-lactam inhibitor mechanism allows reversible binding without destruction'']', '[''Inactive alone against NDM metallo-beta-lactamases (requires combining with Aztreonam)'', ''High cost and strict antimicrobial stewardship restrictions'']', '[''First-line therapy for KPC and OXA-48 carbapenemase-producing Enterobacteriaceae'', ''Pair with Aztreonam to overcome NDM metallo-beta-lactamases'']', 'AVI-bactam: AVIates over carbapenem resistance (KPC and OXA-48).', '-tazidime / -avibactam', 'sef-TAZ-i-deem / AV-i-bak-tam', 'सेफ़्टाज़ि-डीम / एवी-बैक्टम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('colistin', 'Colistin', 'Colistin (Colistimethate Sodium)', 'Colistimethate sodium (CMS)', 'Colistimethate sodium', 'Antibiotics', 'A polymyxin antibiotic prodrug acting as a cationic detergent against Gram-negative outer membranes, reserved for extensively drug-resistant (XDR) pathogens.', '[''1 million IU (approx. 80 mg CMS)'', ''2 million IU'', ''3 million IU vial'', ''4.5 million IU vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection/Infusion or Inhalation'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Inhalation'']', 'Binds to lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and phospholipids in the outer cell membrane of Gram-negative bacteria, displacing calcium and magnesium, causing membrane disruption and leakage of intracellular contents.', 'Rapid concentration-dependent bactericidal action against carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter baumannii, and Klebsiella pneumoniae; inherently inactive against Proteus, Serratia, and Burkholderia.', 'Not absorbed orally; administered IV as inactive prodrug CMS which slowly hydrolyzes to active colistin in plasma.', 'Poor penetration into pleural space, lung parenchyma, and CSF; nebulized route used as adjunct for ventilator pneumonia; 50% protein bound.', 'CMS undergoes spontaneous non-enzymatic hydrolysis to active colistin in blood; colistin itself is cleared non-renally.', 'CMS is excreted largely unchanged by renal glomerular filtration; because clearance is renal, a greater percentage of CMS is converted to active colistin in renal impairment.', '100% (IV)', '2 to 3 hours for CMS; 14 to 18 hours for formed colistin', '50%', 'Loading dose required (9 million IU) to achieve therapeutic concentrations rapidly', '8 to 12 hours', '[''Severe infections caused by multidrug-resistant and carbapenem-resistant Gram-negative bacteria (Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas)'', ''Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia (IV plus adjunctive nebulized)'', ''Complicated urinary tract infections due to XDR organisms'', ''Cystic fibrosis pulmonary Pseudomonas colonisation (inhalation)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to colistimethate sodium or polymyxins'', ''Myasthenia gravis (causes neuromuscular blockade)'']', '[''High risk of dose-dependent nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis)'', ''Neurotoxicity (paresthesias, ataxia, neuromuscular blockade, apnea)'', ''Loading dose mandatory due to slow conversion from CMS'']', '[''Elevated serum creatinine'', ''Oliguria'', ''Perioral and peripheral paresthesia'', ''Dizziness'', ''Bronchospasm (inhalation)'']', '[''Acute kidney injury / tubular necrosis'', ''Respiratory muscle paralysis / apnea (neuromuscular blockade)'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, vancomycin (drastically increases nephrotoxicity)'', ''Neuromuscular blocking agents (pancuronium, vecuronium - prolonged paralysis)'', ''Loop diuretics'']', '[''Serum creatinine and BUN daily'', ''Urine output every shift'', ''Signs of perioral numbness or muscle weakness'']', 'Category C; crosses placenta, potential nephrotoxicity; use only when no alternatives exist.', 'Excreted in low amounts in breast milk; use with caution.', 'Dosing based on mg of CBA (colistin base activity) or IU per kg; monitor renal status strictly.', 'High incidence of nephrotoxicity; close monitoring of renal parameters.', 'Dose adjustment mandatory: if CrCl 30-50 mL/min, give 5.5-7.5 million IU/day in 2 divided doses; if CrCl 10-29 mL/min, give 3-4.5 million IU/day in 2 divided doses; if CrCl < 10 mL/min, give 1.5-3 million IU/day. On hemodialysis, supplemental dose after dialysis.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''Retains activity against heavily resistant Gram-negative bacilli when all newer agents fail'', ''Can be administered via nebulization directly into the lungs'']', '[''High rate of acute nephrotoxicity (up to 30-50%)'', ''Complex dosing terminology (CMS vs CBA vs IU) creates medical calculation errors'']', '[''Always administer an initial loading dose (9 million IU) in critically ill patients'', ''Watch serum creatinine daily and monitor for perioral tingling'']', 'COLI-stin: Cold-hearted On Lipopolysaccharide; Kills Kidneys (nephrotoxic) and Nerves.', '-colistin', 'koe-LIS-tin', 'कोलिस-टिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('polymyxin-b', 'Polymyxin B', 'Polymyxin B', 'Polymyxin B sulfate', 'Polymyxin B sulfate', 'Antibiotics', 'An active polymyxin antibiotic administered directly as active drug (not a prodrug), preferred over colistin for systemic bloodstream infections due to predictable pharmacokinetics and lower renal dependency.', '[''500,000 IU vial (approx. 50 mg)'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Injection/Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Ophthalmic'', ''Topical'']', 'Binds to anionic lipid A portion of LPS in bacterial outer membrane, destabilizing membrane integrity and causing osmotic leakage of cellular contents.', 'Rapid concentration-dependent bactericidal action against carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Klebsiella pneumoniae.', 'Administered directly as the active molecule; no conversion required (unlike colistimethate).', 'Moderate tissue distribution; does not penetrate intact blood-brain barrier; 78-92% bound to serum proteins.', 'Cleared predominantly by non-renal metabolic and tissue binding pathways.', 'Less than 5% excreted unchanged in urine; predominantly eliminated via non-renal mechanisms.', '100% (IV)', '9 to 12 hours', '78% to 92%', 'Immediate upon infusion; loading dose (20,000-25,000 IU/kg) recommended', '12 hours', '[''Severe bacteremia and septic shock due to carbapenem-resistant Gram-negative bacteria (CRAB, CRE, CRPA)'', ''Hospital-acquired pneumonia due to XDR pathogens (IV component)'', ''Complicated skin and wound infections (topical/IV)'', ''Superficial eye infections (ophthalmic drops)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to polymyxins'', ''Concurrent administration with neurotoxic or nephrotoxic agents whenever possible'']', '[''Nephrotoxicity and acute tubular necrosis'', ''Neuromuscular blockade causing respiratory paralysis'', ''Skin hyperpigmentation (especially on face and neck) reported in prolonged therapy'']', '[''Transient facial/cutaneous flushing and hyperpigmentation'', ''Perioral numbness'', ''Elevated serum creatinine'', ''Phlebitis'']', '[''Acute renal failure / tubular necrosis'', ''Respiratory failure due to neuromuscular blockade'', ''Neurotoxicity (ataxia, convulsions)'']', '[''Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity and neuromuscular block)'', ''Amphotericin B'', ''Neuromuscular blockers (curare-like effects prolonged)'']', '[''Serum creatinine and BUN daily'', ''Urine output'', ''Neurological checks for paresthesia and muscle strength'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy unless benefits outweigh significant potential nephrotoxic and neuromuscular risks.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use with extreme caution.', 'Weight-based dosing (15,000-25,000 IU/kg/day in 2 divided doses); monitor renal parameters closely.', 'High risk of acute kidney injury; close laboratory monitoring required.', 'No routine dose reduction required for renal impairment (unlike colistin) because clearance is non-renal; some clinicians make modest reductions for severe toxicity.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''Administered as active drug (predictable rapid serum levels without relying on prodrug conversion)'', ''Does not require renal dose adjustments, making it the preferred polymyxin for bloodstream infections'']', '[''Not appropriate for urinary tract infections (low urinary excretion)'', ''High nephrotoxicity and risk of skin hyperpigmentation'']', '[''Preferred over colistin for blood and lung infections because blood levels are predictable'', ''Do NOT use for urinary tract infections due to minimal urinary excretion'']', 'POLYMYXIN-B: Best for Blood; Bad for Bladder (no urine clearance).', '-myxin', 'pol-i-MIX-in BEE', 'पॉलीमिक्सिन बी', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('tigecycline', 'Tigecycline', 'Tigecycline', 'Tigecycline', 'Tigecycline', 'Antibiotics', 'A first-in-class glycylcycline antibiotic overcoming classical tetracycline efflux and ribosomal protection mechanisms, used for complicated skin, soft tissue, and intra-abdominal infections.', '[''50 mg vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Binds reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit with 5 times higher affinity than minocycline, halting peptide chain growth and overcoming classical tetracycline efflux pumps and ribosomal protection proteins.', 'Broad bacteriostatic activity against MRSA, VRE, ESBL-producing Enterobacteriaceae, Acinetobacter baumannii, and anaerobes; inherently inactive against Pseudomonas, Proteus, and Providencia.', 'Administered exclusively by intravenous infusion over 30 to 60 minutes.', 'Extremely large volume of distribution (7 to 10 L/kg); concentrates heavily in tissues, bile, and bone; low serum concentrations; 71-89% protein bound.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism (<20%); primarily cleared unchanged.', 'Biliary excretion into faeces (approx. 59%) and renal excretion (approx. 33%).', '100% (IV)', '42 hours (prolonged terminal elimination half-life)', '71% to 89%', '100 mg IV loading dose followed by 50 mg every 12 hours', '12 hours', '[''Complicated intra-abdominal infections (peritonitis, abscesses)'', ''Complicated skin and soft tissue infections (including MRSA)'', ''Community-acquired pneumonia (when other alternatives fail)'', ''Part of combination therapy for multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter or CRE'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to tigecycline or tetracycline class antibiotics'']', '[''FDA Black Box Warning: Increased risk of all-cause mortality compared to other treatments; reserve for when alternatives are unavailable'', ''Severe nausea and vomiting in up to 30% of patients'', ''Low serum levels: avoid in primary bacteremia'']', '[''Nausea (very common, up to 30%)'', ''Vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'', ''Abdominal pain'']', '[''Acute pancreatitis (can be severe and fatal)'', ''All-cause mortality increase'', ''Severe hepatotoxicity / cholestasis'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'']', '[''Warfarin (monitor INR carefully)'', ''Oral contraceptives (decreased efficacy)'', ''Calcineurin inhibitors'']', '[''Serum amylase and lipase (discontinue if pancreatitis develops)'', ''Liver function tests'', ''Clinical signs of treatment failure'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; causes fetal bone growth restriction and tooth discolouration.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Avoid in children under 8 years of age due to permanent tooth staining and enamel hypoplasia.', 'No dose adjustment needed based on age.', 'No dose adjustment required in renal impairment or hemodialysis.', 'In severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C), reduce maintenance dose to 25 mg every 12 hours following the 100 mg loading dose.', '[''Broad spectrum against MRSA, VRE, ESBLs, and Acinetobacter'', ''No dose adjustment needed in kidney failure'', ''High tissue penetration for intra-abdominal infections'']', '[''Black box mortality warning; not suitable for bloodstream infections due to low serum levels'', ''High incidence of nausea/vomiting and risk of pancreatitis'']', '[''Do NOT use for primary bloodstream infections due to low serum concentrations (high tissue distribution)'', ''Watch for severe nausea, vomiting, and acute pancreatitis'']', 'TIGE-cycline: TISSUE tiger (great in tissue, terrible in blood), watch Pancreas and Nausea.', '-cycline', 'tye-ge-SYE-kleen', 'टाइगे-साइक्लिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('minocycline', 'Minocycline', 'Minocycline', 'Minocycline hydrochloride', 'Minocycline hydrochloride', 'Antibiotics', 'A highly lipophilic second-generation tetracycline with excellent tissue and CNS penetration, widely used for moderate-to-severe acne vulgaris, MRSA skin infections, and multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter.', '[''50 mg'', ''100 mg'', ''100 mg IV vial'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Tablet'', ''Extended-Release Tablet'', ''Powder for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing entry of aminoacyl-tRNA into the ribosomal acceptor (A) site, inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis; also possesses anti-inflammatory and matrix metalloproteinase inhibitory properties.', 'Broad bacteriostatic activity against Cutibacterium acnes, MRSA, atypical pathogens, Nocardia, and Acinetobacter baumannii; exhibits anti-inflammatory suppression of neutrophil chemotaxis.', 'Near-complete absorption (90-100%) from stomach and upper small intestine; minimally affected by food and dairy compared to older tetracyclines.', 'Highly lipophilic; achieves high concentrations in sebum, skin, CNS, tears, and saliva; 70-75% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Metabolized in the liver (approx. 50%) to several inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 20-30%) and urine (approx. 5-10% unchanged).', '90% to 100%', '15 to 22 hours (permits once or twice daily dosing)', '70% to 75%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 to 24 hours', '[''Moderate to severe inflammatory acne vulgaris'', ''Skin and soft tissue infections due to community-acquired MRSA'', ''Multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii infections (often IV component)'', ''Rheumatoid arthritis (disease-modifying adjunct due to MMP inhibition)'', ''Meningococcal carrier state eradication'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to tetracyclines'', ''Pregnancy and lactation'', ''Children under 8 years of age'']', '[''Vestibular side effects (dizziness, vertigo, ataxia) occur more commonly in females'', ''Drug-induced lupus erythematosus and autoimmune hepatitis with prolonged acne therapy'', ''Blue-grey hyperpigmentation of skin, nails, sclera, and oral mucosa'']', '[''Dizziness / vertigo'', ''Nausea'', ''Dyspepsia'', ''Photosensitivity'', ''Diarrhoea'']', '[''Drug-induced lupus syndrome'', ''Autoimmune hepatitis'', ''Pseudotumor cerebri (benign intracranial hypertension)'', ''Severe cutaneous drug reactions (DRESS syndrome)'']', '[''Multivalent cations: antacids, iron, calcium (chelate minocycline and reduce absorption; space by 2 hours)'', ''Isotretinoin (concurrent use contraindicated due to pseudotumor cerebri risk)'', ''Warfarin (potentiates anticoagulant effect)'']', '[''Signs of autoimmune symptoms (joint pain, rash, fever)'', ''Liver function in extended courses (>6 months)'', ''Inspection for skin/scleral hyperpigmentation'']', 'Contraindicated; causes fetal bone growth retardation and permanent teeth staining.', 'Excreted into breast milk; contraindicated in nursing mothers.', 'Contraindicated under 8 years due to permanent tooth discoloration and enamel defects.', 'Higher incidence of dizziness and vestibular ataxia; cautious dosing.', 'Does not accumulate significantly in renal failure; safe in moderate renal impairment without dose adjustment.', 'Use with caution in preexisting liver disease.', '[''Superior lipophilicity and tissue penetration for skin and sebaceous glands'', ''No significant accumulation in renal impairment compared to older tetracyclines'', ''Proven activity against community-associated MRSA and Acinetobacter'']', '[''Vestibular adverse effects (dizziness, lightheadedness)'', ''Risk of drug-induced lupus and slate-grey skin pigmentation on prolonged use'']', '[''Never combine with oral isotretinoin (causes dangerous intracranial hypertension / pseudotumor cerebri)'', ''Take with a full glass of water while sitting upright to prevent pill-induced oesophagitis'']', 'MINO-cycline: MINImizes acne and MRSA; watch for MINER''s dizziness (vestibular) and slate-grey skin.', '-cycline', 'min-oh-SYE-kleen', 'मिनो-साइक्लिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('tobramycin', 'Tobramycin', 'Tobramycin', 'Tobramycin sulfate', 'Tobramycin sulfate', 'Antibiotics', 'A potent aminoglycoside antibiotic with superior intrinsic activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa compared to gentamicin, administered IV or via inhalation for cystic fibrosis.', '[''20 mg/2 mL'', ''80 mg/2 mL vial'', ''300 mg/5 mL inhalation solution'', ''0.3% eye drops'']', '[''Solution for Injection'', ''Inhalation Solution'', ''Ophthalmic Drops'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Inhalation'', ''Ophthalmic'']', 'Binds irreversibly to the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of genetic code and premature termination of peptide chain synthesis, leading to cell membrane damage and bactericidal death.', 'Rapid concentration-dependent bactericidal killing (Cmax/MIC driven) with significant post-antibiotic effect (PAE); potent against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Gram-negative bacilli.', 'Poor oral absorption (<1%); 100% bioavailable parenterally; inhalation provides high local bronchial concentrations with low systemic absorption.', 'Confined largely to extracellular fluid; poor CSF and bronchial penetration after IV; <10% protein bound.', 'Not metabolized; excreted entirely as active parent drug.', 'Greater than 90% eliminated unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration.', '100% (IV/IM); systemic bioavailability approx. 12% following inhalation', '2 to 3 hours in adults with normal renal function', '<10%', 'Rapid peak serum concentration within 30 to 60 minutes after infusion', '24 hours (extended-interval once-daily dosing) or 8 hours (multiple daily dosing)', '[''Severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections and hospital-acquired sepsis'', ''Chronic pulmonary Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection in cystic fibrosis (inhalation)'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal and urinary tract infections (with beta-lactams)'', ''Bacterial conjunctivitis and keratitis (ophthalmic)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to tobramycin or other aminoglycosides'', ''Myasthenia gravis (causes neuromuscular blockade)'']', '[''Black box warnings: Nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis) and Ototoxicity (vestibular and irreversible cochlear hearing loss)'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring (trough and peak levels) mandatory'', ''Risk of fetal harm (congenital deafness)'']', '[''Elevated serum creatinine'', ''Cough and voice alteration (inhalation)'', ''Transient dizziness'', ''Ocular irritation (drops)'']', '[''Permanent sensorineural hearing loss / cochlear damage'', ''Acute tubular necrosis / renal failure'', ''Neuromuscular blockade / respiratory arrest'']', '[''Loop diuretics (furosemide - greatly potentiates ototoxicity)'', ''Vancomycin, colistin, cisplatin, amphotericin B (additive nephrotoxicity)'', ''Neuromuscular blocking agents'']', '[''Therapeutic drug monitoring: trough level (<1 mcg/mL for once-daily) and peak levels'', ''Serum creatinine and BUN every 2 to 3 days'', ''Audiometry and vestibular function checks'']', 'Category D; crosses placenta, risk of bilateral congenital irreversible deafness.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; poorly absorbed by infant gut, but use caution.', 'Commonly used in neonatal sepsis and pediatric cystic fibrosis; calculate dose carefully by weight.', 'Renal clearance is reduced; once-daily dosing preferred with strict trough monitoring.', 'Strict dose and interval adjustments mandatory: extend dosing interval (e.g. q36h or q48h) or reduce dose based on creatinine clearance and trough monitoring.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''2 to 4 times more active in vitro against Pseudomonas aeruginosa than gentamicin'', ''Once-daily extended interval dosing maximizes bactericidal peak while minimizing trough nephrotoxicity'', ''Inhalation formulation targets lungs without systemic toxicity'']', '[''Narrow therapeutic index requiring blood level monitoring'', ''Irreversible ototoxicity and cumulative nephrotoxicity'']', '[''More potent against Pseudomonas aeruginosa than gentamicin'', ''Keep trough levels below 1 mcg/mL to avoid renal damage and hearing loss'']', 'TOBRA-mycin: Top Antipseudomonal aminoglycoside; Target low Trough to protect ears and kidneys.', '-mycin', 'toe-bra-MYE-sin', 'टोब्रा-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('netilmicin', 'Netilmicin', 'Netilmicin', 'Netilmicin sulfate', 'Netilmicin sulfate', 'Antibiotics', 'A semi-synthetic aminoglycoside antibiotic derived from sisomicin with resistance to several bacterial modifying enzymes and demonstrably lower ototoxicity than gentamicin.', '[''25 mg/mL'', ''100 mg/mL'', ''300 mg/3 mL vial'', ''0.3% eye drops'']', '[''Solution for Injection'', ''Ophthalmic Solution'']', '[''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Ophthalmic'']', 'Binds irreversibly to the 30S bacterial ribosomal subunit, interfering with initiation complex formation and causing misreading of mRNA, leading to rapid bactericidal killing.', 'Broad bactericidal spectrum against Enterobacteriaceae (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter, Serratia) and Pseudomonas aeruginosa; active against many gentamicin-resistant strains due to stability against modifying enzymes.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed after intramuscular injection; 100% bioavailable IV.', 'Distributes primarily into extracellular fluids; achieves therapeutic levels in synovial, peritoneal, and pleural fluids; <10% protein bound.', 'Not metabolized in humans; eliminated entirely as active parent drug.', 'Greater than 80% excreted unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration within 24 hours.', '100% (IV/IM)', '2 to 2.5 hours', '<10%', 'Peak serum level within 30 to 60 minutes after IM or end of IV infusion', '24 hours (once-daily regimen) or 8-12 hours', '[''Severe systemic infections and bacteremia due to susceptible Gram-negative bacilli'', ''Complicated intra-abdominal sepsis (in combination with metronidazole)'', ''Complicated urinary tract infections and acute pyelonephritis'', ''Bacterial ocular infections (conjunctivitis, blepharitis)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to netilmicin or aminoglycosides'', ''Myasthenia gravis'']', '[''Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity (although reported to be less than gentamicin, clinical caution remains mandatory)'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring recommended in elderly or renal insufficiency'', ''Adequate patient hydration must be ensured'']', '[''Mild serum creatinine rise'', ''Dizziness'', ''Tinnitus'', ''Transient local injection site pain'']', '[''Ototoxicity (cochlear and vestibular damage)'', ''Acute tubular necrosis / renal failure'', ''Neuromuscular blockade'']', '[''Loop diuretics (furosemide - potentiates ototoxicity)'', ''Other nephrotoxic drugs (amphotericin B, vancomycin, cisplatin)'', ''Muscle relaxants'']', '[''Renal function tests (BUN / creatinine)'', ''Serum peak and trough drug levels'', ''Audiometric checks if treatment exceeds 7-10 days'']', 'Category D; potential risk of fetal eighth cranial nerve damage.', 'Excreted in small amounts in breast milk; use with caution.', 'Approved for pediatric and neonatal sepsis; dosing adjusted based on age and weight.', 'Renal clearance is reduced; once-daily dosing with trough monitoring is strongly advised.', 'Dose adjustment required based on creatinine clearance: extend dosing interval or decrease dose; monitor trough levels (target <1-2 mcg/mL).', 'No dose adjustment needed.', '[''Resistant to several bacterial aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, overcoming gentamicin resistance'', ''Significantly lower cochlear and vestibular toxicity reported in clinical trials than gentamicin'']', '[''Narrow therapeutic margin requiring monitoring'', ''Inactive against anaerobic pathogens'']', '[''Lower ototoxicity profile than gentamicin'', ''Can be given as a convenient single daily dose'']', 'NETIL-micin: NEWER aminoglycoside, NEATER on the ears than gentamicin.', '-micin', 'ne-til-MYE-sin', 'नेटिल-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('kanamycin', 'Kanamycin', 'Kanamycin', 'Kanamycin sulfate', 'Kanamycin sulfate', 'Antibiotics', 'An aminoglycoside antibiotic with bactericidal activity against aerobic Gram-negative bacilli and Mycobacterium tuberculosis, reserved for MDR-TB and serious hospital infections.', '[''500 mg vial'', ''1 g vial'']', '[''Powder for Injection'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Binds irreversibly to the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of codons and inhibition of translocation, terminating bacterial protein synthesis.', 'Concentration-dependent bactericidal action against Enterobacteriaceae and second-line activity against Mycobacterium tuberculosis.', 'Poorly absorbed orally (<1%); rapidly absorbed after IM injection with peak serum levels in 1 hour.', 'Distributes mainly into extracellular fluid; poor CSF and bronchial penetration; <10% protein bound.', 'Not metabolized; excreted entirely as active parent drug.', 'Greater than 90% eliminated unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration within 24 hours.', '100% (parenteral)', '2 to 3 hours', '<10%', 'Rapid peak serum concentration within 1 hour after IM injection', '12 to 24 hours', '[''Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB second-line injectable)'', ''Severe Gram-negative sepsis (when less toxic alternatives are contraindicated)'', ''Hepatic encephalopathy adjunct (oral, historically)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to kanamycin or other aminoglycosides'', ''Myasthenia gravis'', ''Preexisting high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss'']', '[''Black box warnings: Ototoxicity (high-frequency hearing loss and vestibular toxicity) and Nephrotoxicity'', ''Neuromuscular blockade and respiratory paralysis'', ''Avoid concurrent or sequential use of other ototoxic/nephrotoxic drugs'']', '[''Elevated serum creatinine'', ''Ototoxicity / tinnitus'', ''Dizziness'', ''Pain at injection site'']', '[''Irreversible bilateral sensorineural deafness'', ''Acute tubular necrosis / renal failure'', ''Neuromuscular paralysis'']', '[''Loop diuretics (furosemide - greatly increases ototoxicity risk)'', ''Vancomycin, amphotericin B (additive nephrotoxicity)'', ''Neuromuscular blocking agents'']', '[''Baseline and periodic audiometry'', ''Serum creatinine and BUN'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring (trough < 2-4 mcg/mL)'']', 'Category D; risk of congenital deafness in the fetus.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; use with caution.', 'Use restricted to multi-drug resistant tuberculosis in specialized centers.', 'Marked reduction in renal clearance; strict dose adjustment and auditory monitoring.', 'Strict dose reduction mandatory: extend interval to 24-48 hours and monitor peak and trough serum levels.', 'No dose adjustment required.', '[''Core historical second-line injectable for drug-resistant tuberculosis regimens'', ''Rapid concentration-dependent bactericidal killing'']', '[''High risk of irreversible hearing loss with prolonged courses'', ''Narrow therapeutic index requiring blood level monitoring'']', '[''Audit hearing with periodic audiometry during prolonged TB regimens'', ''Strictly monitor renal function and trough levels'']', 'KANA-mycin: Kill Acid-fast bacilli (MDR-TB), but Keep Audiometry Nearby.', '-mycin', 'kan-a-MYE-sin', 'काना-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('chloramphenicol', 'Chloramphenicol', 'Chloramphenicol', 'Chloramphenicol sodium succinate', 'Chloramphenicol sodium succinate', 'Antibiotics', 'A broad-spectrum classical antibiotic with excellent CSF penetration, reserved for life-threatening infections such as bacterial meningitis and rickettsial diseases when other options fail.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'', ''1 g vial'', ''0.5% eye drops'', ''1% eye ointment'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Powder for Injection'', ''Ophthalmic Solution'', ''Ophthalmic Ointment'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Ophthalmic'']', 'Binds reversibly to the 50S subunit of 70S bacterial ribosomes, inhibiting the peptidyl transferase reaction and preventing peptide bond formation.', 'Bacteriostatic against most organisms; bactericidal against Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis, and Streptococcus pneumoniae.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract (75-90% bioavailability); peak levels in 1 to 2 hours.', 'Exceptional tissue and lipid solubility; crosses the blood-brain barrier effectively (CSF levels 50-65% of serum); 50-60% protein bound.', 'Extensively metabolized by hepatic glucuronyl transferase (approx. 90%) to inactive glucuronide conjugates.', 'Excreted primarily in urine (approx. 90%), with about 10% as unchanged drug via glomerular filtration.', '75% to 90%', '1.5 to 3.5 hours in adults (significantly prolonged in neonates to 24+ hours)', '50% to 60%', '1 to 2 hours', '6 to 8 hours', '[''Acute bacterial meningitis due to H. influenzae, N. meningitidis, or S. pneumoniae when penicillins/cephalosporins are contraindicated'', ''Severe rickettsial infections (Rocky Mountain spotted fever, epidemic typhus)'', ''Severe Salmonella infections / typhoid fever (second-line reserve)'', ''Bacterial conjunctivitis (topical eye drops)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to chloramphenicol'', ''Preexisting bone marrow depression or blood dyscrasias'', ''Routine treatment of minor infections or prophylaxis'']', '[''Black box warning: Serious and fatal blood dyscrasias (aplastic anaemia, hypoplastic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, granulocytopenia)'', ''Grey baby syndrome in neonates due to immature glucuronyl transferase enzyme (vomiting, ashen cyanosis, hypothermia, cardiovascular collapse)'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring recommended (target peak 10-20 mcg/mL)'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Glossitis / stomatitis'', ''Dose-dependent reversible bone marrow suppression'']', '[''Idiosyncratic irreversible fatal aplastic anaemia (1 in 25,000-40,000 patients)'', ''Grey baby syndrome (neonatal fatal toxicity)'', ''Optic and peripheral neuritis (prolonged therapy)'']', '[''Phenytoin, warfarin, sulfonylureas (inhibits hepatic CYP metabolism, increasing their toxicity)'', ''Phenobarbital, rifampicin (induce chloramphenicol metabolism)'', ''Iron supplements'']', '[''Complete blood count with differential every 2 to 3 days'', ''Serum chloramphenicol levels'', ''Hepatic and renal function'']', 'Category C; contraindicated near term and during labor due to grey baby syndrome risk.', 'Excreted in human milk; avoid during breastfeeding due to infant bone marrow suppression risk.', 'Contraindicated in neonates and premature infants (grey baby syndrome); monitor levels strictly in older children.', 'Assess hepatic and renal function; dose adjustment needed in liver disease.', 'No major dose reduction required for parent drug, but inactive metabolites accumulate; monitor CBC closely.', 'Dose reduction mandatory in hepatic impairment to prevent toxic accumulation.', '[''Superior CSF penetration (equal to or higher than beta-lactams)'', ''Potent bactericidal activity against major meningeal pathogens'']', '[''Risk of fatal idiosyncratic aplastic anaemia (independent of dose)'', ''Lethal grey baby syndrome in neonates'']', '[''Strictly contraindicated in neonates (causes fatal grey baby syndrome)'', ''Risk of unpredictable, fatal aplastic anaemia restricts use to critical reserve indications'']', 'CHLORAM-PHENICOL: Causes Hematologic Aplastic-anaemia, Puts Neonates In Collapse (Grey baby syndrome).', '-phenicol', 'klor-am-FEN-i-kol', 'क्लोरम-फेनिकोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('pristinamycin', 'Pristinamycin', 'Pristinamycin', 'Pristinamycin (mixture of pristinamycin IA and IIA)', 'Pristinamycin', 'Antibiotics', 'An oral streptogramin antibiotic consisting of a synergistic combination of two factors (IA and IIA), used for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) and refractory streptococcal infections.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Consists of pristinamycin IA (a macrolactone) and IIA (a polyunsaturated macrolactone) that bind sequentially to separate sites on the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit; binding of IIA increases affinity for IA by 100-fold, transforming bacteriostatic action into bactericidal inhibition.', 'Synergistic bactericidal activity against MRSA, Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Enterococcus faecalis.', 'Moderately absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; food increases absorption; peak concentrations achieved in 2 to 3 hours.', 'Good penetration into skin, subcutaneous fat, tonsils, bone, and synovial fluid; 70-80% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Metabolized in the liver by oxidation and ester hydrolysis to several active and inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 70-80%) via the biliary system; less than 10% in urine.', 'Approximately 40% to 50%', '2 to 4 hours', '70% to 80%', '1 to 2 hours', '8 hours (administered three times daily)', '[''Skin and soft tissue infections caused by MRSA and Streptococcus'', ''Streptococcal pharyngitis and tonsillitis when beta-lactams and macrolides are unsuitable'', ''Exacerbations of chronic bronchitis due to susceptible pathogens'', ''Bone and joint infections (osteomyelitis) as step-down oral therapy'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to pristinamycin or other streptogramins (e.g., quinupristin/dalfopristin)'', ''Severe hepatic impairment'']', '[''Gastrointestinal intolerance is common; take with meals to reduce nausea'', ''Risk of Clostridioides difficile colitis'', ''Potential for CYP3A4 inhibition'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Epigastric discomfort'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Cutaneous rash'']', '[''Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (AGEP - acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis)'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'', ''Hepatotoxicity'']', '[''CYP3A4 substrates (cyclosporine, tacrolimus, carbamazepine - increases their serum levels)'', ''Oral contraceptives'', ''Warfarin'']', '[''Liver enzymes in prolonged courses'', ''Skin evaluation for pustular or blistering rash'', ''Stool frequency'']', 'Use during pregnancy only if clearly necessary; limited human safety data.', 'Excretion in breast milk unknown; use with caution.', 'Tablets may be crushed for children over 6 years of age (50 mg/kg/day in 2-3 divided doses).', 'No primary dose adjustment needed; assess gastrointestinal tolerance.', 'No dose adjustment required in renal impairment (eliminated primarily via the biliary route).', 'Dose reduction recommended in hepatic insufficiency.', '[''Oral bactericidal option for MRSA skin and soft tissue infections'', ''Synergistic dual-component mechanism prevents rapid emergence of resistance'', ''Safe in renal impairment without dose adjustment'']', '[''Frequent dosing required (three times daily with meals)'', ''Significant incidence of gastrointestinal side effects'']', '[''Take with meals to improve absorption and reduce stomach upset'', ''Synergistic streptogramin with oral activity against MRSA'']', 'PRISTINA-mycin: Pristine oral skin MRSA fighter; 2 parts work synergistically.', '-mycin', 'pris-tin-a-MYE-sin', 'प्रिस्टिना-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('tedizolid', 'Tedizolid', 'Tedizolid Phosphate', 'Tedizolid phosphate', 'Tedizolid phosphate', 'Antibiotics', 'A second-generation oxazolidinone antibiotic 4 to 8 times more potent than linezolid, administered once daily for 6 days for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI).', '[''200 mg'', ''200 mg IV vial'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Lyophilized Powder for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Prodrug converted by plasma phosphatases to active tedizolid, which binds to the 23S rRNA of the 50S bacterial ribosomal subunit, inhibiting initiation complex formation and protein synthesis.', 'Potent activity against MRSA, VRSA, VRE (Enterococcus faecalis and faecium), and linezolid-resistant strains carrying the cfr chloramphenicol-florfenicol resistance methyltransferase gene.', 'Rapidly and near-completely absorbed orally; 91% absolute oral bioavailability; food does not affect exposure.', 'Extensive tissue distribution into skin and adipose tissue; 70-90% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Prodrug rapidly dephosphorylated in blood to active tedizolid; hepatic metabolism to inactive sulfate conjugate; minimal CYP involvement.', 'Excreted primarily in faeces (approx. 82%) and urine (approx. 18%, mostly as inactive sulfate conjugate).', 'Approximately 91%', '12 hours (enables once-daily dosing)', '70% to 90%', '1 to 2 hours', '24 hours', '[''Acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI) caused by MRSA, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Enterococcus faecalis'', ''Diabetic foot infections involving resistant Gram-positive cocci'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to tedizolid or oxazolidinones'']', '[''Myelosuppression and peripheral/optic neuropathy (lower risk than linezolid during the approved 6-day course, but monitor in prolonged use)'', ''Weak reversible MAO inhibitor (caution with serotonergic agents)'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Dizziness'']', '[''Thrombocytopenia / myelosuppression (rare in 6-day course)'', ''Peripheral and optic neuropathy (with extended off-label use)'', ''Serotonin syndrome (rare)'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'']', '[''Serotonergic agents (SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans - lower MAO inhibition than linezolid, but monitor)'', ''Adrenergic agents (pseudoephedrine - potential blood pressure elevation)'']', '[''Complete blood counts (platelets, hemoglobin) if therapy exceeds 6 days'', ''Visual symptoms if used off-label for prolonged periods'', ''Clinical resolution of skin lesions'']', 'Category C; use only if potential benefit justifies potential fetal risk.', 'Excreted in animal milk; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 12 years and older (200 mg once daily).', 'No dosage adjustment needed based on age.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment or hemodialysis.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild, moderate, or severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Once-daily dosing for just 6 days (compared to linezolid BID for 10-14 days)'', ''4-8 times more potent in vitro than linezolid'', ''Much lower rate of myelosuppression and monoamine oxidase inhibition during standard courses'', ''No dose adjustment in renal or hepatic failure'']', '[''Approved only for skin infections (limited clinical trial data for pneumonia and bacteremia)'', ''Higher acquisition cost'']', '[''Once-daily 200 mg dose for only 6 days for skin infections'', ''Overcomes some linezolid-resistant cfr strains'']', 'TEDI-zolid: TIME-SAVING (once daily, 6 days), Tougher on MRSA than linezolid.', '-zolid', 'te-DIZ-oh-lid', 'टेडिज़ो-लिड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('dalbavancin', 'Dalbavancin', 'Dalbavancin', 'Dalbavancin hydrochloride', 'Dalbavancin hydrochloride', 'Antibiotics', 'A second-generation lipoglycopeptide antibiotic with an ultra-long terminal half-life of 14 days, permitting complete therapy of acute bacterial skin infections in one or two doses.', '[''500 mg vial'']', '[''Lyophilized Powder for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Binds to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of growing bacterial peptidoglycan chains, blocking transglycosylation and transpeptidation; lipophilic side chain anchors to membrane, increasing potency.', 'Rapid bactericidal killing against MRSA, MSSA, Streptococcus pyogenes, and vancomycin-susceptible Enterococcus faecalis.', '100% bioavailable intravenously; infused over 30 minutes.', 'Extensive tissue distribution into skin and blister fluid; 93% bound to human plasma proteins (predominantly albumin).', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism; not a substrate, inhibitor, or inducer of CYP450 enzymes.', 'Excreted unchanged in urine (approx. 33%) and faeces (approx. 20%), with remainder eliminated through slow non-enzymatic pathways.', '100% (IV)', '14.4 days (approx. 346 hours - ultra-long terminal half-life)', '93%', 'Peak serum level immediately at end of 30-minute IV infusion', '14 days per single dose', '[''Acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI) caused by MRSA, Streptococcus species, and Enterococcus faecalis'', ''Off-label use in osteomyelitis, joint infections, and infective endocarditis requiring outpatient therapy'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to dalbavancin or glycopeptides'']', '["Infusion-related reactions (flushing, rash, pruritus - slow infusion to 30 minutes or longer to avoid ''red-man'' like syndrome)", ''Dose reduction required in severe renal impairment'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Rash'', ''Infusion site irritation'']', '[''Hypersensitivity / anaphylactoid reactions'', ''Hepatotoxicity (ALT elevations >3x ULN)'', ''Pseudomembranous colitis'']', '[''No major CYP-mediated drug interactions'', ''Concomitant nephrotoxic drugs (monitor renal function)'']', '[''Renal function tests (eGFR)'', ''Liver function tests'', ''Clinical signs of infusion reactions'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk to fetus.', 'Excreted in animal milk; use caution during breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients from birth for ABSSSI (single-dose weight-adjusted regimen).', 'No dose adjustment based on age alone; assess renal function.', 'In chronic renal impairment with eGFR < 30 mL/min not on regular hemodialysis, reduce single dose to 1000 mg (or 750 mg followed by 375 mg on day 8); no adjustment needed on regular scheduled hemodialysis.', 'No dose adjustment needed in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Entire course delivered in a single 1500 mg infusion (or 1000 mg day 1 + 500 mg day 8), eliminating compliance failure and PICC lines'', ''14-day half-life revolutionizes outpatient parenteral antibiotic therapy (OPAT)'']', '[''Extremely long half-life means adverse reactions cannot be quickly washed out'', ''High initial acquisition cost'']', '[''A single 1500 mg IV infusion covers 14 full days of therapy for MRSA skin infections'', ''Infuse over at least 30 minutes to avoid flushing and itching'']', 'DALBA-vancin: DAYS AND WEEKS in blood (14-day half-life), Done in A Single Dose.', '-vancin', 'dal-ba-VAN-sin', 'डाल्बा-वैन्सिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('daptomycin', 'Daptomycin', 'Daptomycin', 'Daptomycin', 'Daptomycin', 'Antibiotics', 'A cyclic lipopeptide bactericidal antibiotic that binds to and depolarizes bacterial cell membranes, used for MRSA bacteremia, right-sided infective endocarditis, and severe skin infections.', '[''350 mg vial'', ''500 mg vial'']', '[''Lyophilized Powder for Injection'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Binds in a calcium-dependent manner to bacterial cell membranes, oligomerizes, and inserts into the lipid bilayer, creating pores that cause rapid potassium ion efflux, membrane depolarization, and cessation of DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis without cell lysis.', 'Rapid concentration-dependent bactericidal action against MRSA, MSSA, VRE (Enterococcus faecalis and faecium), and coagulase-negative staphylococci; inactivated by pulmonary surfactant, therefore INEFFECTIVE for pneumonia.', '100% bioavailable intravenously; given as a 30-minute IV infusion or 2-minute IV injection.', 'Distributes mainly into extracellular space (Vd approx. 0.1 L/kg); 90-93% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Little to no hepatic metabolism; does not interact with cytochrome P450 enzymes.', 'Excreted primarily unchanged by the kidneys (approx. 78% in urine via glomerular filtration); approx. 6% in faeces.', '100% (IV)', '8 to 9 hours', '90% to 93%', 'Rapid peak serum concentration at end of 30-minute infusion', '24 hours (administered once daily)', '[''Staphylococcus aureus bloodstream infections (bacteremia) and right-sided infective endocarditis'', ''Complicated skin and soft tissue infections caused by MRSA and Enterococcus faecalis'', ''Vancomycin-resistant enterococcal (VRE) bacteremia (off-label high-dose)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to daptomycin'', ''Treatment of pneumonia (inactivated by alveolar surfactant)'']', '[''STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in pneumonia: pulmonary surfactant binds and inactivates daptomycin'', ''Myopathy and rhabdomyolysis: monitor serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) weekly'', ''Eosinophilic pneumonia: rare, can present 2-4 weeks into therapy with fever, hypoxemia, and pulmonary infiltrates'']', '[''Elevated CPK levels'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Constipation'']', '[''Rhabdomyolysis with acute kidney injury'', ''Eosinophilic pneumonia (requires immediate cessation and corticosteroids)'', ''Peripheral neuropathy'', ''Clostridioides difficile colitis'']', '[''HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins - temporarily suspend during daptomycin to prevent additive rhabdomyolysis)'', ''Tobramycin / aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity)'']', '[''Serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) at baseline and at least weekly'', ''Renal function (BUN and creatinine)'', ''Monitor for new fever, dyspnea, or cough (eosinophilic pneumonia)'']', 'Category B; use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 1 year and older for bacteremia and skin infections.', 'Renal function declines with age; monitor CPK and creatinine clearance closely.', 'Dose adjustment required if CrCl < 30 mL/min (including hemodialysis): administer dose every 48 hours instead of every 24 hours.', 'No dose adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment.', '[''Rapid bactericidal killing against MRSA without bacterial lysis (reduced toxin release)'', ''Once-daily dosing with 2-minute bolus option'', ''Gold standard bactericidal alternative to vancomycin for MRSA endocarditis and bacteremia'']', '[''Cannot be used for pneumonia (inactivated by surfactant)'', ''Risk of skeletal muscle toxicity requires weekly CPK monitoring; statins must be held'']', '[''Never use for pneumonia because lung surfactant inactivates the drug'', ''Stop statins and check CPK weekly to prevent rhabdomyolysis'']', 'DAPTO-mycin: DEPOLARIZES membranes, DO NOT use in Pneumonia, DANGER to muscles (CPK).', '-mycin', 'DAP-toe-mye-sin', 'डैप्टो-माइसिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('voriconazole', 'Voriconazole', 'Voriconazole', 'Voriconazole', 'Voriconazole', 'Antifungals', 'A second-generation broad-spectrum triazole antifungal agent, established as the first-line treatment of choice for invasive aspergillosis.', '[''50 mg'', ''200 mg'', ''200 mg IV vial'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Powder for Solution for Infusion'', ''Oral Suspension'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Inhibits fungal cytochrome P450-dependent 14-alpha-sterol demethylase, blocking the conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol, leading to accumulation of toxic 14-methylated sterols and fungal cell membrane lysis.', 'Fungicidal against Aspergillus species, Scedosporium, and Fusarium; fungistatic against Candida species (including fluconazole-resistant C. krusei and C. glabrata).', 'Rapidly and almost completely absorbed (approx. 96% oral bioavailability); food decreases absorption, so take 1 hour before or 1 hour after meals.', 'Widely distributed into tissues and CSF (CSF levels 50% of plasma levels); 58% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized by hepatic CYP2C19 (subject to genetic polymorphism), CYP2C9, and CYP3A4; exhibits non-linear saturation pharmacokinetics in adults.', 'Less than 2% excreted unchanged in urine; approximately 80% eliminated in urine as inactive metabolites and 20% in faeces.', 'Approximately 96%', '6 hours (dose-dependent due to saturable metabolism)', '58%', 'IV loading dose (6 mg/kg q12h x 2 doses) achieves steady state within 24 hours', '12 hours', '[''Invasive aspergillosis (first-line treatment of choice)'', ''Candidemia in non-neutropenic patients and disseminated Candida infections'', ''Invasive Scedosporium and Fusarium infections'', ''Esophageal candidiasis refractory to fluconazole'', ''Antifungal prophylaxis in high-risk allogeneic stem cell transplant recipients'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to voriconazole or other azoles'', "Co-administration with potent CYP3A4 inducers (rifampicin, carbamazepine, St. John''s wort)", ''Concurrent use of terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride, pimozide, or quinidine'']', '[''Visual disturbances (photopsia, altered color perception, blurred vision) occur in approx. 30% of patients within 30 minutes of dosing'', ''Hepatotoxicity: monitor LFTs at baseline and regularly during therapy'', ''Phototoxicity and risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the skin with prolonged therapy'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) strongly recommended (target trough 1.5 to 5.0 mcg/mL)'']', '[''Transient visual disturbances (photopsia / blurred vision)'', ''Fever'', ''Nausea'', ''Maculopapular rash'', ''Elevated ALT/AST'']', '[''Severe hepatotoxicity / fulminant hepatic failure'', ''QTc interval prolongation / Torsades de pointes'', ''Squamous cell skin carcinoma'', ''Fluorosis and periostitis (in prolonged therapy)'', ''Hallucinations / neurotoxicity'']', '[''Potent CYP3A4/CYP2C19 substrate and inhibitor: greatly increases levels of tacrolimus, cyclosporine, sirolimus (sirolimus contraindicated)'', ''Warfarin (substantially increases INR)'', ''Statins (rhabdomyolysis risk)'', ''Omeprazole (doubles omeprazole levels)'']', '[''Therapeutic drug monitoring: trough concentration (trough target 1.5 - 5.0 mcg/mL)'', ''Liver function tests (baseline, weekly for 1 month, then monthly)'', ''Visual function checks'', ''Skin examination for sun sensitivity and premalignant lesions'']', 'Category D; embryotoxic and teratogenic in animal models; avoid during pregnancy unless life-saving.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; discontinue breastfeeding during therapy.', 'Approved for children 2 years and older; clearance is faster in children, requiring higher mg/kg weight-based dosing.', 'No dose adjustment based on age alone; assess hepatic and cardiac parameters.', 'Oral tablet requires no renal adjustment; IV formulation contains sulfobutyl ether beta-cyclodextrin (SBECD) which accumulates in renal impairment (switch to oral if CrCl < 50 mL/min).', 'Standard loading dose, but reduce maintenance dose by 50% in mild to moderate hepatic cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A and B).', '[''First-line drug of choice for invasive aspergillosis with superior survival over amphotericin B'', ''Near 100% oral bioavailability allows seamless IV to oral step-down'', ''Excellent penetration into brain tissue and CSF for fungal CNS infections'']', '[''Non-linear saturable pharmacokinetics require frequent therapeutic drug monitoring'', ''Photosensitivity and skin cancer risk with long-term use'']', '[''First-line drug of choice for invasive aspergillosis'', ''Counsel patients about temporary visual brightness or color changes that resolve within 30 minutes'', ''Monitor trough levels (target 1.5 - 5.0 mcg/mL)'']', 'VORI-conazole: Visual disturbances, Overcomes aspergillus, Requires In-depth TDM.', '-conazole', 'vor-i-KON-a-zole', 'वोरिकॉना-ज़ोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('posaconazole', 'Posaconazole', 'Posaconazole', 'Posaconazole', 'Posaconazole', 'Antifungals', 'An extended-spectrum triazole antifungal with potent activity against Aspergillus, Candida, and Mucorales (zygomycetes), widely used for prophylaxis in hematological malignancy and salvage therapy.', '[''100 mg delayed-release tablet'', ''300 mg/16.7 mL IV vial'', ''40 mg/mL oral suspension'']', '[''Delayed-Release Tablet'', ''Solution for Infusion'', ''Oral Suspension'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Inhibits fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, disrupting ergosterol synthesis and destabilizing the fungal cell membrane.', 'Broad spectrum covering Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus, Fusarium, and uniquely Mucorales (Rhizopus, Mucor, Lichtheimia).', 'Delayed-release tablets provide consistent, pH-independent absorption (much superior to oral suspension); bioavailability enhanced when taken with food.', 'Very large volume of distribution (approx. 5 to 25 L/kg); penetrates well into pulmonary alveolar cells and tissues; >98% bound to albumin.', 'Predominantly undergoes phase II UDP-glucuronidation; minimal hepatic CYP-mediated oxidation.', 'Excreted mainly in faeces (approx. 77%, mostly unchanged) and urine (approx. 14% as glucuronides).', 'High with delayed-release tablets (>80% with food); erratic with oral suspension', '26 to 35 hours (enables once-daily maintenance dosing with delayed-release tablets)', '>98%', 'Loading dose on day 1 (300 mg BID) achieves steady-state faster', '24 hours', '[''Prophylaxis of invasive fungal infections in patients with AML/MDS receiving induction chemotherapy'', ''Prophylaxis of invasive fungal infections in allogeneic HSCT recipients with graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)'', ''Treatment of invasive aspergillosis refractory to or intolerant of first-line agents'', ''Treatment of mucormycosis (second-line or oral step-down after amphotericin B)'', ''Oropharyngeal candidiasis refractory to itraconazole or fluconazole'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to posaconazole or other azoles'', ''Concurrent administration of CYP3A4 substrates that prolong QTc (ergot alkaloids, terfenadine, pimozide)'']', '[''QTc prolongation and cardiac arrhythmias'', ''Hepatotoxicity: monitor liver function tests'', ''Delayed-release tablets and oral suspension are NOT interchangeable due to differing dosing and bioavailability'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Pyrexia'', ''Hypokalemia'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe hepatotoxicity / jaundice'', ''Torsades de pointes / QTc prolongation'', ''Pseudohyperaldosteronism (hypertension and hypokalemia) in high doses'']', '[''Potent CYP3A4 inhibitor: increases blood concentrations of cyclosporine, tacrolimus, and sirolimus (mandatory dose reductions and monitoring)'', ''Midazolam, statins, vincristine (neurotoxicity risk with vincristine)'']', '[''Therapeutic drug monitoring (target trough >0.7 mcg/mL for prophylaxis; >1.0-1.5 mcg/mL for treatment)'', ''Liver function tests'', ''Serum electrolytes (potassium and magnesium)'', ''ECG for QTc interval'']', 'Category C; use only when benefit justifies potential risk to fetus.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 2 years and older for prophylaxis and treatment (delayed-release tablets).', 'No dose adjustment based on age alone.', 'No dose adjustment required for oral tablets in renal failure; IV formulation contains betadex sulfobutyl ether sodium which accumulates in moderate to severe renal impairment (eGFR < 50 mL/min).', 'Use caution; monitor liver enzymes.', '[''Broader spectrum than voriconazole, covering Mucorales (zygomycosis)'', ''Delayed-release tablet formulation allows once-daily maintenance dosing'', ''Proven survival benefit in antifungal prophylaxis for AML/GVHD'']', '[''Oral suspension and delayed-release tablets have different dosing and cannot be swapped milligram-for-milligram'', ''Potent CYP3A4 inhibition necessitates close immunosuppressant dose adjustment'']', '[''Delayed-release tablets are preferred over suspension due to predictable absorption'', ''Active against Mucorales fungi unlike voriconazole'']', 'POSA-conazole: Protective against Opportunistic Sickness and Aspergillosis, Plus Zygomycetes.', '-conazole', 'poe-sa-KON-a-zole', 'पोसाकॉना-ज़ोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('caspofungin', 'Caspofungin', 'Caspofungin', 'Caspofungin acetate', 'Caspofungin acetate', 'Antifungals', 'A first-in-class parenteral echinocandin antifungal that inhibits fungal cell wall synthesis, used for invasive candidiasis, esophageal candidiasis, and salvage therapy for invasive aspergillosis.', '[''50 mg vial'', ''70 mg vial'']', '[''Lyophilized Powder for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Non-competitively inhibits beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase, an enzyme absent in mammalian cells, preventing synthesis of beta-(1,3)-D-glucan in the fungal cell wall, causing osmotic fragility and lysis.', 'Concentration-dependent fungicidal activity against Candida species (including azole-resistant C. glabrata and C. krusei); fungistatic against Aspergillus species; inactive against Cryptococcus and Mucorales.', 'Not absorbed from gastrointestinal tract; administered solely as a 1-hour intravenous infusion.', 'Extensive tissue distribution into liver, kidneys, and spleen; minimal CSF and ocular penetration; 97% bound to human albumin.', 'Slowly metabolized by hydrolysis and N-acetylation in the liver to inactive peptide breakdown products; not cleared by CYP450 enzymes.', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 35%) and urine (approx. 41%, with <2% unchanged).', '100% (IV)', '9 to 11 hours (beta phase); terminal half-life 40 to 50 hours', '97%', '70 mg loading dose on day 1 achieves steady state rapidly, followed by 50 mg once daily', '24 hours', '[''Invasive candidiasis and candidemia in neutropenic and non-neutropenic patients'', ''Empirical therapy for presumed fungal infections in febrile neutropenic patients'', ''Esophageal candidiasis'', ''Salvage therapy for invasive aspergillosis in patients refractory to or intolerant of voriconazole or amphotericin B'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to caspofungin or other echinocandins'']', '[''Hepatic dysfunction: monitor liver enzymes during therapy'', ''Dose adjustment required in moderate hepatic impairment'', ''Histamine-medi