('colchicine', 'Colchicine', 'Colchicine', 'Colchicine', 'Colchicine', 'Gout', 'An alkaloid derived from Colchicum autumnale that disrupts microtubule polymerization, selectively inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis in acute gout and Mediterranean fever.', '[''0.5 mg'', ''0.6 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds to tubulin dimers, preventing microtubule assembly; suppresses neutrophil extravasation, degranulation, and inflammasome activation (NLRP3) triggered by monosodium urate crystals.', 'Dramatic anti-inflammatory effect in acute gout flares within 24-48 hours; does not alter serum uric acid levels directly.', 'Rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; peak plasma concentrations reached within 0.5 to 2 hours.', 'High concentration in leukocytes, liver, spleen, and kidneys; volume of distribution is very high (5-8 L/kg).', 'Partially metabolised in the liver by cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4); substrate of P-glycoprotein (P-gp).', 'Approximately 40-65% excreted unchanged in urine; bile and faecal excretion account for the remainder.', 'Approximately 45%', '20 to 40 hours (prolonged in elderly and renal impairment)', '39% to 50%', '12 to 24 hours after oral dose', 'Anti-inflammatory effect lasts 48 to 72 hours', '[''Acute gouty arthritis flare'', ''Prophylaxis of gout flares during initiation of urate-lowering therapy'', ''Familial Mediterranean fever (FMF)'', ''Recurrent pericarditis'']', '[''Severe renal impairment (eGFR < 10 mL/min) when combined with P-gp or CYP3A4 inhibitors'', ''Severe hepatic failure with concomitant CYP3A4 inhibitors'']', '[''Narrow therapeutic index: toxicity can be fatal'', ''Gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhoea, vomiting) often signal early toxicity'', ''Neuromyopathy with prolonged use'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Abdominal cramping'']', '[''Myelosuppression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia)'', ''Rhabdomyolysis and myopathy (especially with statins or renal impairment)'', ''Multiorgan failure in overdose'']',