('febuxostat', 'Febuxostat', 'Febuxostat', 'Febuxostat', 'Febuxostat', 'Gout', 'A potent non-purine selective inhibitor of xanthine oxidase, providing significant serum uric acid reduction for chronic gout without hypersensitivity cross-reactivity to allopurinol.', '[''40 mg'', ''80 mg'', ''120 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Potently and selectively inhibits both the oxidized and reduced forms of xanthine oxidase by blocking the pterin-binding channel, decreasing uric acid production.', 'Causes a rapid and sustained decrease in serum urate levels; unlike allopurinol, it does not inhibit other purine or pyrimidine metabolic enzymes.', 'Rapidly absorbed (>84%) after oral administration; peak plasma levels reached in 1 to 1.5 hours.', 'Apparent volume of distribution is 50 L; 99.2% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by glucuronidation via UGT enzymes and oxidation via CYP1A2, 2C8, 2C9.', 'Approximately 49% excreted in urine (chiefly as metabolites) and 45% in faeces.', 'High (over 80%)', '5 to 8 hours', '99.2%', 'Serum uric acid drops within 2 weeks of initiation', '24 hours', '[''Chronic hyperuricaemia in patients with gout'', ''Gout patients intolerant to or unresponsive to allopurinol'', ''Hyperuricaemia associated with cancer chemotherapy (tumour lysis syndrome)'']', '[''Concomitant use with azathioprine or mercaptopurine (severe bone marrow toxicity)'', ''Hypersensitivity to febuxostat'']', '[''Gout flare prophylaxis (colchicine or NSAID) recommended when starting therapy'', ''Cardiovascular safety monitoring (caution in severe pre-existing ischaemic heart disease)'', ''Hepatic failure reports'']', '[''Liver function test abnormalities'', ''Nausea'', ''Joint pain / acute gout flare during initiation'', ''Skin rash'', ''Diarrhoea'']', '[''Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (DRESS, SJS)'', ''Cardiovascular thrombotic events'', ''Hepatic failure'']', '[''Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine (contraindicated; marked increase in thiop