('thiocolchicoside', 'Thiocolchicoside', 'Thiocolchicoside', 'Thiocolchicoside', 'Thiocolchicoside', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A semi-synthetic sulfur derivative of colchicoside with selective GABAA and glycinergic receptor agonist properties, widely prescribed in India for acute painful muscle spasm.', '[''4 mg'', ''8 mg'', ''4 mg/2 mL injection'', ''0.25% ointment'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'', ''Ointment'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Topical'']', 'Selective agonist at GABA-A and strychnine-sensitive glycine receptors in the spinal cord and brainstem; decreases motor neuron excitability without curare-like muscle paralysis.', 'Relieves acute muscle hypertonia, rigidity, and reflex contractures associated with acute spinal and musculoskeletal pathology.', 'Rapidly absorbed after oral intake; undergoes extensive intestinal and hepatic first-pass metabolism to active aglycone metabolites (SL59.0955).', 'Active metabolite binds moderately to serum proteins (approx. 13%); distributes into central nervous system tissues.', 'Metabolized primarily to aglycone metabolite SL59.0955 and glucuronyl derivative SL18.0740.', 'Mainly eliminated through faeces (approx. 79%) via bile, with minimal renal elimination.', 'Approximately 25% for the parent drug due to first-pass metabolism', 'Approximately 7 to 8 hours for the active aglycone metabolite', 'Low (13%)', '1 to 2 hours (oral); 30 to 45 minutes (IM)', '6 to 8 hours', '[''Acute painful muscle spasm'', ''Lumbago and acute low back pain'', ''Cervicobrachial neuralgia'', ''Post-traumatic torticollis'']', '[''Pregnancy and lactation'', ''Women of childbearing potential not using effective contraception'', ''Hypersensitivity to colchicine derivatives'', ''Flaccid paralysis'']', '[''Aneuploidy risk: Metabolite SL59.0955 can cause aneuploidy in dividing cells; limit oral duration to a maximum of 7 consecutive days'', ''Do not exceed maximum daily dose (16 mg/day orally)'', ''May lower seizure threshold in epileptic patients'']', '[''Somnolence'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Gastralgia'', ''Nausea'', ''Vasovagal syncope (with IM injection)'']', '[''Seizures in susceptible individuals'', ''Severe allergic reactions / angioedema'', ''Aneuploidy / teratogenic risk'']', '[''CNS depressants (additive sedation)'', ''Other muscle relaxants'', ''Antiepileptic medications (potential seizure provocation)'']', '[''Duration of therapy (strictly ≤7 days oral, ≤5 days IM)'', ''Signs of hypersensitivity'', ''Gastrointestinal tolerance'']', 'Contraindicated; experimental data demonstrate chromosomal damage and embryo-fetal toxicity.', 'Contraindicated; passes into breast milk.', 'Contraindicated in children and adolescents under 16 years of age.', 'Use with caution due to increased risk of somnolence, dizziness, and fall risk.', 'No major renal dosage adjustment, but exercise clinical caution.', 'Use with caution in patients with hepatic impairment.', '[''Does not produce peripheral muscular weakness or paralysis'', ''Widely combined with NSAIDs like aceclofenac in India for acute back sprains'']', '[''Strict 7-day treatment duration limit due to genotoxicity concerns'', ''Risk of inducing seizures in epilepsy patients'']', '[''Strictly limit use to maximum 7 days orally (8 mg twice daily max)'', ''Contraindicated in women of childbearing potential without effective contraception'']', 'THIO-colchicoside: TIGHT muscles relax; limit to 7 days due to aneuploidy risk.', '-colchicoside', 'thee-oh-KOL-chi-koh-side', 'थायो-कोलचिको-साइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('baclofen', 'Baclofen', 'Baclofen', 'Baclofen', 'Baclofen', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A centrally acting GABA-B receptor agonist that suppresses monosynaptic and polysynaptic spinal reflexes, relieving spasticity resulting from multiple sclerosis and spinal cord lesions.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''20 mg'', ''0.05 mg/mL intrathecal'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Intrathecal Solution'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intrathecal'']', 'Specific agonist at presynaptic and postsynaptic GABA-B receptors in the spinal cord; inhibits calcium influx, reducing the release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, aspartate).', 'Reduces muscle tone, painful flexor and extensor spasms, and clonus; improves passive range of motion and functional mobility in chronic spastic states.', 'Rapidly and near-completely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; peak plasma concentrations in 1.5 to 2 hours.', 'Crosses the blood-brain barrier in modest concentrations; 30% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Only about 15% undergoes hepatic deamination; mostly excreted unchanged.', 'Approximately 70-80% excreted unchanged in urine within 72 hours; remainder excreted in faeces.', '70% to 85%', '3 to 4 hours in plasma; cerebrospinal fluid clearance is slower', '30%', 'Oral effect evident within 1 to 2 hours; optimal effect achieved with gradual dose titration', '6 to 8 hours', '[''Spasticity from multiple sclerosis'', ''Spinal cord injury and myelopathy'', ''Cerebral palsy'', ''Trigeminal neuralgia (second-line)'', ''Hiccups / intractable singultus (off-label)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to baclofen'', ''Active peptic ulcer disease'', ''Intrathecal formulation contraindicated in systemic infection'']', '[''Severe withdrawal syndrome: abrupt cessation can cause hyperpyrexia, profound spasticity, hallucinations, seizures, and death'', ''Sedation and impaired motor coordination'', ''Renal impairment requires immediate dose reduction to avoid neurotoxicity'']', '[''Sedation / drowsiness'', ''Dizziness'', ''Muscular weakness / hy