('chlorzoxazone', 'Chlorzoxazone', 'Chlorzoxazone', 'Chlorzoxazone', 'Chlorzoxazone', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A centrally acting muscle relaxant used as an adjunct in acute painful musculoskeletal conditions, acting primarily at spinal and subcortical levels.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Inhibits multisynaptic reflex arcs involved in producing and maintaining skeletal muscle spasm at spinal cord and subcortical areas.', 'Relieves painful muscle spasm, increases mobility, and reduces discomfort in acute strains, sprains, and backaches.', 'Readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; peak plasma concentrations reached within 1 to 2 hours.', 'Widely distributed throughout body tissues; crosses placenta.', 'Rapidly metabolised in the liver, primarily by CYP2E1, to 6-hydroxychlorzoxazone, which is then glucuronidated.', 'Almost entirely excreted in urine as glucuronide conjugates within 24 hours.', 'High (over 70%)', '1.1 hours', 'Moderate', 'Within 30 to 60 minutes', '3 to 4 hours', '[''Acute painful musculoskeletal conditions'', ''Muscle sprains and strains'', ''Lumbago and fibrositis (adjunct to rest and physical therapy)'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to chlorzoxazone'', ''Pre-existing hepatic disease'']', '[''Serious and sometimes fatal hepatotoxicity has been reported; discontinue immediately if jaundice or dark urine appears'', ''Urine discoloration (orange to purple-red) is harmless but may alarm patients'']', '[''Drowsiness'', ''Dizziness'', ''Lightheadedness'', ''Gastrointestinal distress'', ''Orange or reddish urine discoloration'']', '[''Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity / acute liver necrosis'', ''Anaphylactic reactions'', ''Angioneurotic edema'']', '[''Alcohol (additive CNS depression and potential CYP2E1-mediated hepatotoxicity)'', ''Other CNS depressants'', ''Sedatives and opioids'']', '[''Liver enzymes if used beyond brief acute course'', ''Clinical symptoms of liver dysfunction'']', 'Use only if clearly needed and benefi