holinergic drugs'']', '[''Sedation level'', ''Heart rate and ECG if history of cardiac risk'', ''Signs of anticholinergic toxicity'']', 'Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excretion in human milk is likely; use with caution.', 'Not recommended in children under 15 years.', 'Significantly increased risk of falls, delirium, and urinary retention; avoid or use lowest dose (5 mg).', 'No specific dose adjustments required.', 'Use caution and lower dose (5 mg) in mild hepatic impairment; avoid in moderate to severe impairment.', '[''Effective for severe acute back spasm when insomnia and pain co-exist'', ''Convenient once-daily extended-release formulation'']', '[''Prominent sedation and next-day grogginess'', ''Anticholinergic side effects like dry mouth and constipation'']', '[''Limit treatment duration to 2-3 weeks'', ''Avoid driving due to significant drowsiness'']', 'CYCLO-benzaprine: Related to tri-CYCLIC antidepressants; causes dry mouth and sleepiness.', '-prine', 'sye-kloe-BEN-za-preen', 'साइक्लो-बेंज़ा-प्रीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('nefopam', 'Nefopam', 'Nefopam', 'Nefopam hydrochloride', 'Nefopam hydrochloride', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A non-opioid, non-NSAID centrally acting analgesic that inhibits monoamine reuptake and modulates voltage-sensitive calcium/sodium channels, devoid of respiratory depression.', '[''30 mg'', ''20 mg/mL injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Ampoule for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'']', 'Triple monoamine reuptake inhibitor (serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine) and inhibitor of voltage-gated calcium/sodium channels in central nociceptive pathways.', 'Produces analgesia comparable to mild-to-moderate opioids without binding to opioid receptors; does not cause respiratory depression, sedation, or constipation.', 'Rapidly absorbed from the GI tract; peak plasma levels in 1 to 2 hours.', 'Crosses the blood-brain barrier readily; 73% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolised in the liver by N-demethylation to active desmethylnefopam.', 'Approximately 87% eliminated in urine (mostly as metabolites) and 8% in faeces.', 'Approximately 40%', '3 to 5 hours', '73%', '30 minutes (oral); 15 minutes (IV/IM)', '4 to 6 hours', '[''Moderate-to-severe acute post-operative pain'', ''Acute trauma and orthopaedic pain'', ''Chronic neuropathic and musculoskeletal pain (multimodal regimen)'']', '[''History of convulsive disorders or epilepsy'', ''Concurrent use of MAO inhibitors'', ''Urinary retention / closed-angle glaucoma'']', '[''Sympathomimetic effects: tachycardia, palpitations, and hypertension'', ''Convulsions have occurred in overdose or predisposed individuals'', ''Anticholinergic effects'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Dry mouth'', ''Tachycardia'', ''Sweating / hyperhidrosis'', ''Insomnia / agitation'']', '[''Convulsions / status epilepticus'', ''Cardiac arrhythmias'', ''Severe hypertension'']', '[''MAO inhibitors (contraindicated)'', ''Tricyclic antidepressants and sympathomimetics (additive cardiac excitation)'', ''Anticholinergic drugs'']', '[''Heart rate and blood pressure'', ''Signs of agitation or tremor'', ''Pain scores'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient clinical safety data.', 'Excreted in breast milk; use not recommended.', 'Not recommended in children under 12 years.', 'Start with lowest dose; greater risk of tachycardia and confusion.', 'Use with caution in renal disease; metabolites cleared by kidneys.', 'Use with caution in impaired liver function.', '[''Non-opioid and non-NSAID: avoids respiratory depression, GI bleeding, and renal injury'', ''Useful component of multimodal post-operative analgesia'']', '[''Can cause tachycardia, sweating, and nausea'', ''Contraindicated in patients with a seizure history'']', '[''Unique non-opioid, non-NSAID central painkiller'', ''Does not suppress breathing or cause constipation'']', 'NEFO-pam: NEither Narcotic nOr NSAID.', '', 'NEF-oh-pam', 'नेफ़ो-पैम', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('buprenorphine', 'Buprenorphine', 'Buprenorphine', 'Buprenorphine hydrochloride', 'Buprenorphine hydrochloride', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A high-affinity partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors and antagonist at kappa receptors, with a ceiling effect on respiratory depression, used for chronic pain and opioid dependence.', '[''0.2 mg sublingual'', ''2 mg'', ''8 mg'', ''5 mcg/hr patch'', ''10 mcg/hr patch'', ''20 mcg/hr patch'', ''0.3 mg/mL injection'']', '[''Sublingual Tablet'', ''Transdermal Patch'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Sublingual'', ''Transdermal'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors with very high affinity and slow receptor dissociation rate; antagonist at kappa-opioid and ORL-1 receptors.', 'Potent analgesia with a safer respiratory depression ceiling compared to full mu-agonists; high affinity displaces other opioids, precipitating withdrawal if given too soon.', 'Poor oral bioavailability due to high first-pass metabolism; excellent sublingual and transdermal absorption.', 'Extensively distributed into tissues; 96% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Hepatic N-dealkylation via CYP3A4 to active norbuprenorphine, followed by glucuronidation.', 'Approximately 70% excreted in faeces via biliary elimination, and 30% in urine.', '30% to 50% (sublingual); 15% (transdermal delivery rate determines blood levels)', '24 to 48 hours (sublingual); up to 30 hours after patch removal', '96%', '30 minutes (sublingual); transdermal patch takes 12-24 hours to reach effective levels', '6 to 8 hours (sublingual); 7 days (transdermal patch)', '[''Moderate to severe chronic pain (e.g., cancer pain, severe osteoarthritis)'', ''Opioid dependence maintenance and detoxification'', ''Acute post-operative pain'']', '[''Severe respiratory depression'', ''Severe hepatic impairment (for sublingual combination products)'', ''Acute intoxication with alcohol or CNS depressants'']', '[''May precipitate acute withdrawal in opioid-dependent patients if administered while full agonists occupy receptors'', ''Risk of neonatal abstinence syndrome in pregnancy'', ''High affinity makes naloxone reversal more difficult'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Nausea'', ''Headache'', ''Sedation'', ''Application site erythema (patches)'']', '[''Respiratory depression (especially when combined with benzodiazepines)'', ''Hepatotoxicity'', ''Severe precipitated opioid withdrawal'']', '[''Benzodiazepines (fatal respiratory arrest risk)'', ''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, ritonavir)'', ''Full opioid agonists (antagonism/precipitated withdrawal)'']', '[''Respiratory status'', ''Liver function tests'', ''Signs of misuse or diversion'', ''Patch adhesion and skin integrity'']', 'Used as a standard maintenance therapy for opioid-dependent pregnant women; risk of neonatal abstinence.', 'Excreted in human milk in low amounts; acceptable under medical supervision for maintenance.', 'Not recommended for chronic pain in young children.', 'Preferred opioid in renal impairment due to primary biliary/faecal excretion.', 'Safe in renal impairment without dosage adjustment due to biliary elimination route.', 'Dose reduction needed in moderate to severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Ceiling effect on respiratory depression increases safety margin'', ''7-day transdermal patch provides excellent compliance for cancer pain'', ''Safe in chronic kidney disease and dialysis patients'']', '[''Slow dissociation makes naloxone reversal slower in overdose'', ''Can precipitate withdrawal if given too soon after full agonists'']', '[''Transdermal patches last 7 days: rotate patch application sites'', ''Safe in renal disease because it is excreted through bile/faeces'']', 'BUP-renorphine: Biliary clearance (safe in kidneys), Unique Partial agonist with a ceiling on respiratory arrest.', '-norphone', 'byoo-pre-NOR-feen', 'ब्यूप्रे-नॉर्फ़ीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('fentanyl', 'Fentanyl', 'Fentanyl', 'Fentanyl citrate', 'Fentanyl citrate', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A potent synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist (50-100 times more potent than morphine) utilized for anaesthesia, breakthrough cancer pain, and transdermal chronic pain management.', '[''12 mcg/hr patch'', ''25 mcg/hr patch'', ''50 mcg/hr patch'', ''100 mcg/hr patch'', ''50 mcg/mL injection'', ''100 mcg lozenge'']', '[''Transdermal Patch'', ''Solution for Injection'', ''Buccal Lozenge / Film'']', '[''Transdermal'', ''Intravenous'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Buccal'', ''Sublingual'']', 'Selective and potent agonist at mu-opioid receptors in the central nervous system; inhibits adenylate cyclase, hyperpolarizes neurons, and reduces nociceptive transmission.', 'Produces profound analgesia, sedation, and miosis; rapid onset and short duration parenterally due to high lipophilicity and rapid redistribution.', 'Rapid absorption buccally; transdermal patches form a subcutaneous depot with steady delivery over 72 hours.', 'Extremely lipophilic; rapid distribution into brain and fat depots; 80-85% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Rapid and extensive hepatic N-dealkylation via CYP3A4 to inactive norfentanyl.', 'Approximately 75% excreted in urine (mostly as inactive metabolites; <10% unchanged) and 9% in faeces.', '92% (transdermal); 50% (buccal); negligible oral due to first-pass breakdown', '2 to 4 hours (IV); 17 to 24 hours (transdermal patch elimination half-life after removal)', '80% to 85%', 'Immediate to 2 minutes (IV); 12 to 24 hours for full transdermal patch effect', '30 to 60 minutes (IV bolus); 72 hours (transdermal patch)', '[''Chronic severe cancer pain in opioid-tolerant patients (patches)'', ''Surgical anaesthesia and analgesia'', ''Breakthrough cancer pain (transmucosal)'']', '[''Opioid non-tolerant patients (transdermal patch fatal risk)'', ''Acute or post-operative pain in non-tolerant individuals'', ''Severe respiratory depression or acute severe bronchial asthma'', ''Paralytic ileus'']', '[''Transdermal patches are strictly for opioid-tolerant patients only'', ''External heat (fever, heating pads, saunas) drastically accelerates absorption and causes fatal overdose'', ''High potential for respiratory arrest and death'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Nausea'', ''Somnolence'', ''Pruritus'', ''Hypoventilation'', ''Application site erythema'']', '[''Fatal respiratory depression'', ''Severe bradycardia and hypotension'', "Chest wall rigidity (''wooden chest'') with rapid IV bolus", ''Addiction and dependence'']', '[''CYP3A4 inhibitors (clarithromycin, ritonavir, ketoconazole - increases fentanyl toxicity)'', ''Benzodiazepines and other CNS depressants'', ''MAO inhibitors'']', '[''Respiratory rate and depth'', ''Consciousness level'', ''Pain relief and bowel function'', ''Proper patch disposal (fold sticky sides together)'']', 'Prolonged use can cause neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome; use during labor can cause neonatal respiratory depression.', 'Excreted in breast milk; monitor infant closely for respiratory depression and sedation.', 'Patches restricted to opioid-tolerant pediatric cancer patients under strict specialist protocol.', 'Significantly reduced clearance; lower starting doses and vigilant monitoring.', 'Relatively safe in renal failure compared to morphine as metabolites are inactive.', 'Dose reduction necessary in moderate to severe hepatic impairment.', '[''50-100x potency of morphine with minimal histamine release and less hypotension'', ''72-hour transdermal delivery provides consistent pain control'', ''No active neurotoxic metabolites (safe in renal failure)'']', '[''Fatal if used in opioid-naive patients'', ''External heat causes sudden toxic dumping into bloodstream'']', '[''Transdermal patch is changed every 72 hours; never apply direct heat (hot baths, heating pads)'', ''Strictly for opioid-tolerant patients only'']', 'FEN-tanyl: FAST acting, 50x morphine strength, Fatal if heated or given to opioid-naive.', '-fentanil', 'FEN-ta-nil', 'फेन्टा-निल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('oxycodone', 'Oxycodone', 'Oxycodone', 'Oxycodone hydrochloride', 'Oxycodone hydrochloride', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A semi-synthetic pure mu-opioid receptor agonist with high oral bioavailability, used in immediate- and controlled-release formulations for severe pain.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''20 mg'', ''40 mg'', ''80 mg CR'']', '[''Immediate-Release Tablet'', ''Controlled-Release Tablet'', ''Oral Solution'']', '[''Oral'', ''Subcutaneous'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Full agonist at mu-opioid receptors with some affinity for kappa- and delta-opioid receptors; inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opening potassium channels and blocking presynaptic calcium influx.', 'Provides potent analgesia, anxiolysis, and sedation; approximately 1.5 times more potent than oral morphine.', 'High oral bioavailability compared to morphine; food does not significantly affect absorption.', 'Apparent volume of distribution 2.6 L/kg; 45% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 to noroxycodone (major, weak) and CYP2D6 to oxymorphone (potent).', 'Excreted via the kidneys as free and conjugated metabolites; approximately 8% excreted unchanged.', '60% to 87% (significantly higher oral bioavailability than morphine)', '3 to 4.5 hours (IR); 4.5 to 6.5 hours (CR)', '45%', '20 to 30 minutes (IR); 1 hour (CR)', '3 to 6 hours (IR); 12 hours (CR)', '[''Moderate to severe acute post-operative pain'', ''Severe chronic cancer pain'', ''Chronic non-malignant pain unresponsive to non-opioids'']', '[''Significant respiratory depression'', ''Acute or severe bronchial asthma'', ''Paralytic ileus or GI obstruction'', ''Known hypersensitivity to oxycodone'']', '[''High risk of addiction, abuse, and misuse'', ''Accidental ingestion of CR tablets can be fatal, especially in children'', ''Never crush, chew, or dissolve CR tablets'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Nausea'', ''Somnolence'', ''Dizziness'', ''Pruritus'', ''Vomiting'']', '[''Life-threatening respiratory depression'', ''Severe hypotension / syncope'', ''Adrenal insufficiency'', ''Opioid-induced bowel obstruction'']', '[''CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin) raise oxycodone levels'', ''Benzodiazepines and alcohol (fatal sedation and respiratory arrest)'', ''CYP3A4 inducers (rifampicin, carbamazepine) reduce efficacy'']', '[''Respiratory rate and oxygen saturation'', ''Bowel function (proactive laxative regimen)'', ''Pain severity and sedation score'']', 'Prolonged use during pregnancy can lead to neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome; avoid unless essential.', 'Excreted in human milk; avoid during breastfeeding due to infant respiratory depression risk.', 'Controlled-release approved only in opioid-tolerant children 11 years and older with chronic cancer pain.', 'Increased plasma levels; start with lower doses and titrate slowly.', 'Metabolites accumulate in renal failure; dose reduction required.', 'Elimination impaired in hepatic disease; start at 1/3 to 1/2 of normal dose.', '[''Superior oral bioavailability compared to oral morphine'', ''Predictable response with both IR and 12-hourly CR formulations'']', '[''High potential for abuse and dependence'', ''CR tablets must never be crushed or chewed'']', '[''Do not crush, chew, or break controlled-release tablets'', ''Always initiate concurrent prophylactic bowel stimulant laxative'']', 'OXY-codone: Oral bioavailability is eXtra high compared to morphine.', '-codone', 'ox-ee-KOH-done', 'ऑक्सी-कोडोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('methadone', 'Methadone', 'Methadone', 'Methadone hydrochloride', 'Methadone hydrochloride', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A long-acting synthetic mu-opioid agonist and NMDA receptor antagonist used for opioid dependence maintenance and refractory neuropathic cancer pain.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''40 mg dispersible'', ''5 mg/5 mL oral solution'', ''10 mg/mL injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Dispersible Tablet'', ''Oral Solution'', ''Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Subcutaneous'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Agonist at mu-opioid receptors, antagonist at NMDA (N-methyl-D-aspartate) receptors, and inhibitor of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.', 'Suppresses opioid withdrawal symptoms without producing sharp euphoria peaks; NMDA antagonism prevents opioid tolerance and relieves neuropathic pain.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract; peak plasma concentrations in 2 to 4 hours.', 'Highly lipophilic with extensive tissue binding; volume of distribution 4-7 L/kg; 85-90% bound to plasma proteins (alpha-1 acid glycoprotein).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via CYP3A4, CYP2B6, and CYP2C19 to inactive EDDP.', 'Excreted in urine and bile as metabolites and unchanged drug; elimination shifts to faeces in renal impairment.', '70% to 85%', '15 to 60 hours (mean 24-36 hours; exhibits significant accumulation)', '85% to 90%', '30 to 60 minutes', '4 to 8 hours for single-dose analgesia; 24 to 48 hours for withdrawal suppression at steady state', '[''Opioid use disorder (maintenance and detoxification)'', ''Severe chronic pain / neuropathic cancer pain unresponsive to other opioids'']', '[''Severe respiratory depression'', ''Severe bronchial asthma'', ''Paralytic ileus'', ''Known QT prolongation or concurrent QT-prolonging drugs'']', '[''Cumulative toxicity: elimination half-life (up to 60 hours) far exceeds duration of analgesia (4-8 hours); repeat dosing can cause lethal accumulation'', ''QT prolongation and torsades de pointes'', ''High overdose mortality during titration'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Sweating'', ''Sedation'', ''Nausea'', ''Dizziness'', ''Weight gain'']', '[''Fatal respiratory arrest from drug accumulation'', ''QT interval prolongation and Torsades de pointes'', ''Severe bradycardia'']', '[''CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 inducers (rifampicin, efavirenz - precipitates withdrawal)'', ''CYP3A4 inhibitors (fluconazole, ciprofloxacin - raises methadone levels)'', ''QT-prolonging drugs'', ''Benzodiazepines'']', '[''Baseline and periodic 12-lead ECG for QTc interval'', ''Respiratory rate and sedation'', ''Adherence and urine drug screens in addiction programmes'']', 'Standard of care for opioid use disorder in pregnancy; prevents withdrawal complications, though neonatal abstinence syndrome may occur.', 'Excreted into breast milk; breastfeeding encouraged under medical supervision in stable maintenance.', 'Not recommended in pediatric patients.', 'Slower clearance; high risk of fatal accumulation. Titrate with utmost caution.', 'Safe in renal failure without dose reduction as fecal elimination compensates.', 'Use caution; liver metabolises the drug and severe impairment prolongs half-life.', '[''Inexpensive with once-daily dosing for opioid dependence'', ''Dual mu-agonist and NMDA-antagonist mechanism effective for neuropathic pain'', ''Safe in chronic kidney disease'']', '[''Prolonged variable half-life leads to accidental cumulative fatal overdose'', ''Causes dose-dependent QT prolongation and ventricular arrhythmias'']', '[''Analgesic duration (4-8 hours) is much shorter than elimination half-life (24-60 hours)'', ''Perform baseline ECG to monitor QTc interval'']', 'METHA-done: NMDA antagonism halts tolerance, but QTc prolongation must be monitored.', '-done', 'METH-a-done', 'मेथा-डोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('pentazocine', 'Pentazocine', 'Pentazocine', 'Pentazocine lactate', 'Pentazocine lactate', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A benzomorphan opioid with kappa-opioid agonist and weak mu-antagonist/partial agonist properties, frequently formulated with naloxone to prevent intravenous abuse.', '[''25 mg'', ''50 mg'', ''30 mg/mL injection'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intramuscular'', ''Intravenous'', ''Subcutaneous'']', 'Agonist at kappa-opioid receptors producing spinal analgesia and sedation; weak antagonist or partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors; binds sigma receptors.', 'Relieves moderate pain; kappa agonism produces dysphoria, psychotomimetic effects (hallucinations), and increases systemic vascular resistance and cardiac workload.', 'Well absorbed from the GI tract; undergoes substantial hepatic first-pass metabolism.', 'Widely distributed throughout the body; 60% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by oxidation and glucuronidation.', 'Excreted predominantly in urine as glucuronide conjugates; less than 10% unchanged.', 'Approximately 20% due to first-pass metabolism', '2 to 3 hours', '60%', '15 to 30 minutes (oral); 2 to 3 minutes (IV); 15 to 20 minutes (IM)', '3 to 4 hours', '[''Moderate to severe acute pain'', ''Post-operative surgical pain'', ''Pre-anaesthetic or pre-operative medication'']', '[''Acute myocardial infarction (increases cardiac workload and pulmonary artery pressure)'', ''Severe respiratory depression'', ''Head injury and increased intracranial pressure'', ''Patients dependent on pure mu-opioids (precipitates withdrawal)'']', '[''Increases myocardial oxygen consumption and pulmonary artery pressure'', ''Psychotomimetic reactions (hallucinations, disorientation, dysphoria due to kappa and sigma agonism)'', ''Severe tissue damage / sclerosis at injection sites with chronic parenteral use'']', '[''Sedation'', ''Nausea'', ''Dizziness'', ''Sweating'', ''Dysphoria / vivid dreams'']', '[''Respiratory depression'', ''Hallucinations and acute psychosis'', ''Injection site fibrosis and ulceration'', ''Precipitated withdrawal in mu-dependent individuals'']', '[''Pure mu-opioids (morphine, fentanyl - causes antagonism and sudden withdrawal)'', ''CNS depressants and alcohol'', ''MAO inhibitors'']', '[''Heart rate and blood pressure'', ''Respiratory rate'', ''Psychotomimetic signs or hallucinations'', ''Injection site inspection'']', 'Avoid during pregnancy; can precipitate withdrawal in dependent mothers and neonates.', 'Excreted into breast milk in small amounts; use caution.', 'Not recommended in children under 12 years of age.', 'Increased risk of confusion, hallucinations, and dysphoria.', 'Excreted by kidneys; reduce dose in renal impairment.', 'Clearance significantly reduced in hepatic disease; lower dose.', '[''Lower abuse potential than morphine due to kappa-mediated dysphoric effects at high doses'', ''Less biliary spasm than morphine'']', '[''Increases blood pressure and cardiac workload (contraindicated in acute MI)'', ''Frequent unpleasant hallucinations and nightmares'']', '[''Contraindicated in myocardial infarction because it increases pulmonary arterial pressure'', ''Precipitates sudden withdrawal if given to patients on regular morphine'']', 'PENTA-zocine: Pressure Elevates, Negative hallucinations (kappa agonist).', '-zocine', 'pen-TAZ-oh-seen', 'पेन्टाज़ो-सीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('hydrocodone', 'Hydrocodone', 'Hydrocodone', 'Hydrocodone bitartrate', 'Hydrocodone bitartrate', 'Pain & Inflammation', 'A semi-synthetic phenanthrene opioid mu-agonist with antitussive and analgesic actions, widely prescribed in combination with paracetamol for moderate to severe pain.', '[''5 mg'', ''7.5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''20 mg ER'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Extended-Release Tablet'', ''Oral Liquid'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Pure agonist at mu-opioid receptors; inhibits ascending pain pathways and suppresses the medullary cough reflex center.', 'Provides analgesia and suppresses severe non-productive cough; produces central sedation, euphoria, and respiratory depression at higher doses.', 'Well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; peak plasma concentrations reached in 1.3 hours.', 'Apparent volume of distribution is approximately 3.3 to 4.7 L/kg; 36% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Hepatic O-demethylation via CYP2D6 to hydromorphone (active, high potency) and N-demethylation via CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone.', 'Excreted primarily in urine as glucuronides and free drug within 24 hours.', 'High (over 70%)', '3.8 to 4.5 hours', '36%', '10 to 30 minutes', '4 to 6 hours (immediate release); 12 hours (ER)', '[''Moderate to severe acute and chronic pain'', ''Severe intractable non-productive cough in adults (antitussive)'']', '[''Severe respiratory depression'', ''Acute or severe bronchial asthma'', ''Paralytic ileus'', ''Concurrent MAO inhibitor use'']', '[''High risk of addiction, abuse, and dependence'', ''Often combined with paracetamol: risk of accidental paracetamol hepatotoxicity if exceeding 4000 mg/day'', ''Respiratory depression risk'']', '[''Constipation'', ''Nausea'', ''Lightheadedness'', ''Drowsiness'', ''Vomiting'']', '[''Fatal respiratory arrest'', ''Severe hypotension'', ''Paracetamol hepatotoxicity in combination overdoses'', ''Serotonin syndrome'']', '[''CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 inhibitors'', ''Benzodiazepines and alcohol (fatal respiratory arrest)'', ''Serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, SNRIs)'', ''Muscle relaxants'']', '[''Respiratory status'', ''Bowel movements (laxative co-prescription)'', ''Total daily paracetamol intake when using fixed-dose combinations'']', 'Prolonged maternal use causes neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome; avoid in pregnancy.', 'Hydrocodone and hydromorphone pass into breast milk; can cause infant sedation and respiratory arrest.', 'Not recommended in children; cough preparations strictly contraindicated under 18 in many jurisdictions.', 'Increased risk of falls, delirium, and severe constipation; initiate at lowest dose.', 'Metabolites cleared by kidneys; dose reduction required in renal disease.', 'Dose reduction necessary in moderate to severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Effective oral analgesic and potent antitussive'', ''Reliable oral absorption'']', '[''High potential for misuse and diversion'', ''Risk of unintentional paracetamol toxicity in combination formulations'']', '[''Check total paracetamol content when using combination pills to protect the liver'', ''Never exceed prescribed doses'']', 'HYDRO-codone: High-grade relief, Half-life ~4h, Halt cough.', '-codone', 'hye-droe-KOH-done', 'हाइड्रो-कोडोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('nebivolol', 'Nebivolol', 'Nebivolol Hydrochloride', 'Nebivolol hydrochloride', 'Nebivolol hydrochloride', 'Cardiovascular', 'A third-generation highly cardioselective beta-1 adrenergic blocker with nitric oxide-mediated endothelial vasodilation and neutral metabolic profile.', '[''2.5 mg'', ''5 mg'', ''10 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Dual mechanism: highly selective competitive beta-1 adrenergic antagonist, and stimulates endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) via beta-3 adrenergic receptors, releasing nitric oxide and causing peripheral vasodilation.', 'Reduces peripheral vascular resistance and blood pressure without reducing stroke volume; neutral effects on glucose and lipid metabolism; preserves erectile function.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1.5 to 4 hours; food does not affect absorption.', 'High volume of distribution (approx. 10 L/kg); 98% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Extensively metabolized in liver by CYP2D6 via aromatic hydroxylation and glucuronidation; active hydroxylated metabolites contribute to beta-blocking effect.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 38%) and faeces (approx. 48%) predominantly as metabolites.', '12% in extensive CYP2D6 metabolizers; 96% in poor metabolizers', '10 hours (extensive metabolizers); 32 hours (poor metabolizers)', '98%', '1 to 2 hours', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension'', ''Stable mild to moderate chronic heart failure (in addition to standard therapies in elderly patients ≥70 years)'', ''Hypertension with metabolic syndrome'']', '[''Severe bradycardia (<50 bpm)'', ''Second- or third-degree atrioventricular heart block'', ''Cardiogenic shock / decompensated heart failure'', ''Severe hepatic impairment'']', '[''Abrupt cessation may trigger rebound hypertension or myocardial infarction (taper over 1-2 weeks)'', ''May mask hypoglycemic tachycardia in diabetics'', ''Bronchospasm in severe reactive airway disease'']', '[''Headache'', ''Dizziness'', ''Fatigue'', ''Paresthesia'', ''Nausea'', ''Dyspnea'']', '[''Severe bradycardia / complete heart block'', ''Worsening heart failure'', ''Bronchospasm'', "Raynaud''s phenomenon"]', '[''CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine - increase nebivolol levels)'', ''Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem - severe bradycardia/AV block)'', ''Digoxin'', ''Clonidine'']', '[''Blood pressure and resting heart rate'', ''ECG for PR interval prolongation'', ''Blood glucose in diabetics'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; beta-blockers reduce placental perfusion and cause fetal bradycardia and growth restriction.', 'Excreted in breast milk; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in children.', 'Initiate with 2.5 mg daily in elderly patients; excellent tolerability in senior heart failure trials (SENIORS trial).', 'If severe renal impairment (eGFR < 30 mL/min), initiate with 2.5 mg once daily.', 'Contraindicated in moderate to severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Highest beta-1 cardioselectivity among beta-blockers'', ''Endothelial nitric oxide release promotes peripheral vasodilation'', ''Favorable metabolic profile (no worsening of insulin resistance or dyslipidemia)'', ''Significantly lower incidence of erectile dysfunction than other beta-blockers'']', '[''Variability in pharmacokinetics based on CYP2D6 genetic polymorphism'', ''Higher cost than atenolol or metoprolol'']', '[''Third-generation beta-blocker that dilates vessels by releasing nitric oxide'', ''Safest beta-blocker for male sexual function and metabolic syndrome'']', 'NEBI-volol: NO (Nitric Oxide) Endothelial Beta-blocker; Neutral on Insulin.', '-olol', 'ne-BIV-oh-lol', 'नेबिवो-लोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('labetalol', 'Labetalol', 'Labetalol Hydrochloride', 'Labetalol hydrochloride', 'Labetalol hydrochloride', 'Cardiovascular', 'A non-selective competitive beta-blocker with postsynaptic alpha-1 adrenergic blocking activity, established as the first-line drug of choice for hypertensive emergencies and hypertension in pregnancy.', '[''100 mg'', ''200 mg'', ''5 mg/mL (20 mL IV ampoule)'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'', ''Solution for Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Competitively blocks beta-1, beta-2, and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors; oral ratio of alpha:beta blockade is approx. 1:3; IV ratio is approx. 1:7; alpha-1 blockade reduces systemic vascular resistance while beta-1 blockade prevents reflex tachycardia.', 'Produces prompt fall in blood pressure with maintained cardiac output, renal blood flow, and cerebral blood flow, without marked reflex tachycardia.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed from GI tract; undergoes substantial hepatic first-pass metabolism.', 'Extensive tissue distribution; crosses placenta and blood-brain barrier; 50% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via glucuronidation to inactive metabolites.', 'Less than 5% excreted unchanged in urine; remainder eliminated as inactive glucuronides in urine (approx. 60%) and bile.', 'Approximately 25% (oral)', '6 to 8 hours', '50%', 'IV: 2 to 5 minutes; Oral: 20 minutes to 2 hours', 'IV: 2 to 4 hours; Oral: 8 to 12 hours', '[''Hypertensive emergencies and urgencies (IV bolus or continuous infusion)'', ''Hypertension in pregnancy (pre-eclampsia and chronic maternal hypertension - drug of choice)'', ''Acute aortic dissection (combined with vasodilator)'', ''Post-operative hypertension'']', '[''Severe sinus bradycardia'', ''Greater than first-degree heart block'', ''Cardiogenic shock / overt heart failure'', ''Bronchial asthma / severe COPD (beta-2 blockade)'']', '[''Postural / orthostatic hypotension (due to alpha-1 blockade - keep patients supine for 3 hours after IV injection)'', ''Hepatotoxicity: rare severe idiosyncratic liver injury reported'', ''May mask signs of hypoglycemia'']', '[''Dizziness / postural lightheadedness'', ''Scalp tingling / formication (characteristic alpha-blocker effect)'', ''Nausea'', ''Fatigue'', ''Nasal congestion'']', '[''Severe symptomatic postural hypotension'', ''Bradycardia / heart block'', ''Severe bronchospasm in asthmatics'', ''Drug-induced liver necrosis'']', '[''Verapamil / diltiazem (additive negative inotropic and dromotropic effects)'', ''Inhalation anaesthetics (myocardial depression)'', ''Cimetidine (increases labetalol bioavailability)'']', '[''Blood pressure and heart rate continuously during IV infusion'', ''Keep patient supine during and for 3 hours post-IV dose'', ''Liver function tests in prolonged oral therapy'']', 'Category C; drug of choice and first-line standard of care worldwide for gestational hypertension and pre-eclampsia.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Dosing established for pediatric hypertensive emergencies (0.2-1 mg/kg IV); use under specialist supervision.', 'Higher risk of orthostatic hypotension; start with 100 mg BID orally.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment.', 'Use with caution; titrate carefully due to reduced first-pass hepatic metabolism.', '[''Drug of choice for pregnancy-induced hypertension and pre-eclampsia'', ''Rapid, titratable IV blood pressure control in hypertensive emergencies without reflex tachycardia'', ''Preserves uteroplacental and cerebral blood flow'']', '[''Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension and characteristic scalp tingling'', ''Contraindicated in severe asthma and decompensated heart failure'']', '[''First-choice antihypertensive in pregnancy and pre-eclampsia'', ''IV drug of choice for hypertensive emergencies (give 20 mg slow IV bolus, double every 10 min)'']', 'LAB-ETA-LOL: Lowers Arterial Bloodpressure in Emergency & Tremendous in Pre-eclamps-IA.', '-lalol', 'la-BAY-ta-lol', 'लाबे-टालोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('esmolol', 'Esmolol', 'Esmolol Hydrochloride', 'Esmolol hydrochloride', 'Esmolol hydrochloride', 'Cardiovascular', 'An ultra-short-acting, cardioselective beta-1 adrenergic blocker metabolized rapidly by red blood cell esterases, used for rapid intraoperative and emergency control of supraventricular tachycardia and aortic dissection.', '[''100 mg/10 mL vial'', ''2.5 g/10 mL ampoule for infusion'']', '[''Solution for Injection'', ''Concentrate for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Selective competitive antagonist at myocardial beta-1 adrenergic receptors with negligible intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity; rapidly hydrolyzed by cytoplasmic esterases in red blood cells.', 'Rapidly slows sinus rate and AV nodal conduction velocity, decreasing myocardial contractility and oxygen consumption; effects dissipate within 15-20 minutes of stopping infusion.', 'Administered exclusively by continuous intravenous infusion; 100% bioavailable.', 'Rapidly distributes throughout the vascular compartment; Vd approx. 3.4 L/kg; 55% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Rapidly metabolized via hydrolysis of its methyl ester linkage by red blood cell cytosol esterases into an acid metabolite (ASL-8123) with 1/1500th of esmolol''s beta-blocking activity; completely independent of hepatic and renal enzymes.', 'Acid metabolite excreted by the kidneys in urine (>73%); less than 2% unchanged esmolol.', '100% (IV)', '9 minutes (ultra-short elimination half-life)', '55%', 'Immediate (peak effect within 2 to 6 minutes)', '10 to 20 minutes after stopping infusion', '[''Rapid control of ventricular rate in supraventricular tachycardia (atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter, sinus tachycardia)'', ''Perioperative and post-operative hypertension and tachycardia'', ''Acute aortic dissection (immediate beta-blockade before vasodilator therapy)'']', '[''Severe sinus bradycardia (<50 bpm)'', ''Heart block greater than first degree'', ''Cardiogenic shock / overt acute heart failure'', ''Severe asthma or acute bronchospasm'']', '[''Dose-dependent hypotension occurs frequently (titrate carefully and monitor BP continuously)'', ''Tissue necrosis upon extravasation; avoid infusion concentrations >10 mg/mL'', ''Rapid reversal allows safety: turning off infusion terminates effect within 15 minutes'']', '[''Hypotension (approx. 25% of patients)'', ''Diaphoresis'', ''Dizziness'', ''Nausea'', ''Injection site phlebitis'']', '[''Severe symptomatic hypotension / syncope'', ''Severe bradycardia / asystole'', ''Bronchospasm in susceptible patients'']', '[''IV verapamil / diltiazem (severe cardiac arrest/asystole risk; do not combine)'', ''Digoxin (additive AV nodal conduction delay)'', ''Morphine (increases esmolol plasma levels)'']', '[''Continuous blood pressure and ECG telemetry throughout infusion'', ''Infusion site inspection for extravasation'']', 'Category C; use in pregnancy only if maternal cardiac emergency demands it; crosses placenta.', 'Excretion in milk unknown; half-life is 9 minutes so maternal drug clears rapidly.', 'Used off-label in pediatric intensive care for tachyarrhythmias (loading 500 mcg/kg then 50-300 mcg/kg/min).', 'Elderly patients are more sensitive to hypotensive effects; titrate cautiously.', 'No dosage adjustment required for parent esmolol; acid metabolite accumulates in renal failure but is largely inactive.', 'No dosage adjustment required (metabolized exclusively by red blood cell esterases).', '[''Ultra-short 9-minute half-life provides unparalleled safety: if hypotension develops, stopping the infusion reverses the effect within 15 minutes'', ''Metabolism by red blood cell esterases is independent of liver and kidney function'', ''Ideal for unstable perioperative and ICU patients'']', '[''Intravenous infusion only; cannot be used for chronic maintenance'', ''High rate of infusion-related hypotension requires continuous arterial line or automated BP checks'']', '[''Ultra-short-acting beta-blocker with a 9-minute half-life metabolized by red blood cells'', ''Turn off infusion to reverse adverse effects within 15 minutes'']', 'ESMOLOL: Extremely Short-acting; Metabolized by Oxygen-carrier (RBC) esterases; Lasts Only minutes.', '-olol', 'EZ-moe-lol', 'एस्मो-लोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('perindopril', 'Perindopril', 'Perindopril Erbumine / Arginine', 'Perindopril erbumine', 'Perindopril erbumine', 'Cardiovascular', 'A long-acting, lipophilic prodrug ACE inhibitor that provides sustained 24-hour tissue ACE inhibition, extensive endothelial protection, and proven reduction of cardiovascular events in stable coronary artery disease.', '[''2 mg'', ''4 mg'', ''8 mg (erbumine)'', ''2.5 mg'', ''5 mg'', ''10 mg (arginine)'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Hydrolyzed in the liver to active metabolite perindoprilat, which competitively inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), blocking conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, decreasing aldosterone, and inhibiting bradykinin degradation.', 'Reduces peripheral vascular resistance without reflex tachycardia; promotes vascular remodeling and reduces left ventricular hypertrophy; enhances endothelial function via bradykinin-mediated nitric oxide release.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak perindopril concentration in 1 hour; 20% converted to active perindoprilat (peak perindoprilat in 3 to 4 hours); food decreases conversion to active metabolite, take before breakfast.', 'Widely distributed into vascular tissues and endothelial cells; perindoprilat is 20% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively hydrolyzed in the liver by carboxylesterases into active perindoprilat and inactive glucuronide conjugates.', 'Perindoprilat is eliminated unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration and tubular secretion.', 'Approximately 65-70% (prodrug)', 'Parent perindopril 1 hour; active perindoprilat effective half-life 17 hours (terminal dissociation half-life 30-120 hours)', '20%', '1 to 2 hours (peak effect in 4 to 6 hours)', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension'', ''Stable coronary artery disease (EUROPA trial: reduces risk of cardiac events)'', ''Symptomatic congestive heart failure'', ''Post-myocardial infarction secondary prevention'']', '[''History of ACE inhibitor-induced or hereditary angioedema'', ''Pregnancy (all trimesters - teratogenic)'', ''Concomitant use with aliskiren in diabetic patients'', ''Concomitant sacubitril/valsartan (must observe 36-hour washout)'']', '[''Black box warning: Fetal toxicity and neonatal death if used during pregnancy; discontinue immediately upon pregnancy detection'', ''Angioedema (face, lips, larynx - can be fatal)'', ''Hyperkalemia and acute renal impairment in bilateral renal artery stenosis'', ''Dry persistent hacking cough (bradykinin accumulation)'']', '[''Dry persistent cough'', ''Dizziness / lightheadedness'', ''Headache'', ''Fatigue'', ''Hyperkalemia'']', '[''Life-threatening angioedema of airway'', ''Acute kidney injury'', ''Severe symptomatic hypotension / syncope'', ''Agranulocytosis / neutropenia (rare)'']', '[''Potassium supplements / potassium-sparing diuretics (severe hyperkalemia)'', ''NSAIDs (impair antihypertensive effect and precipitate acute renal failure)'', ''Lithium (elevates serum lithium toxicity)'', ''Sacubitril (angioedema - 36h washout required)'']', '[''Serum creatinine and eGFR at baseline and within 1-2 weeks of initiation'', ''Serum potassium levels'', ''Blood pressure sitting and standing'']', 'Black box warning: Strictly contraindicated in pregnancy (fetotoxic, causes renal dysgenesis and oligohydramnios).', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in pediatric patients.', 'Start with 2 mg (erbumine) or 2.5 mg (arginine) in elderly patients; titrate based on renal function.', 'Dose reduction based on CrCl: if CrCl 30-60 mL/min, give 2 mg daily; if CrCl 15-30 mL/min, give 2 mg every other day; if CrCl < 15 mL/min, give 2 mg on dialysis days.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''EUROPA trial proven: significantly reduces cardiovascular death and myocardial infarction in stable coronary disease'', ''True 24-hour trough-to-peak ratio (>75%) ensuring consistent blood pressure control'', ''High tissue ACE lipophilicity improves vascular endothelial function'']', '[''Class-wide ACE inhibitor dry cough in 10-15% of patients'', ''Risk of angioedema and hyperkalemia'']', '[''Take once daily in the morning before breakfast'', ''Proven to reduce heart attacks in stable coronary artery disease (EUROPA trial)'']', 'PERINDO-pril: Proven Endothelial Remodeling In Narrowed Diseased vessels (EUROPA trial).', '-pril', 'per-IN-doe-pril', 'पेरिन्डो-प्रिल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('candesartan', 'Candesartan', 'Candesartan Cilexetil', 'Candesartan cilexetil', 'Candesartan cilexetil', 'Cardiovascular', 'An ester prodrug angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) with insurmountable, tight AT1-receptor binding kinetics, proven to reduce cardiovascular mortality and hospitalizations in heart failure (CHARM trial).', '[''4 mg'', ''8 mg'', ''16 mg'', ''32 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Completely bioactivated during gastrointestinal absorption to active candesartan, which binds selectively and insurmountably to the AT1 receptor with high affinity and slow dissociation, blocking vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion.', 'Reduces peripheral vascular resistance and systemic blood pressure without causing dry cough; improves cardiac output and reduces left ventricular remodelling in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).', 'Completely hydrolyzed to active candesartan in the gastrointestinal wall; peak plasma concentration in 3 to 4 hours; food does not affect bioavailability.', 'Highly bound to plasma albumin (>99%); small volume of distribution (approx. 0.13 L/kg).', 'Undergoes minor hepatic metabolism by CYP2C9 to an inactive metabolite; clearance is predominantly through direct excretion.', 'Eliminated by both renal excretion (approx. 33%) and biliary/fecal excretion (approx. 67%) predominantly as unchanged active candesartan.', 'Approximately 15% (cilexetil ester)', '9 to 12 hours (effective biological half-life longer due to tight receptor binding)', '>99%', '2 to 4 hours (full effect in 2 to 4 weeks)', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension in adults and children 1 to 17 years'', ''Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF - CHARM trial: reduces CV mortality and hospitalizations)'', ''Hypertensive diabetic nephropathy with microalbuminuria'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to candesartan'', ''Pregnancy (fetal toxicity)'', ''Concomitant use of aliskiren in patients with diabetes mellitus'']', '[''Black box warning: Fetal toxicity (contraindicated in pregnancy)'', ''Hyperkalemia, especially when combined with potassium-sparing diuretics or ACE inhibitors'', ''Hypotension in volume- or salt-depleted patients'', ''Renal impairment in bilateral renal artery stenosis'']', '[''Dizziness'', ''Headache'', ''Back pain'', ''Hyperkalemia'', ''Fatigue'']', '[''Acute renal failure'', ''Severe symptomatic hypotension'', ''Angioedema (rare compared to ACE inhibitors)'', ''Hyperkalemia (>5.5 mEq/L)'']', '[''Potassium supplements, spironolactone, eplerenone (additive hyperkalemia risk)'', ''NSAIDs (decrease antihypertensive effect and risk of acute kidney injury)'', ''Lithium (increases lithium levels)'', ''ACE inhibitors (dual RAS blockade increases toxicity)'']', '[''Serum creatinine and eGFR at baseline and within 1-2 weeks'', ''Serum potassium levels'', ''Blood pressure'']', 'Black box warning: Strictly contraindicated throughout pregnancy; causes fetal skull hypoplasia, oligohydramnios, and neonatal death.', 'Excreted in animal milk; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric hypertension in children 1 year and older; dosed by body weight.', 'Initiate at 4 mg once daily in frail elderly patients; titrate gradually.', 'No initial dose adjustment required in mild to moderate renal impairment; initiate at 4 mg in severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min).', 'Initiate at 2-4 mg once daily in moderate hepatic impairment; use with caution in biliary obstruction.', '[''Insurmountable AT1 receptor binding provides prolonged 24-hour blood pressure control even after missed doses'', ''Gold standard ARB for heart failure with proven mortality reduction (CHARM Alternative and Added trials)'', ''Virtually zero incidence of dry cough compared to ACE inhibitors'']', '[''Prodrug oral bioavailability is relatively modest (15%)'', ''Strictly contraindicated in pregnancy'']', '[''Proven heart failure mortality reduction (target dose 32 mg daily in heart failure)'', ''Does not cause the dry hacking cough seen with ACE inhibitors'']', 'CANDE-sartan: CAN Decrease Eventual mortality in heart failure (CHARM trial).', '-sartan', 'kan-de-SAR-tan', 'कैंडे-सारटन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('cilnidipine', 'Cilnidipine', 'Cilnidipine', 'Cilnidipine', 'Cilnidipine', 'Cardiovascular', 'A unique fourth-generation dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with dual L-type and N-type calcium channel blockade, suppressing sympathetic nerve activity and significantly reducing pedal oedema and proteinuria.', '[''5 mg'', ''10 mg'', ''20 mg'']', '[''Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Dual blockade: blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle cells (causing arterial vasodilation) AND N-type calcium channels at sympathetic nerve endings (inhibiting norepinephrine release and sympathetic overactivity).', 'Reduces peripheral vascular resistance and systemic blood pressure without causing reflex tachycardia; dilates both afferent and efferent renal arterioles, reducing glomerular capillary pressure and suppressing proteinuria; minimizes dependent pedal oedema by balancing capillary hydrostatic pressures.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1.8 to 2.2 hours; food delays peak but does not reduce overall bioavailability.', 'High volume of distribution; extensively bound to human plasma proteins (>98%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 via dehydrogenation and ester cleavage to inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted predominantly in faeces (approx. 80%) and urine (approx. 20%) as inactive metabolites.', 'Approximately 13% to 20%', '2.5 hours (plasma elimination); biological tissue duration exceeds 24 hours', '>98%', '1 to 2 hours', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension, particularly in patients with morning hypertension or sympathetic hyperactivity'', ''Hypertension with chronic kidney disease and proteinuria'', ''Hypertension complicated by amlodipine-induced pedal oedema (replacement therapy)'', ''White-coat hypertension and stress-induced blood pressure elevation'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to cilnidipine or other dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers'', ''Severe cardiogenic shock or unstable angina'', ''Severe aortic stenosis'']', '[''Hypotension and dizziness'', ''Hepatic impairment requires cautious dosing (cleared by CYP3A4)'', ''Gradually taper when discontinuing to prevent rebound hypertension'']', '[''Headache'', ''Flushing'', ''Dizziness'', ''Palpitations (much less than amlodipine)'', ''Nausea'']', '[''Severe symptomatic hypotension'', ''Excessive bradycardia (rare, due to N-type blockade in sinus node)'', ''Hepatotoxicity'']', '[''CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin - increase cilnidipine levels)'', ''CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin, phenytoin - reduce efficacy)'', ''Digoxin (modest increase in digoxin levels)'', ''Other antihypertensives'']', '[''Blood pressure and heart rate sitting and standing'', ''Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (monitors antiproteinuric effect)'', ''Inspection of ankles for resolution of pedal oedema'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; insufficient human safety data; use established pregnancy agents (labetalol, nifedipine).', 'Excreted in breast milk in animal studies; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in children.', 'Start with a low dose (5 mg once daily); assess blood pressure response carefully.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment; beneficial antiproteinuric action in chronic kidney disease.', 'Dose reduction recommended in moderate to severe hepatic impairment (metabolized by CYP3A4).', '[''Dual L- and N-type calcium channel blocker inhibits sympathetic hyperactivity, preventing reflex tachycardia'', ''Significantly lower incidence of ankle pedal oedema compared to amlodipine'', ''Dilates both afferent AND efferent renal arterioles, reducing intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria'']', '[''Not approved in the United States (extensively approved and prescribed in India and Japan)'', ''Metabolized by CYP3A4, leading to drug interactions with azoles and macrolides'']', '[''Drug of choice when patients develop swollen ankles (pedal oedema) on amlodipine'', ''Dual L-type and N-type channel blocker that protects kidneys and suppresses sympathetic spikes'']', 'CILNI-dipine: Calms Sympathetic Nerves (N-type), Cancels Leg Infiltration (no pedal oedema).', '-dipine', 'sil-NI-di-peen', 'सिलनी-डिपिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('benidipine', 'Benidipine', 'Benidipine Hydrochloride', 'Benidipine hydrochloride', 'Benidipine hydrochloride', 'Cardiovascular', 'A triple L-, T-, and N-type calcium channel blocker with high vascular membrane affinity, conferring long-lasting 24-hour antihypertensive, cardioprotective, and renoprotective effects with minimal pedal oedema.', '[''4 mg'', ''8 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Triple calcium channel blocker: blocks L-type channels (arteriolar dilation), T-type channels (aldosterone suppression and cardiac protective effect), and N-type channels (sympathetic nerve inhibition and efferent arteriolar dilation).', 'Slowly associates and dissociates from the dihydropyridine binding site within the cell membrane (membrane-bound reservoir), producing smooth, long-lasting blood pressure reduction without reflex tachycardia or renin-aldosterone activation.', 'Well absorbed from the GI tract; peak plasma concentrations reached within 1 to 1.5 hours; takes longer to dissociate from vascular receptors than from plasma.', 'Widely distributed into vascular tissue; highly bound to plasma proteins (>98%).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by cytochrome P450 3A4 to inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine and faeces almost entirely as inactive metabolites; negligible renal excretion of unchanged drug.', 'Approximately 15% to 25%', '1 to 2 hours in plasma; tissue receptor half-life >24 hours', '>98%', '1 hour', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension'', ''Renovascular hypertension and hypertension associated with chronic kidney disease'', ''Angina pectoris (vasospastic and effort angina)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to benidipine or dihydropyridines'', ''Severe cardiogenic shock'', ''Advanced aortic stenosis'']', '[''Severe hepatic impairment (hepatic clearance reduced)'', ''Hypotension during initiation in volume-depleted patients'']', '[''Headache'', ''Facial flushing'', ''Dizziness'', ''Palpitations'', ''Constipation'']', '[''Excessive hypotension'', ''Atrioventricular conduction block (rare)'', ''Drug-induced liver injury'']', '[''CYP3A4 inhibitors (itraconazole, erythromycin - elevate benidipine exposure)'', ''CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin - reduce efficacy)'', ''Other antihypertensives (additive hypotensive effect)'']', '[''Blood pressure and heart rate'', ''Liver function tests in hepatic impairment'', ''Renal parameters'']', 'Avoid during pregnancy; animal studies show teratogenicity and fetal distress.', 'Avoid during breastfeeding; excreted in animal milk.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in pediatric patients.', 'Initiate at 2 to 4 mg once daily in elderly patients; titrate cautiously.', 'No dosage adjustment required in renal impairment; renoprotective profile.', 'Start with 2 mg once daily in patients with hepatic dysfunction.', '[''Triple L-, T-, and N-type calcium channel blocker offering comprehensive vascular, renal, and cardiac protection'', ''Dilates efferent arterioles, reducing glomerular hypertension and proteinuria'', ''Membrane-reservoir kinetics guarantee smooth 24-hour blood pressure control'']', '[''Plasma half-life is short (requires high tissue affinity for extended action)'', ''Mainly available in Asian markets (India, Japan)'']', '[''Triple calcium channel blocker (L, T, and N types)'', ''Protects kidneys by dilating both afferent and efferent renal arterioles'']', 'BENI-dipine: Beneficial Triple blocker (L, T, N types) for Blood pressure, Efferent arterioles, and Nitrogenous kidney waste.', '-dipine', 'ben-I-di-peen', 'बेनी-डिपिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('eplerenone', 'Eplerenone', 'Eplerenone', 'Eplerenone', 'Eplerenone', 'Cardiovascular', 'A selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (MRA) with high specificity for aldosterone receptors, proven to reduce mortality in heart failure post-MI without the antiandrogenic side effects of spironolactone.', '[''25 mg'', ''50 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively binds to the mineralocorticoid receptor, competitively blocking aldosterone binding in the renal distal tubule and collecting duct; prevents sodium and water reabsorption while conserving potassium and magnesium; attenuates myocardial and vascular fibrosis.', 'Reduces blood pressure, decreases left ventricular remodelling, reduces myocardial fibrosis, and prevents sudden cardiac death in heart failure; possesses 1000-fold lower affinity for androgen and progesterone receptors than spironolactone (no gynecomastia).', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentrations achieved in approximately 1.5 to 2 hours; absorption unaffected by food.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 43 to 90 L; approximately 50% bound to plasma proteins (predominantly alpha-1 acid glycoprotein).', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver predominantly by CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites; no active circulating metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 67%) and faeces (approx. 32%) as inactive metabolites; less than 5% unchanged in urine.', 'Approximately 69%', '4 to 6 hours', '50%', 'Peak antihypertensive effect within 2 to 4 weeks', '24 hours', '[''Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) post-myocardial infarction (EPHESUS trial)'', ''Chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction NYHA class II (EMPHASIS-HF trial)'', ''Essential hypertension (resistant hypertension add-on)'']', '[''Serum potassium > 5.0 mEq/L at initiation'', ''Severe renal impairment (eGFR < 30 mL/min or serum creatinine >2.0 mg/dL in females, >2.5 mg/dL in males)'', ''Concurrent use with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin)'', ''Concurrent use with potassium-sparing diuretics or high-dose potassium supplements'']', '[''Hyperkalemia is the principal life-threatening risk; monitor serum potassium at baseline, 1 week, 1 month, and periodically'', ''Risk of hyperkalemia increased in patients with diabetes and moderate renal impairment'', ''Dose adjustment required with moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors'']', '[''Hyperkalemia'', ''Dizziness'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Cough'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe / fatal hyperkalemia (>6.0 mEq/L) causing cardiac arrest'', ''Acute kidney injury / elevation in serum creatinine'', ''Angioedema (rare)'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin - contraindicated due to 5-fold eplerenone increase)'', ''Moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (diltiazem, verapamil, fluconazole - do not exceed 25 mg daily)'', ''ACE inhibitors and ARBs (monitor potassium strictly)'', ''NSAIDs (precipitate acute renal failure)'']', '[''Serum potassium and serum creatinine at baseline, day 7, day 30, and every 3-6 months thereafter'', ''Blood pressure sitting and standing'']', 'Category B; use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; use caution or alternative.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in pediatric patients.', 'Higher risk of hyperkalemia due to age-related decline in renal function; initiate with 25 mg daily and monitor electrolytes closely.', 'Contraindicated if eGFR < 30 mL/min; if eGFR 30-49 mL/min, initiate with 25 mg every other day.', 'No dosage adjustment needed in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; avoid in severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Selective aldosterone antagonist that does NOT cause gynecomastia, breast pain, or menstrual irregularities (unlike spironolactone)'', ''Proven reduction in all-cause mortality and sudden cardiac death in post-MI heart failure (EPHESUS trial)'', ''Effective for resistant hypertension'']', '[''Significantly more expensive than generic spironolactone'', ''Requires strict monitoring of potassium and renal function to avoid fatal hyperkalemia'']', '[''No gynecomastia or breast pain (selective for aldosterone receptors, sparing androgen receptors)'', ''Check potassium at 1 week and 1 month after starting (stop if K+ > 5.5 mEq/L)'']', 'EPLEREN-one: Eliminates Progestational/androgenic Effects; Lowers End-organ Remodeling; check Potassium.', '-one', 'eh-PLEER-en-own', 'एप्लेरे-नोन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('indapamide', 'Indapamide', 'Indapamide', 'Indapamide', 'Indapamide', 'Cardiovascular', 'A non-thiazide chlorosulfamoyl indole diuretic with dual diuretic and direct vascular smooth muscle vasodilating properties, established in major trials (HYVET, ADVANCE) for stroke and mortality reduction.', '[''1.5 mg SR'', ''2.5 mg'']', '[''Sustained-Release Tablet'', ''Immediate-Release Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Dual action: inhibits sodium and chloride reabsorption in the early distal convoluted tubule (producing saluresis), AND directly reduces vascular reactivity to pressor amines by modulating transmembrane calcium influx in arterial smooth muscle cells.', 'Lowers peripheral vascular resistance with minimal impact on plasma volume at low doses (1.5 mg SR); metabolically neutral: does not adversely affect lipid profiles or glucose tolerance; reduces left ventricular hypertrophy.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed; 1.5 mg sustained-release formulation provides stable plateau concentrations over 24 hours.', 'Widely distributed into vascular tissue and erythrocytes; 76-79% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver (approx. 90%) to numerous inactive metabolites.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 60-70%, with only 5-7% unchanged) and faeces (approx. 20%).', 'Near 100%', '14 to 24 hours (sustained release)', '76% to 79%', '1 to 2 hours (peak antihypertensive effect in 2 to 4 weeks)', '24 hours', '[''Essential hypertension in adults'', ''Isolated systolic hypertension in the elderly (HYVET trial: reduced stroke and all-cause mortality)'', ''Hypertension in diabetic patients (ADVANCE trial: combined with perindopril)'']', '[''Severe renal failure (anuria)'', ''Hepatic encephalopathy / severe hepatic impairment'', ''Refractory hypokalemia'', ''Known hypersensitivity to indapamide or sulfonamides'']', '[''Hypokalemia and hyponatremia: check baseline and periodic serum electrolytes'', ''May precipitate or exacerbate hepatic encephalopathy in liver cirrhosis'', ''Hyperuricemia and gout precipitation'']', '[''Hypokalemia'', ''Fatigue'', ''Dizziness'', ''Headache'', ''Muscle cramps'', ''Nausea'']', '[''Severe life-threatening hypokalemia / cardiac arrhythmias'', ''Severe hyponatremia'', ''Hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhotics'', ''Stevens-Johnson syndrome'']', '[''Lithium (decreases lithium clearance, causing toxicity)'', ''QT-prolonging drugs (hypokalemia increases Torsades de pointes risk)'', ''NSAIDs (attenuate antihypertensive efficacy)'', ''Digoxin (hypokalemia potentiates digoxin toxicity)'']', '[''Serum electrolytes (potassium, sodium) at baseline and 4-6 weeks after initiation'', ''Serum uric acid and fasting glucose'', ''Blood pressure'']', 'Category B; diuretics are generally avoided in pregnancy due to risk of maternal hypovolemia and placental hypoperfusion.', 'Excreted in breast milk; breastfeeding not recommended.', 'Safety and efficacy not established in children.', 'First-line agent in elderly patients with isolated systolic hypertension (HYVET trial demonstrated 30% stroke reduction in patients ≥80 years).', 'Ineffective as a diuretic if eGFR < 30 mL/min, but vasodilating effect may persist; monitor electrolytes.', 'Contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment / hepatic encephalopathy.', '[''Proven to reduce stroke and all-cause mortality in the very elderly (HYVET trial)'', ''Metabolically neutral at 1.5 mg SR dose (no worsening of lipids or diabetes)'', ''Direct vasodilating action lowers blood pressure with minimal kaliuresis'']', '[''Sulfonamide cross-reactivity'', ''Risk of hypokalemia and hyponatremia if combined with other diuretics'']', '[''Proven stroke reduction in the very elderly (HYVET trial)'', ''Sustained-release 1.5 mg once daily taken in the morning'']', 'INDAP-amide: Invaluable in Nursing-home/Decade-8 patients (HYVET trial); Arterial Vasodilator.', '-amide', 'in-DAP-a-mide', 'इन्डापा-माइड', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('ivabradine', 'Ivabradine', 'Ivabradine Hydrochloride', 'Ivabradine hydrochloride', 'Ivabradine hydrochloride', 'Cardiovascular', 'A selective and specific hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated (HCN) pacemaker channel inhibitor that lowers heart rate without reducing myocardial contractility or blood pressure.', '[''5 mg'', ''7.5 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Selectively blocks the hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated (HCN) channel (If ''funny'' pacemaker current) in the sinoatrial node, slowing the diastolic depolarization slope and reducing heart rate at rest and during exercise.', 'Selectively reduces heart rate without affecting myocardial contractility (inotropy), intracardiac conduction (dromotropy), ventricular repolarization, or systemic blood pressure; decreases myocardial oxygen consumption and prolongs diastolic coronary perfusion time.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed; peak plasma concentration in approximately 1 hour on an empty stomach; food delays absorption by 1 hour and increases exposure by 20-30%, so take with meals.', 'Apparent volume of distribution approx. 100 L; 70% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver by N-demethylation to an active N-desmethylated metabolite (S-18982) which has equal potency.', 'Excreted equally in urine and faeces (approx. 4% unchanged in urine).', 'Approximately 40% (due to first-pass metabolism)', '2 hours (effective half-life in plasma); terminal half-life 11 hours', '70%', 'Heart rate reduction within 1 hour', '12 hours (dosed twice daily with meals)', '[''Chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF NYHA II-IV, LVEF ≤35%) in sinus rhythm with resting heart rate ≥70 bpm (SHIFT trial)'', ''Stable chronic angina pectoris in patients with normal sinus rhythm who have contraindications to or intolerance of beta-blockers'', ''Inappropriate sinus tachycardia (off-label)'']', '[''Resting heart rate < 70 bpm prior to treatment'', ''Cardiogenic shock / acute decompensated heart failure'', ''Acute myocardial infarction'', ''Severe hypotension (<90/50 mmHg)'', ''Severe hepatic impairment'', ''Sick sinus syndrome or sino-atrial block'', ''Third-degree AV block'', ''Pacemaker-dependent rhythm (ineffective)'', ''Atrial fibrillation or flutter (ineffective, requires sinus rhythm)'']', '[''Atrial fibrillation risk: monitor regularly for irregular rhythm (discontinue if permanent AF develops)'', ''Visual phosphenes (enhanced brightness in parts of visual field) occur in approx. 15% due to retinal Ih channel inhibition'', ''Avoid strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers'', ''Not indicated for acute angina attacks'']', '[''Phosphenes (transient visual luminous phenomena / brightness)'', ''Bradycardia (<50 bpm)'', ''Headache'', ''Dizziness'', ''Atrial fibrillation'']', '[''Severe symptomatic bradycardia'', ''Atrial fibrillation'', ''Ventricular arrhythmias / Torsades de pointes (secondary to profound bradycardia)'', ''Syncope'']', '[''Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin - contraindicated)'', ''Moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (diltiazem, verapamil - increase ivabradine levels and cause excessive bradycardia; avoid combination)'', "CYP3A4 inducers (rifampicin, St. John''s wort, phenytoin - decrease efficacy)", ''QT-prolonging drugs'']', '[''Resting heart rate (target 50 to 60 bpm in heart failure)'', ''Regular pulse check and ECG for atrial fibrillation'', ''Vision assessment for phosphenes'']', 'Strictly contraindicated during pregnancy; embryotoxic and teratogenic in animal studies; effective contraception required.', 'Excreted in animal milk; contraindicated during breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric heart failure (dilated cardiomyopathy) 6 months and older in sinus rhythm.', 'Initiate at 2.5 mg twice daily in patients aged 75 years or older.', 'No dose adjustment required if CrCl > 15 mL/min; use caution if CrCl < 15 mL/min.', 'Contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C); no adjustment in mild.', '[''Pure heart rate reduction without decreasing blood pressure or myocardial contractility (ideal in hypotensive heart failure)'', ''Reduces cardiovascular mortality and heart failure hospitalizations in patients with resting HR ≥70 bpm (SHIFT trial)'', ''Does not cause bronchospasm, depression, or erectile dysfunction (unlike beta-blockers)'']', '[''Only effective in patients with sinus rhythm (completely ineffective in atrial fibrillation)'', ''Visual phosphenes occur in 15% of patients due to retinal Ih channels'']', '[''Pure heart rate lowering drug that does NOT lower blood pressure'', ''Only works if patient is in sinus rhythm (ineffective in atrial fibrillation)'', ''Counsel patients about bright visual lights/flashes (phosphenes) in dim lighting'']', 'IVA-BRAD-ine: Inhibits ''funny'' (If) current, Valued in heart failure, Acts strictly on Bradycardia.', '-bradine', 'eye-VA-bra-deen', 'इवाब्रा-डीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('isavuconazole', 'Isavuconazole', 'Isavuconazole (Isavuconazonium Sulfate)', 'Isavuconazonium sulfate', 'Isavuconazonium sulfate', 'Antifungals', 'A novel water-soluble triazole prodrug with broad-spectrum activity against invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis, notable for causing QTc shortening rather than prolongation.', '[''100 mg'', ''200 mg vial'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Powder for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma esterases to active isavuconazole, which inhibits fungal 14-alpha-lanosterol demethylase, blocking ergosterol synthesis.', 'Fungicidal against Aspergillus species and Mucorales; fungistatic against Candida and Cryptococcus.', 'Near complete oral bioavailability (approx. 98%); unaffected by food or gastric pH.', 'Very high volume of distribution (approx. 450 L); >99% bound to plasma albumin.', 'Extensively metabolized by CYP3A4, CYP3A5, and subsequently glucuronidated.', 'Excreted in faeces (approx. 46%) and urine (approx. 46%) predominantly as metabolites; <1% unchanged in urine.', '98%', '130 hours (long half-life allows once-daily maintenance)', '>99%', 'Loading doses required over first 48 hours', '24 hours', '[''Invasive aspergillosis'', ''Invasive mucormycosis (zygomycosis)'', ''Candidemia in refractory patients'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to isavuconazole'', ''Familial short QT syndrome'', ''Concurrent administration of strong CYP3A4 inducers/inhibitors'']', '[''Shortens the QTc interval (dose-dependent QTc shortening, unique among azoles)'', ''Hepatotoxicity: monitor LFTs'', ''Infusion-related hypersensitivity'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'', ''Elevated liver enzymes'', ''Peripheral oedema'']', '[''Severe hepatotoxicity / hepatic failure'', ''Anaphylaxis'', ''Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SCAR)'']', '[''CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine - reduce levels drastically)'', ''Ketoconazole / high-dose CYP3A4 inhibitors'', ''Increases tacrolimus, cyclosporine, sirolimus exposure'']', '[''Liver function tests (baseline and weekly)'', ''ECG (assess QTc shortening)'', ''Therapeutic drug monitoring in complex cases'']', 'Avoid in pregnancy; embryotoxic in animal models.', 'Excreted in milk; avoid breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 1 year and older for aspergillosis and mucormycosis.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age.', 'No dosage adjustment needed in renal impairment (does not contain cyclodextrin).', 'No adjustment needed in mild to moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh A and B); halve maintenance in severe.', '[''Does not contain nephrotoxic cyclodextrin in IV formulation (safe in renal failure)'', ''Shortens rather than prolongs QTc interval'', ''Once-daily maintenance with 98% oral bioavailability'']', '[''Extremely high cost'', ''Long half-life means slow drug clearance if toxicity occurs'']', '[''First-line option for mucormycosis alongside amphotericin B'', ''Safe in renal impairment because IV formulation has no cyclodextrin'']', 'ISA-VU-conazole: Infuses Safely in Acute kidney failure (no cyclodextrin); Shortens QT.', '-conazole', 'eye-sa-voo-KON-a-zole', 'आइसवुकोना-ज़ोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('anidulafungin', 'Anidulafungin', 'Anidulafungin', 'Anidulafungin', 'Anidulafungin', 'Antifungals', 'A semi-synthetic echinocandin antifungal that undergoes non-enzymatic chemical degradation, making it completely independent of renal and hepatic metabolism.', '[''100 mg vial'']', '[''Powder for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Non-competitively inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, halting fungal cell wall synthesis and causing osmotic lysis.', 'Concentration-dependent fungicidal activity against Candida species (including C. glabrata and C. krusei); fungistatic against Aspergillus.', 'Administered exclusively by intravenous infusion; 100% bioavailable.', 'Extensively distributed into tissues; minimal CNS/ocular penetration; 84% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Undergoes slow non-enzymatic chemical degradation at physiological temperature and pH into an open-ring peptide; not metabolized by hepatic enzymes.', 'Less than 1% excreted in urine; degradation products excreted in faeces via biliary elimination.', '100% (IV)', '40 to 50 hours (terminal half-life)', '84%', '200 mg loading dose on day 1 achieves steady state rapidly', '24 hours', '[''Invasive candidiasis and candidemia in adult patients'', ''Esophageal candidiasis'', ''Intra-abdominal and peritoneal Candida infections'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to anidulafungin or echinocandins'']', '[''Histamine-mediated infusion reactions (flush, pruritus, dyspnea - infuse at <1.1 mg/min)'', ''Hepatic effects (monitor liver transaminases)'']', '[''Phlebitis'', ''Hypokalemia'', ''Nausea'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'', ''Elevated transaminases'']', '[''Severe anaphylaxis / shock'', ''Acute hepatic dysfunction'', ''Coagulopathy'']', '[''Remarkably free of drug-drug interactions; no interaction with cyclosporine, tacrolimus, or CYP substrates'']', '[''Liver function tests'', ''Serum potassium'', ''Injection site checks'']', 'Category C; use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.', 'Excreted in animal milk; use caution.', 'Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients established for candidemia (dosed by weight).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age.', 'Zero dose adjustment in any degree of renal impairment or dialysis (non-enzymatic degradation).', 'Zero dose adjustment in any degree of hepatic impairment (non-hepatic chemical clearance).', '[''Zero hepatic or renal clearance adjustments needed; uniquely cleared by chemical degradation'', ''No significant cytochrome P450 or drug interactions'']', '[''Parenteral only'', ''Inactive against Cryptococcus, Mucorales, and Fusarium'']', '[''Drug of choice for candidemia in severe liver or renal failure due to chemical clearance'', ''Requires 200 mg loading dose on Day 1, then 100 mg daily'']', 'ANIDULA-fungin: ANY-organ friendly; Non-enzymatic Degradation In Liver-less, Kidney-less clearance.', '-fungin', 'a-nye-doo-la-FUN-jin', 'अनिडुला-फ़ंजिन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('sertaconazole', 'Sertaconazole', 'Sertaconazole Nitrate', 'Sertaconazole nitrate', 'Sertaconazole nitrate', 'Antifungals', 'A topical broad-spectrum imidazole antifungal containing a unique benzothiophene ring, providing dual fungistatic and direct fungicidal action with potent anti-inflammatory and antipruritic properties.', '[''2% cream'', ''2% lotion'', ''2% dust powder'', ''500 mg vaginal suppository'']', '[''Topical Cream'', ''Topical Lotion'', ''Topical Powder'', ''Vaginal Tablet'']', '[''Topical'', ''Vaginal'']', 'Dual mechanism: at low concentrations, inhibits 14-alpha-demethylase blocking ergosterol synthesis; at higher concentrations, benzothiophene ring directly inserts into fungal plasma membrane, forming pores and causing immediate cell death.', 'Broad fungicidal activity against Trichophyton rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, Candida albicans, and Malassezia furfur; suppresses inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha).', 'Negligible systemic absorption after topical or vaginal administration.', 'Remains concentrated within the stratum corneum with cutaneous residence time exceeding 48 hours.', 'Minimal systemically absorbed drug is metabolized by hepatic oxidation.', 'Excreted in trace amounts in faeces and urine.', '<1% systemic bioavailability', 'Cutaneous retention half-life >48 hours', '>99%', 'Rapid reduction of pruritus within 48 to 72 hours', '24 hours (applied once or twice daily)', '["Tinea pedis (athlete''s foot)", ''Tinea cruris (jock itch)'', ''Tinea corporis (ringworm)'', ''Tinea versicolor'', ''Vulvovaginal candidiasis (500 mg single-dose ovule)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to sertaconazole, imidazole antifungals, or formulation components'']', '[''For topical or vaginal use only; avoid contact with eyes'', ''Discontinue if contact dermatitis or sensitization develops'']', '[''Application site erythema'', ''Mild burning sensation'', ''Dry skin'', ''Pruritus'']', '[''Severe allergic contact dermatitis'', ''Cutaneous blistering / vesiculation'']', '[''No clinically significant systemic drug interactions due to negligible absorption'']', '[''Clinical evaluation of fungal lesions and relief of itching'']', 'Category C; negligible absorption, but use with caution in pregnancy.', 'Safe on intact skin; avoid application to nipples before breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 12 years and older.', 'No dose adjustment required.', 'No dosage adjustment required (topical).', 'No dosage adjustment required (topical).', '[''Dual fungicidal mechanism via benzothiophene ring overcomes resistance'', ''Potent intrinsic anti-inflammatory and antipruritic effect provides rapid symptom relief'', ''High lipophilicity provides 48-hour cutaneous reservoir'']', '[''Topical formulation only; ineffective for systemic or deep onychomycosis'', ''Higher cost than generic clotrimazole'']', '[''Dual-action imidazole with built-in anti-inflammatory property'', ''Single-dose 500 mg ovule highly effective for vaginal candidiasis'']', 'SERTA-conazole: SOOTHES (anti-inflammatory) and STRIKES (fungicidal benzothiophene).', '-conazole', 'ser-ta-KON-a-zole', 'सर्टाकॉना-ज़ोल', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('amorolfine', 'Amorolfine', 'Amorolfine Hydrochloride', 'Amorolfine hydrochloride', 'Amorolfine hydrochloride', 'Antifungals', 'A morpholine antifungal nail lacquer and cream with a unique dual-enzyme inhibition mechanism in the ergosterol pathway, applied once or twice weekly for onychomycosis.', '[''5% nail lacquer'', ''0.25% cream'']', '[''Nail Lacquer'', ''Topical Cream'']', '[''Topical'']', 'Inhibits two separate fungal enzymes in the ergosterol biosynthesis pathway: delta-14-reductase and delta-7,delta-8-isomerase, depleting ergosterol and causing accumulation of toxic ignosterol.', 'Broad-spectrum fungicidal and fungistatic action against dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum), yeasts (Candida), and nondermatophyte moulds causing nail infections.', 'Nail lacquer forms a non-water-soluble film that penetrates nail plate deeply; systemic absorption is negligible (<1%).', 'Concentrates in the nail plate and underlying nail bed; remains active in nail for up to 14 days after application.', 'Trace amounts absorbed systemically are metabolized into polar metabolites.', 'Eliminated in trace amounts via urine and faeces.', '<1%', 'Retention in nail bed >14 days', 'High locally', 'Nail cure requires 6 months (fingernails) to 9-12 months (toenails)', '7 days (applied once weekly)', '[''Distal and lateral subungual onychomycosis (without matrix involvement)'', ''Tinea pedis, tinea cruris, and tinea corporis (cream formulation)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to amorolfine or lacquer ingredients'', ''Onychomycosis involving the nail matrix (lunula) requires oral therapy'']', '[''Nails should be filed down and cleansed with alcohol swabs before each application'', ''Avoid cosmetic nail varnish during treatment'', ''External nail use only'']', '[''Mild periungual burning'', ''Transient nail discoloration'', ''Dry brittle nails'', ''Erythema'']', '[''Severe contact dermatitis / paronychia (rare)'']', '[''No systemic drug interactions reported due to minimal absorption'']', '[''Monthly nail clipping and monitoring of clear nail growth from the proximal nail fold'']', 'Avoid during pregnancy due to lack of adequate human data.', 'Avoid applying to breast area; use with caution.', 'Not recommended for children under 18 years due to limited safety data.', 'No dose adjustment required.', 'No dosage adjustment required.', 'No dosage adjustment required.', '[''Once-weekly application improves patient compliance drastically over daily lacquers'', ''Dual-enzyme inhibition produces potent fungicidal action in the nail bed'', ''Zero systemic hepatotoxicity risk compared to oral terbinafine'']', '[''Treatment must be continued for 6 to 12 months until healthy nail regrows'', ''Ineffective if nail matrix/lunula is involved (>50% nail involvement)'']', '[''Apply once or twice weekly to affected fingernails (6 months) or toenails (9-12 months)'', ''Always file down the thickened nail surface before reapplying'']', 'AMOR-olfine: A Month of Regular filing Overcomes Rigid fungal nails.', '-olfine', 'a-MORE-ol-feen', 'अमोरोल-फ़ीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('griseofulvin', 'Griseofulvin', 'Griseofulvin', 'Griseofulvin microsize', 'Griseofulvin', 'Antifungals', 'An oral fungistatic antifungal that binds to fungal tubulin and concentrates in newly formed keratin, established as the gold standard for tinea capitis (scalp ringworm) in children.', '[''125 mg'', ''250 mg'', ''500 mg'', ''125 mg/5 mL suspension'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Oral Suspension'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Binds to fungal tubulin and disrupts the mitotic spindle apparatus, arresting fungal cells in metaphase; also binds to human keratin, rendering newly formed hair, skin, and nails resistant to fungal invasion.', 'Fungistatic against dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton); completely inactive against Candida, bacteria, and deep mycoses.', 'Variable and erratic absorption; microsize formulations improve absorption; absorption is doubled when taken with a high-fat meal (milk, cheese, butter).', 'Deposits preferentially in keratin precursor cells of the skin, hair shafts, and nails; binds firmly to keratin; 84% bound to plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver by 6-demethylation and glucuronidation to inactive 6-demethylgriseofulvin.', 'Excreted in urine (<1% unchanged), faeces (approx. 36%), and perspiration.', 'Approximately 50% fasting; significantly higher with fatty meals', '9 to 24 hours', '84%', 'Requires weeks to months of continuous therapy as infected keratin is shed', '24 hours', '[''Tinea capitis (scalp ringworm - gold standard therapy in children)'', ''Tinea barbae (beard infection)'', ''Extensive tinea corporis and cruris unresponsive to topical agents'', ''Tinea unguium (onychomycosis - alternative)'']', '[''Severe liver disease / hepatic failure'', ''Porphyria (induces ALA synthetase and exacerbates acute porphyria)'', ''Pregnancy (teratogenic / embryotoxic)'']', '[''Must be taken with fatty food to ensure adequate absorption'', ''Photosensitivity: advise sun protection'', ''Potential for Disulfiram-like ethanol reaction'']', '[''Headache (very common at treatment initiation)'', ''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Photosensitivity rash'']', '[''Hepatotoxicity / cholestasis'', ''Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SJS/TEN)'', ''Granulocytopenia / leukopenia'', ''Lupus-like syndrome'']', '[''Warfarin (griseofulvin induces CYP, decreasing anticoagulant effect)'', ''Oral contraceptives (decreased contraceptive efficacy; barrier methods required)'', ''Alcohol (flushing, tachycardia - disulfiram-like effect)'', ''Barbiturates (decrease griseofulvin absorption)'']', '[''Complete blood counts every 2 to 4 weeks'', ''Liver and renal function tests during prolonged therapy (>1 month)'']', 'Strictly contraindicated (Category X); teratogenic and aneuploidic; men must wait 6 months before fathering a child.', 'Contraindicated during breastfeeding; excretion in human milk unknown.', 'First-line drug of choice for pediatric tinea capitis (10-20 mg/kg/day for 6-8 weeks with milk).', 'No primary age-related adjustment; assess hepatic function.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild to moderate renal disease.', 'Strictly contraindicated in severe liver impairment; monitor LFTs.', '[''Gold standard agent for tinea capitis with decades of proven pediatric safety'', ''Concentrates selectively into keratinizing hair follicles and nails'']', '[''Requires prolonged courses (6-8 weeks for scalp, 6-12 months for nails)'', ''Multiple drug interactions and teratogenicity risk'']', '[''Always take with whole milk or fatty food for absorption'', ''First-choice oral treatment for scalp ringworm in school children'']', 'GRISEO-fulvin: Grows Into hair/nails; Requires Ingestion with Fat; Stops Spindle.', '-fulvin', 'gris-ee-oh-FUL-vin', 'ग्रिसियो-फ़ल्विन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('flucytosine', 'Flucytosine (5-FC)', 'Flucytosine', 'Flucytosine (5-fluorocytosine)', 'Flucytosine', 'Antifungals', 'A fluorinated pyrimidine antifungal that converts intracellularly to 5-fluorouracil, combined synergistically with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis.', '[''250 mg'', ''500 mg'', ''2.5 g/250 mL IV infusion'']', '[''Capsule'', ''Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Transported into fungal cells by cytosine permease and deaminated by fungal cytosine deaminase to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU); 5-FU inhibits thymidylate synthase and incorporates into fungal RNA, terminating protein and DNA synthesis.', 'Concentration-dependent fungicidal synergy with amphotericin B against Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida species; never use as monotherapy due to rapid resistance emergence.', 'Rapidly and well absorbed from GI tract (75-90% bioavailability); peak levels in 1 to 2 hours.', 'Exceptional tissue penetration; penetrates readily into CSF (CSF levels 70-85% of serum levels), aqueous humor, and joints; <4% protein bound.', 'Minimal hepatic metabolism (<5%); eliminated virtually entirely as active drug.', 'Excreted predominantly unchanged in urine via glomerular filtration (>90% within 24 hours).', '75% to 90%', '3 to 5 hours (prolonged to 25-100 hours in severe renal failure)', '<4%', '1 to 2 hours', '6 hours (administered every 6 hours)', '[''Cryptococcal meningitis in HIV/AIDS patients (induction phase combined with amphotericin B)'', ''Severe systemic candidiasis / endocarditis (in combination)'', ''Chromoblastomycosis'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to flucytosine'', ''Known complete dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) enzyme deficiency'']', '[''Black box warning: Bone marrow depression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, aplastic anaemia) occurs with peak levels >100 mcg/mL'', ''Strict therapeutic drug monitoring required (target peak 50 to 100 mcg/mL)'', ''Never administer as monotherapy (resistance develops within days)'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Vomiting'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Abdominal pain'', ''Rash'']', '[''Dose-dependent bone marrow suppression (leukopenia, agranulocytosis, thrombocytopenia)'', ''Hepatotoxicity'', ''Enterocolitis / bowel perforation'']', '[''Amphotericin B (synergistic, but amphotericin nephrotoxicity causes flucytosine accumulation)'', ''Zidovudine / ganciclovir (additive bone marrow suppression)'']', '[''Therapeutic drug monitoring: peak level measured 2 hours post-dose (maintain between 50-100 mcg/mL)'', ''Complete blood counts (platelets, WBC) twice weekly'', ''Renal function (BUN/creatinine) twice weekly'']', 'Category C; teratogenic in animal studies; use in life-threatening cryptococcal meningitis when benefits outweigh fetal risks.', 'Excretion in human milk unknown; discontinue breastfeeding.', 'Weight-based dosing in pediatric cryptococcal infections (100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses); monitor levels closely.', 'Renal clearance decreases with age; high risk of bone marrow suppression; adjust dose strictly.', 'Strict dose reduction mandatory: if CrCl 20-40 mL/min, dose q12h; if CrCl 10-20 mL/min, dose q24h; if CrCl < 10 mL/min, dose q48h or guide by levels.', 'Use with caution; monitor liver enzymes.', '[''Synergistic standard of care with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis, significantly reducing mortality'', ''Superb penetration into cerebrospinal fluid and brain tissue'']', '[''Severe myelosuppression if serum peak exceeds 100 mcg/mL'', ''Rapid emergence of resistance if used as single agent'']', '[''Always combine with amphotericin B; never use alone'', ''Check blood levels to prevent toxic bone marrow suppression'']', 'FLU-CYTOSINE: Fungal Cytosine Deaminase converts to 5-FU; Fatal to marrow if peak >100.', '-cytosine', 'floo-SYE-toe-seen', 'फ़्लू-साइटोसीन', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('remdesivir', 'Remdesivir', 'Remdesivir', 'Remdesivir', 'Remdesivir', 'Antivirals', 'A broad-spectrum monophosphoramidate adenosine nucleotide prodrug that halts viral RNA synthesis, used intravenously for COVID-19 in hospitalized and high-risk patients.', '[''100 mg vial'']', '[''Lyophilized Powder for Injection'', ''Concentrate for Solution for Infusion'']', '[''Intravenous'']', 'Metabolized intracellularly into active remdesivir triphosphate (GS-443902), which competes with ATP for incorporation by viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRp), causing delayed chain termination at position i+3.', 'Potent antiviral activity against Coronaviridae (SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV), Filoviridae (Ebola), and Paramyxoviridae.', 'Administered exclusively by intravenous infusion over 30 to 120 minutes.', 'Widely distributed into tissues; 88-93% bound to human plasma proteins.', 'Extensively metabolized by esterases and phosphoamidases intracellularly; minor CYP3A4 metabolism.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 74%, mostly as metabolite GS-441524) and faeces (approx. 18%).', '100% (IV)', 'Parent drug 1 hour; active intracellular triphosphate metabolite half-life 20 to 25 hours', '88% to 93%', 'Immediate upon IV infusion', '24 hours', '[''Hospitalized patients with moderate to severe COVID-19 requiring supplemental oxygen'', ''High-risk outpatients with mild-to-moderate COVID-19 within 7 days of symptom onset (3-day course)'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to remdesivir or formulation excipients (SBECD)'']', '[''Hepatotoxicity: monitor ALT/AST (discontinue if ALT increases to >10x ULN)'', ''Infusion-related reactions and anaphylaxis'', ''Renal evaluation: formulation contains sulfobutyl ether beta-cyclodextrin (SBECD)'']', '[''Nausea'', ''Elevated AST and ALT'', ''Headache'', ''Rash'', ''Prolonged prothrombin time'']', '[''Severe liver injury / transaminitis'', ''Anaphylactic shock / severe infusion reaction'', ''Sinus bradycardia / cardiac arrhythmias'']', '[''Chloroquine / hydroxychloroquine (antagonizes antiviral activity; co-administration not recommended)'', ''CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin - reduce remdesivir concentrations)'']', '[''Hepatic transaminases (ALT, AST) before initiation and daily during treatment'', ''Renal function tests (BUN / creatinine)'', ''Prothrombin time / INR'']', 'Use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk; reassuring safety registries in severe COVID-19.', 'Excreted in low levels in breast milk; compatible with breastfeeding.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 28 days of age and older weighing at least 3 kg.', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'Recent trial data confirms safety in patients with eGFR < 30 mL/min and on dialysis; monitor renal function.', 'Do not initiate if ALT ≥ 5x ULN; discontinue if ALT ≥ 10x ULN or accompanied by signs of liver inflammation.', '[''Proven reduction in time to clinical recovery in hospitalized COVID-19 patients'', ''Early 3-day outpatient administration reduces hospitalization by 87% in high-risk patients'']', '[''Parenteral IV only; no oral formulation'', ''Transient elevations in liver transaminases are frequent'']', '[''Initiate as early as possible in course of COVID-19 infection'', ''Standard course is 200 mg loading on day 1, followed by 100 mg daily for 5 days total'']', 'REM-desivir: RdRp Enzyme Mistake terminates viral RNA replication.', '-desivir', 'rem-DES-i-veer', 'रेमडे-सिविर', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('molnupiravir', 'Molnupiravir', 'Molnupiravir', 'Molnupiravir', 'Molnupiravir', 'Antivirals', 'An oral ribonucleoside prodrug that induces viral error catastrophe through lethal mutagenesis in viral RNA, used for mild-to-moderate COVID-19 in high-risk outpatients.', '[''200 mg'']', '[''Capsule'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Hydrolyzed in blood to N4-hydroxycytidine (NHC), phosphorylated intracellularly to NHC triphosphate, which is utilized by viral RdRp; mispairs with adenine and guanine, inducing massive catastrophic mutations during replication (''error catastrophe'').', 'Broad antiviral activity against SARS-CoV-2 and RNA respiratory viruses; prevents production of infectious viral progeny.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration of active NHC within 1.5 hours; food has minimal effect.', 'Vd of NHC is approximately 142 L; negligible plasma protein binding (0%).', 'Prodrug cleaved by host esterases to NHC; NHC metabolized by endogenous pyrimidine salvage pathways.', 'Cleared predominantly through metabolic elimination via pyrimidine pathways; minimal renal excretion.', 'Rapid systemic conversion to active NHC', 'Parent drug 1 hour; active metabolite NHC approx. 3.3 hours (intracellular half-life longer)', '0%', '1 to 2 hours', '12 hours (dosed twice daily for 5 days)', '[''Mild to moderate COVID-19 in adults with positive viral testing who are at high risk for progression to severe disease, when alternative approved options are unavailable'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to molnupiravir'', ''Pregnancy and lactation (contraindicated due to embryo-fetal mutagenic toxicity)'']', '[''Strictly contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding'', ''Individuals of childbearing potential must use reliable contraception during and for 4 days after therapy; males must use contraception for 3 months after last dose'', ''Not authorized for use under 18 years of age due to potential bone and cartilage toxicity'']', '[''Diarrhoea'', ''Nausea'', ''Dizziness'', ''Headache'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity reactions / angioedema'', ''Embryo-fetal toxicity'']', '[''No significant CYP-mediated drug interactions or P-glycoprotein interactions reported (major advantage over nirmatrelvir/ritonavir)'']', '[''Confirmation of negative pregnancy test before initiation in females of childbearing potential'', ''Clinical symptom tracking'']', 'Strictly contraindicated in pregnancy; causes fetal harm, anatomical malformations, and embryo-fetal lethality.', 'Contraindicated during treatment and for 4 days after the final dose; discard pumped milk.', 'Strictly contraindicated under 18 years of age (affects bone growth and growth plates).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age.', 'No dosage adjustment required in any degree of renal impairment.', 'No dosage adjustment required in hepatic impairment.', '[''Zero cytochrome P450 drug interactions, making it ideal for patients taking complex cardiac or transplant medications where Paxlovid is contraindicated'', ''No dosage adjustment needed for renal or hepatic impairment'']', '[''Lower efficacy in preventing hospitalization compared to Paxlovid or Remdesivir'', ''Contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and children'']', '[''Take 800 mg (four 200 mg capsules) every 12 hours for exactly 5 days'', ''Start within 5 days of symptom onset'']', 'MOLNU-piravir: Mutagenic Overload Leads to Non-viable Unfunctional viral RNA.', '-piravir', 'mol-noo-PEER-a-veer', 'मोलनु-पिराविर', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('nirmatrelvir', 'Nirmatrelvir / Ritonavir', 'Paxlovid (Nirmatrelvir + Ritonavir)', 'Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir', 'Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir', 'Antivirals', 'A peptidomimetic inhibitor of the SARS-CoV-2 main protease (Mpro / 3CLpro) boosted with ritonavir, reducing COVID-19 hospitalization and death by up to 89% in high-risk patients.', '[''300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) + 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) per dose'']', '[''Tablet (Dose Pack)'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Nirmatrelvir reversible inhibits the SARS-CoV-2 main protease (Mpro/3CLpro), preventing processing of viral polyprotein precursors; low-dose ritonavir acts as a pharmacokinetic booster by inhibiting CYP3A4, sustaining therapeutic nirmatrelvir blood levels.', 'Potent antiviral inhibition of SARS-CoV-2 replication across all variants of concern (Omicron, Delta, etc.).', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak nirmatrelvir concentrations reached in 3 hours when co-administered with ritonavir; food slightly improves absorption.', 'Nirmatrelvir Vd approx. 104 L; 69% bound to human plasma proteins; ritonavir 98-99% bound.', 'Nirmatrelvir undergoes minor CYP3A4 metabolism; protected from metabolism by ritonavir co-administration.', 'Nirmatrelvir excreted in urine (approx. 50%) and faeces (approx. 35%); ritonavir eliminated predominantly in faeces.', 'High when boosted with ritonavir', 'Nirmatrelvir approx. 6 hours; ritonavir 3 to 5 hours', '69%', '1 to 3 hours', '12 hours (dosed twice daily for 5 days)', '[''Treatment of mild-to-moderate COVID-19 in adults and pediatric patients (12 years and older weighing at least 40 kg) at high risk for progression to severe disease'']', '[''Hypersensitivity to nirmatrelvir or ritonavir'', ''Concurrent use of drugs highly dependent on CYP3A4 for clearance whose elevated levels cause life-threatening reactions (amiodarone, simvastatin, rivaroxaban, alfuzosin, sildenafil for PAH)'']', '["Massive list of clinically significant drug-drug interactions due to ritonavir''s potent CYP3A4 inhibition; review all patient medications", ''Dose reduction required in moderate renal impairment (eGFR 30-59 mL/min)'', ''Rebound COVID-19 symptoms or antigen positivity reported in small subset 2-8 days after completing course'']', '[''Dysgeusia (metallic / bitter taste in mouth - very common)'', ''Diarrhoea'', ''Headache'', ''Hypertension'', ''Myalgia'']', '[''Severe hypersensitivity / anaphylaxis'', ''Toxic epidermal necrolysis'', ''Hepatotoxicity / clinical jaundice'']', '["Contraindicated with: carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, rifampin, St. John''s wort (inducers reduce nirmatrelvir efficacy)", ''Drastically increases levels of: calcineurin inhibitors, statins, antiarrhythmics, DOACs, benzodiazepines, PDE5 inhibitors'']', '[''Medication reconciliation before prescribing'', ''Renal function (eGFR) to calibrate dose'', ''Blood pressure in hypertensive patients'']', 'Use during pregnancy only if potential benefit justifies potential risk; limited data available.', 'Compatible with caution; consider withholding breastfeeding during 5-day course.', 'Authorized for adolescents 12 years and older weighing ≥40 kg.', 'No dose adjustment based on age alone; monitor drug interactions closely.', 'If eGFR 30-59 mL/min, reduce dose to 150 mg nirmatrelvir + 100 mg ritonavir twice daily; contraindicated if eGFR < 30 mL/min.', 'No dosage adjustment in mild to moderate hepatic impairment; contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C).', '[''Reduces hospitalization and death by nearly 89% in unvaccinated high-risk patients'', ''Convenient 5-day oral outpatient regimen'']', '[''Extreme potential for drug-drug interactions due to ritonavir CYP3A4 inhibition'', ''Metallic dysgeusia taste experienced by many patients'']', '[''Must be started within 5 days of symptom onset'', "Always screen patient''s complete prescription and OTC drug list for interactions"]', 'NIRMATREL-vir: Cleaves Mpro protease; Requires Ritonavir booster; Review RX list.', '-matrelvir', 'nir-ma-TREL-veer', 'निरमाट्रेल्-विर', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('sofosbuvir', 'Sofosbuvir', 'Sofosbuvir', 'Sofosbuvir', 'Sofosbuvir', 'Antivirals', 'A breakthrough nucleotide analogue NS5B polymerase inhibitor with a high genetic barrier to resistance, serving as the backbone of direct-acting antiviral cures for chronic Hepatitis C.', '[''400 mg'']', '[''Film-coated Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Uridine nucleotide prodrug metabolized in hepatocytes to active 2''-deoxy-2''-alpha-fluoro-beta-C-methyluridine triphosphate (GS-461203); acts as an obligate chain terminator for HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.', 'Pan-genotypic antiviral curative potency against all HCV genotypes (1 through 6); sustained virological response (SVR12 cure) rates exceed 95-98% when combined with NS5A inhibitors.', 'Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration of sofosbuvir within 0.5 to 2 hours; parent drug rapidly taken up by hepatocytes.', '61-65% bound to plasma proteins; major circulating inactive metabolite GS-331007 minimally bound.', 'Extensively metabolized in the liver via cathepsin A and CES1 followed by phosphorylation to active triphosphate GS-461203; dephosphorylated to circulating inactive metabolite GS-331007.', 'Excreted in urine (approx. 80%, primarily as inactive metabolite GS-331007) and faeces (approx. 14%).', 'High (over 80%)', 'Parent sofosbuvir 0.4 hours; inactive metabolite GS-331007 approx. 27 hours', '61% to 65%', 'Rapid onset; 400 mg once daily with or without food', '24 hours', '[''Chronic Hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection genotypes 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 in adults and pediatric patients 3 years and older (combined with velpatasvir, ledipasvir, or daclatasvir)'', ''HCV and HIV-1 coinfection'']', '[''Concurrent administration of amiodarone (life-threatening symptomatic bradycardia and fatal cardiac arrest reported)'', "Strong P-glycoprotein inducers (rifampin, St. John''s wort, carbamazepine - reduce sofosbuvir levels)"]', '[''Black box warning: Risk of Hepatitis B virus (HBV) reactivation in patients coinfected with HCV and HBV (screen for HBV before starting)'', ''Severe bradycardia when combined with amiodarone and another DAA'', ''Must be used in combination regimens (never as monotherapy)'']', '[''Fatigue'', ''Headache'', ''Nausea'', ''Insomnia'', ''Anemia (when paired with ribavirin)'']', '[''Severe symptomatic bradycardia (with amiodarone)'', ''HBV reactivation with fulminant hepatitis'', ''Severe depression (with ribavirin/interferon regimens)'']', '[''Amiodarone (strictly contraindicated due to fatal bradycardia)'', "P-gp inducers (rifampin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, St. John''s wort)", ''Antacids (space by 4 hours)'']', '[''Screen for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) and core antibody (anti-HBc) prior to therapy'', ''HCV RNA viral load at baseline, end of treatment, and 12 weeks post-treatment (SVR12)'', ''Heart rate if patient has cardiac disease'']', 'Category B; if used with ribavirin, ribavirin category X contraindication applies.', 'Excreted in animal milk; compatible with caution.', 'Approved for pediatric patients 3 years and older (pellets and tablets available).', 'No dosage adjustment required based on age alone.', 'Updated guidelines confirm safety without dose adjustment in severe renal impairment and ESRD on hemodialysis.', 'No dosage adjustment required in mild, moderate, or severe hepatic impairment.', '[''Pan-genotypic backbone of direct-acting antiviral cures with SVR12 cure rates >95%'', ''High genetic barrier to resistance'', ''Safe in decompensated cirrhosis and renal failure'']', '[''Fatal bradycardia interaction with amiodarone'', ''Risk of HBV reactivation during HCV clearance'']', '[''Cures Hepatitis C in 12 weeks with oral once-daily combination regimens'', ''Never combine with amiodarone'']', 'SOFOS-buvir: Single Oral Fix Overcomes Sickness in Hepatitis C; Stop Amiodarone.', '-buvir', 'soe-FOS-byoo-veer', 'सोफ़ोस-बुविर', 'active', 'verified', NULL, NULL),
('lacosamide', 'Lacosamide', 'Lacosamide Film-Coated', 'Lacosamide', 'Lacosamide', 'Neurology', 'Third-generation antiepileptic drug selectively enhancing slow inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels.', '[''50 mg'', ''100 mg'', ''150 mg'', ''200 mg'', ''10 mg/mL syrup'', ''200 mg/20 mL IV'']', '[''Tablet'', ''Syrup'', ''Injection'']', '[''Oral'', ''Intravenous'']', 'Selectively enhances slow inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels, terminating sustained repetitive neuronal firing without affecting fast inactivation.', 'Reduces seizure frequency in focal-onset seizures with or without secondary generalization.', 'Rapidly and completely absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 1-4 hours.', 'Vd approx 0.6 L/kg; protein binding <15%.', 'Metabolized by CYP2C19 (minor) and non-CYP pathways to inactive O-desmethyl metabolite.', '95% excreted in urine (40% unchanged, 30% inactive metabolite).', '100%', '13 hours', '<15%', '1 hour', '12 to 24 hours', '[''Focal-onset seizures with or without secondary generalization in adults and children >= 4 years'', ''Adjunctive therapy in primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures'']', '[''Second- or third-degree AV block without pacemaker'', ''Hypersensitivity to lacosamide'']', '[''PR interval prolongation on ECG'', ''Syncope risk'', ''Suicidal ideation'']', '[''Dizziness'', ''Headache'', ''Nausea'', ''Diplopia'']', '[''AV conduction block'', ''Syncope'', ''DRESS syndrome'']', '[''Class I and III antiarrhythmics (additive PR prolongation)'', ''CYP2C19 strong inhibitors'']', '[''Baseline ECG for PR interval'', ''Renal function tests'']', 'Category C; use only if potential benefit justifies fetal risk.', 'Excreted in human milk; use caution.', 'Approved for focal seizures in >= 4 years.', 'Lower initial dose recommended.', 'Max 300 mg/day if CrCl <= 30 mL/min.', 'Max 300 mg/day in mild-to-moderate impairment.', '[''Linear 100% oral bioavailability'', ''Minimal drug interactions'', ''IV formulation available'']', '[''PR prolongation'', ''Dizziness on titration'']', '[''Enhances slow inactivation of sodium channels'', ''Schedule V controlled substance in some regions'']', 'Lacosamide LACS (lacks) fast inactivation, acts on SLOW inactivation.', '-amide', 'luh-KOE-suh-mide', 'लकोसामाइड', 'published', 'verified', '2026-03-15', '1.0'),
('lamotrigine', 'Lamotrigine', 'Lamotrigine Dispersible', 'Lamotrigine', 'Lamotrigine', 'Neurology', 'Broad-spectrum phenyltriazine antiepileptic and mood stabilizer targeting voltage-gated sodium and calcium channels.', '[''25 mg'', ''50 mg'', ''100 mg'', ''200 mg dispersible'']', '[''Dispersible Tablet'']', '[''Oral'']', 'Voltage-gated sodium channel blocker and inhibitor of presynaptic glutamate and aspartate release.', 'Stabilizes neuronal membranes and prevents excitatory neurotransmitter release.', 'Rapid and completely absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 2.5 hours.', 'Vd 0.9 to 1.3 L/kg; protein binding 55%.', 'Extensively metabolized by hepatic glucuronidation via UGT1A4 to inactive 2-N-glucuronide.', '90% excreted in urine primarily as glucuronide conjugates.', '98%', '25 to 30 hours (monotherapy; reduced to 14h by inducers, increased to 60h by valproate)', '55%', '1 to 2 hours', '24 hours', '[''Focal and generalized seizures including Lennox-Gastaut syndrome'', ''Maintenance treatment of Bipolar I disorder to prevent depressive episodes'']', '[''Known hypersensitivity to lamotrigine'']', '[''Black box warning: Serious cutaneous adverse reactions including Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)'', ''Titrate very slowly according to manufacturer schedule'', ''Dose must be halved if co-administered with sodium valproate'']', '[''Dizziness'', ''Headache'', ''Diplopia'', ''Ataxia'', ''Nausea'']', '[''Stevens-Johnson syndrome / TEN'', ''Aseptic meningitis'', ''Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)'']', '[''Sodium valproate (inhibits glucuronidation; doubles half-life, requires half starting dose)'', ''Carbamazepine/Phenytoin (induces glucuronidation, halves half-life)'', ''Oral contraceptives (reduce lamotrigine levels by 50%)'']', '[''Skin inspection for rash at every visit'', ''Hepatic and renal panel'']', 'Category C; reassuring registry data, folate supplementation recommended.', 'Excreted in breast milk; monitor infant for